\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  c.bank steps up  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in yuan trading - sources\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 06:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Aug 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yuan trading, ordering state banks to buy yuan at designated rates on behalf of the monetary authorities, among other emergency measures, banking sources with direct knowledge of the matter told Reuters on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, a unit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recently issued a notice ordering its sub-bureaus to strengthen supervision so as to avoid major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases and to conduct "window guidance", or administrative advice, to banks in case of necessity, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 "When market sentiment causes abnormal volatility, any regulatory bodies will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help stabilise prices," said one source.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said during a news conference on Thursday that it had stopped "regularly" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but would conduct "effective management" only in cases of extreme volatility.\par \par \uc2 The bank also announced that it would intensify monitoring of "abnormal" cross border flows.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fell for a third day on Thursday, after the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  shocked markets by devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 2 percent on Tuesday, the sharpest adjustment in the history of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 But Thursday's losses were slight, and the gap between the guidance rate and the traded spot market rate closed sharply as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to slow a sharp selloff that saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lose around 4 percent in just two days.\par \par \uc2 "The sudden fluctuations of the yuan over the past few days have created many speculative positions and caused more settlement deals," said a dealer at a foreign bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "Excluding those transactions by state banks on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollar purchases apparently exceeding sales."\par \par \uc2 Banks have been asked to help monitor clients' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions, said the sources, who have seen the notice by the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Seven major banks must be included in the monitoring, the notice was quoted as saying, while other can be selectively included.\par \par \uc2 The seven are China's biggest five state-owned banks, including the \uc2 Industrial and Commercial Bank of China\uc2  , as well as two major commercial lenders, \uc2 CITIC Bank\uc2  and \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2 , the sources quoted the notice as saying.\par \par \uc2 Contacted by Reuters, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator officials declined immediate comments.\par \par \uc2 Banks designated to help the authorities to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions were required to record each deal by their biggest 20 clients during Aug. 12 to 14, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 In line with Beijing's orders, bank headquarters have also recently strengthened their own efforts to help curb the yuan's volatility, requiring their branches to report unusual movements, among other steps, the sources said. (Reporting by Beijing newsroom; Writing by Lu Jianxin; Editing by Pete Sweeney & Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-16T04:27:18.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150813eb8d005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
