\par\par \b \uc2 Livestock Highlights: Top Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 23:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TOP STORIES \par  \par Beijing Struggles to Adjust \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 China guided its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower for a third day Thursday after briefly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up the day before, showing how the leadership is struggling to manage the market in largely uncharted territory for Beijing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  engineered what looked like a win-win when it ceded more control of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to markets earlier this week, in a step toward liberalization that also gives Chinese exporters an edge. But now it also has to manage market expectations to keep the yuan from entering a free fall--a challenge for central banks world-wide but one that China has avoided by tightly controlling the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday in the final moments of trading, people familiar with the matter said, after the yuan weakened nearly 2%--the daily limit-- to its lowest level against the dollar in four years. On Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan's fixing only marginally lower, a sign it wants to let the yuan depreciate but only in a measured way. Meeting with reporters in Beijing Thursday for a rare briefing, \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials stuck to their message, saying that the exchange rate will become more market driven but insisting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the ability to keep the market in check with China's "ample" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Farmland Prices Steady to Lower in Much of Central U.S. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Farmland values were flat to lower in parts of the U.S. Midwest and Great Plains in the second quarter, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports Thursday, reflecting the continued impact of lower grain prices on farmers' incomes and the broader agricultural economy. The average price of farmland in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\f28 \uc2 \f2 's district, which includes Illinois, Iowa and other big farm states, fell 3% compared with year-ago levels and 1% compared with the first quarter of 2015, the bank said. Agricultural land values in Illinois and Iowa, the two largest U.S. corn-producing states, both declined on a yearly basis for at least the fourth consecutive quarter, while values in Indiana and Michigan rose from year-ago levels. In the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City district, which includes Kansas, Nebraska and Missouri, the average price of nonirrigated cropland dropped more than 4% compared with the first quarter and was down nearly 3% from the year-earlier period, the bank said. Irrigated land values held steady on a quarter-to-quarter basis but fell more than 3% from a year earlier. Irrigated cropland, common in the region, depends on man-made water systems for moisture rather than rainfall. In the \f28 \uc2 \f2 St. Louis Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's district, which also includes parts of Illinois, Kentucky and Arkansas, prices for "quality" farmland remained largely the same in the second quarter compared with a year ago, despite lower farm incomes in the region, the bank said in a report. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    STORIES OF INTEREST \par  \par Probe Eyes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ex-Chairman \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Criminal and civil authorities are investigating whether the former chairman of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . leaked inside information about a corporate spinoff to a professional gambler who in turn is suspected of tipping off golfer Phil Mickelson, according to people familiar with the probe. The scrutiny of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  ex-Chairman Thomas Davis brings a high-profile investigation first reported by The Wall Street Journal last year into the boardroom of a major company. The showcase probe could test the limits of the government's ability to pursue high-profile insider-trading cases, as its efforts have been significantly set back by a 2014 appellate-court ruling. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Securities and Exchange Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the Manhattan U.S. attorney's office are examining whether Mr. Davis leaked information on his company's 2012 announcement of a spinoff of its \f28 \uc2 \f2 WhiteWave Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  division to Las Vegas bettor William "Billy" Walters, the people familiar with the investigation said. Mr. Davis unexpectedly resigned Friday from the Dallas-based dairy company, ahead of the company's Monday earnings announcement. Mr. Davis's resignation was voluntary, his lawyer said. The lawyer representing him, Thomas Melsheimer, said in a statement that Mr. Davis has "fully and without reservation cooperated with the SEC in their investigation of alleged insider trading in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  stock from day one. He has no knowledge of any material non-public information about \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  being conveyed to Mr. Walters by him or anyone else." Mr. Walters said last year that it would be "preposterous to think that I would involve myself in insider trading." Efforts to reach him Wednesday were unsuccessful. Mr. Mickelson couldn't be reached Wednesday. A lawyer representing him previously said: "Phil has done absolutely nothing wrong. He has been cooperating with the investigation." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a statement said it has "cooperated with the government's requests for information and will continue to do so. Because there is an ongoing government investigation, it would be inappropriate to comment further at this time." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Kraft Heinz to Cut 2,500 Workers \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kraft Heinz Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said it is cutting 2,500 jobs in North America--more than 5% of its global workforce--as it aims to slash at least $1.5 billion from the newly combined company's annual budget. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kraft Foods Group Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 H.J. Heinz Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . merged last month in a deal orchestrated by Heinz owners 3G Capital Partners LP and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Berkshire Hathaway Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s \f28 \uc2 \f2 Warren Buffett\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Now that the integration is under way, a Kraft Heinz spokesman said Wednesday that its new management team--led primarily by legacy Heinz executives and 3G officials--has restructured the company to simplify its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and leverage its much larger scale. "This new structure eliminates duplication to enable faster decision-making, increased accountability and accelerated growth," the spokesman said. As part of that process, the company, with about $28 billion in annual revenue, will eliminate 2,500 jobs in the U.S. and Canada, including 700 employees in its Chicago-area headquarters in Northfield, Ill. The company has a second headquarters in Pittsburgh. Kraft Heinz, like other big food companies, faces an uphill battle to boost sales with conventional packaged foods that are losing traction as U.S. consumers seek out fresher food with simpler ingredients. The Kraft Heinz stable includes Velveeta cheese, Jell-O desserts and frozen Bagel Bites. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    FUTURES MARKETS \par  \par U.S. Live Cattle Higher On Stronger-Demand Signals; Hogs Mixed \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHICAGO--U.S. live-cattle futures finished higher on signs that packers may be willing to pay more in the cash markets. Lean-hog futures were mixed. Cattle futures closed higher after oscillating between gains and losses following a steep drop the previous day. Analysts said selling pressure seems to have subsided while outside markets play a smaller role. An increase in wholesale beef prices in the past week have prompted some traders to speculate packers may be willing to pay more in the cash markets this week. "What we all continue to be surprised about is the continued resilience of the U.S. consumer" amid relatively high beef prices, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rich Nelson\f28 \uc2 \f2 , director of research at commodities advisory firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Allendale Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in McHenry, Ill. He added that so-called short sellers taking profits on bets that prices would fall may also have supported prices. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    CASH MARKETS \par  \par Zumbrota, Minn Hog Market Steady At $50.00 - Aug 13 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barrow and gilt prices at the Zumbrota, Minn., livestock market today were steady at $50.00 a hundredweight. Sow prices are steady. Sows weighing under 400 pounds were at $44.00-$45.00, 400-450 pounds were at $44.00-$45.00, 450-500 pounds were at $44.00-$45.00, and those over 500 pounds were at $47.00-$48.00. The day's total run was estimated at 100 head. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Estimated U.S. Pork Packer Margin Index - Aug 13 \par  \par Date        Standard         Estimated margin \par              Op Mgn          at vertically \par              Index        integrated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par                                     * \par Aug 13      +$19.57             +$ 39.80 \par  \par Aug 12      +$24.27             +$ 43.05 \par  \par Aug 11      +$28.96             +$ 44.13 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Based on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Iowa State University\f28 \uc2 \f2 's latest estimated cost of production. A positive number indicates a processing margin above the cost of production of the animals. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Beef-O-Meter \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This report compares the \f28 \uc2 \f2 USDA\f28 \uc2 \f2 's latest beef and pork carcass composite values with their respective year-ago prices. The figures are in percentages of the same-day year-ago price quotes. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                     Beef \par          For Today              Choice  95.5 \par                                 Select  94.6 \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 USDA\f28 \uc2 \f2  Boxed Beef, Pork Reports \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wholesale choice-grade beef prices on Thursday rose $1.07 per hundred pounds to $245.09, according to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 USDA\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Select-grade prices fell 32 cents per hundred pounds to $235.13. The total load count was 102. Wholesale pork prices fell $1.51 to $88.25 a hundred pounds, based on Omaha, Neb., price quotes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ilan Brat at ilan.brat@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 13, 2015 17:09 ET (21:09 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150813eb8d003l5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
