\par\par \b \uc2 Global Markets Rally After Two-Day Tumble -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Whittall   \par \uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 11:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Global markets rallied Thursday after two days of sharp declines following China's unexpected move to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Europe, the Stoxx Europe 600 index gained 1.7% midmorning. Germany's DAX index rose 1.6%, while France's CAC 40 index was up 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses were higher across the board and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also regained some ground after a recovery in U.S. stock markets late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 China's Shanghai Composite Index closed 1.8% higher, while Japan's Nikkei 225 index gained 1.0%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures indicated a 0.2% opening gain for the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2 . Changes in futures aren't necessarily reflected in market moves after the opening bell.\par \par \uc2 "Markets over the last couple of days were overreacting to what I would argue was quite a healthy move from the Chinese authorities to move toward a more freely floating exchange rate regime," said \uc2 Alan Mudie\uc2 , head of investment strategy at Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale Private Banking, which oversees EUR117 billion in assets.\par \par \uc2 "The bounce back that we've had this morning is really a correction from exaggerated pessimism from yesterday and the day before," he added.\par \par \uc2 Shares in companies sensitive to Chinese demand recovered after plunging over the past two days. In the luxury goods sector, \uc2 LVMH Mo\u235\'00\'EBt Hennessy Louis Vuitton\uc2  was up 3.3% and \uc2 Burberry Group PLC\uc2  rose 2.8%. Car makers \uc2 PSA Peugeot-Citro\u235\'00\'EBn\uc2  and \uc2 Renault SA\uc2  were up 3.0% and 2.2%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's surprise devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week rattled global markets, sending stocks, metals and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply lower.\par \par \uc2 The shift in policy caused investors to raise questions about the health of the world's second-largest economy and threatens to dent the competitiveness of countries that compete with China in the global exports market.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan has lost more than 3% against the U.S. dollar since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it would allow markets greater input in setting the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The onshore yuan fell again Thursday, but the moves have been more gradual since the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Wednesday to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after sharp declines.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro fell 0.4% against the dollar to $1.1114, after rising rapidly Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil was up 1.1% at $50.72 a barrel. Gold was down 0.6% at $1,117.30 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Write to Christopher Whittall at christopher.whittall@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 13, 2015 05:48 ET (09:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150813eb8d000zw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
