\par\par \b \uc2 RUBBER-TOCOM steadies amid uncertainties over China policy\b0\par\par\uc2 287 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 11:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Benchmark TOCOM rubber futures steadied on Thursday as investors tried to clear their positions amid uncertainties over China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was no basis for further depreciation in the yuan.               \par \par \uc2 The Tokyo Commodity Exchange (TOCOM) rubber contract for January delivery <0#2JRU:> finished 0.2 yen higher at 194.3 yen ($1.56) per kg.               \par \par \uc2 "Investors and traders were confused and hesitant to take fresh positions as they were not sure what China really wanted and how its devalution of the yuan this week would impact its economy," a Tokyo-based dealer said.               \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 said there was no basis for more yuan depreciation in the light of strong economic fundamentals, even though the yuan dropped for the third straight day.               \par \par \uc2 Also, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yuan trading, ordering state banks to buy yuan at designated rates on behalf of the monetary authorities, among other emergency measures, banking sources with direct knowledge of the matter told Reuters on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 Trade on TOCOM was thin as many investors were away for Japan's "Obon" holiday with many companies closed for much of the week.               \par \par \uc2 "With many market participants away for holiday, most of the trades were position adjustments," the dealer said.               \par \par \uc2 "Rubber prices are expected to stay under pressure as China's measures to boost its exports may not help shore up domestic demand," he said.               \par \par \uc2 The most-active rubber contract on the Shanghai futures exchange for January delivery rose 350 yuan to end at 12,490 yuan ($1,952.11) per tonne.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-13T10:47:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150813eb8d00c7h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
