\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gets respite as China eases  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war fears\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 10:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Yuan weakens slightly but pace of decline slows\par \par \uc2 * Dollar supported as risk appetite makes modest return\par \par \uc2 * U.S. retail sales eyed\par \par \uc2 By Jemima Kelly\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar rose from a one-month low against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday as the yuan's fall slowed, easing worries that China was trying to sharply devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain competitive advantage.\par \par \uc2 Having tumbled some 3 percent against the greenback since Tuesday in an unprecedented fall, the yuan continued to weaken slightly on Thursday but \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was no basis for further depreciation.\par \par \uc2 The decision to devalue China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi, by pushing its official guidance rate down 2 percent triggered fears of a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" and sent shockwaves through global financial markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors who had held euro-funded positions in the yuan and other riskier emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded by buying back the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , driving it to a one-month high of $1.1215 on Wednesday. But it was half a percent down on the day at $1.1107 in a calmer market on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The market has started to settle a bit (but) there's still a lot of uncertainty about how far the value of the renminbi will fall and how far should it fall given the slowdown in the economy," said \uc2 Rabobank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Jane Foley in London.\par \par \uc2 Banking sources said the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yuan trading in a bid to stabilise exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese move also drove investors to push back their expectations of when the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  would raise interest rates, helping drive the dollar index to a one-month low of 95.926. But it pulled away from that trough on Thursday, up 0.3 percent at 95.593.\par \par \uc2 U.S. retail sales data due at 1230 GMT will be closely watched.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. clearly needs to watch the global economy and China, but ultimately if we get a very strong release today, market expectations for a September interest rate hike will probably bounce right back," Foley said.\par \par \uc2 Against the safe-haven yen, the dollar rose 0.3 percent to 124.56, although it remained below a two-month high of 125.28 yen set on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "If risk-off type of trading recedes that should help support the dollar against the yen," said Masashi Murata, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The Swedish crown gained more than 1 percent against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hit a one-week high of 9.4840 crowns per euro after better-than-expected Swedish inflation data .\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano in Singapore; Editing by Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-13T09:12:25.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150813eb8d00a5p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
