\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Dollar Stronger as Yuan-Related Volatility Eases\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Glynn   \par \uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 10:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY--The Australian dollar was stronger in Asia on Thursday as volatility surrounding this week's progressive devaluation of the yuan subsided somewhat.\par \par \uc2 At 0720 GMT, the Australian dollar was trading at US$0.7355, up from US$0.7262 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened weaker in Asia as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the yuan lower for a third day. The yuan was fixed at 6.4010 against the U.S. dollar, down 0.2% from the previous close and 1.1% below Wednesday's fixing.\par \par \uc2 Despite the devaluation, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth trading of the yuan overnight to manage market expectation about just how far it is likely to fall and how fast, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Meeting with reporters in Beijing for a rare briefing, \uc2 PBOC\uc2  officials said the exchange rate will become more market-driven but insisted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the ability to keep the market in check with China's "ample" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has a war chest of nearly $3.7 trillion to influence its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Local traders expect the yuan will eventually be allowed to fall by around 10% to provide some stimulus to the China's slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 "Overall our sense is that a degree of mystery will remain over the daily fixes but that the extent of the yuan decline will be moderate and the impact on global markets will gradually fade," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Overnight, deputy governor at the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 , \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2 , again highlighted that interest rates can't be relied upon to boost the Australian economy.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy is, ultimately, not a driver of medium-term economic growth," Mr. Lowe said.\par \par \uc2 "While low interest rates are currently helping the economy through a period of transition, an extended period of low interest rates implies ongoing low returns to savers and low underlying returns on assets," he said.\par \par \uc2 "This is not a world to which we should aspire," he added.\par \par \uc2 The comments lent weight to the view that if the RBA does cut interest rates again, it will do so reluctantly. It has already cut rates twice to support growth in the face of plunging coal and iron ore prices this year.\par \par \uc2 -Write to James Glynn at james.glynn@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 13-08-15 0811GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150813eb8d00095\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
