\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares gain on hopes China is slowing yuan's descent\b0\par\par\uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets yuan midpoint weaker for 3rd day but reassures markets\par \par \uc2 * Asian shares encouraged by late recovery on Wall Street\par \par \uc2 * Dollar under pressure as bets pared on Fed hike in Sept\par \par \uc2 * Crude oil futures move away from 6-year lows\par \par \uc2 By Lisa Twaronite\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Asian shares gained on Thursday, taking heart from a late recovery on Wall Street and from reassurances from \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that there was no basis for further yuan depreciation after it devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Financial spreadbetters expected the brighter mood to extend to European bourses.\par \par \uc2 Britain's FTSE 100 was seen opening around 45 points higher, or 0.7 percent; Germany's DAX was seen opening around 150 points higher, or 1.4 percent; and France's CAC 40 was seen to open around 80 points higher, or 1.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that there was no basis for more yuan depreciation in light of strong economic fundamentals, even though the yuan dropped for the third straight day following Beijing's move to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 set its guidance rate at 6.4010 per dollar prior to the market open, weaker than the previous fix of 6.3306. The gap between the guidance rate and the traded spot market rate narrowed sharply as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to curb the yuan's slide.\par \par \uc2 Still, traders remain cautious about how low the yuan might go. Sources told Reuters some powerful voices in the government are pushing for an even deeper devaluation to help China's struggling exporters.\par \par \uc2 PBOC Vice-governor \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  dismissed such talk as groundless, but that did not convince market participants that Chinese authorities would refrain from allowing the yuan to slip further over time given weakness in the economy.\par \par \uc2 For now, though, banking sources said the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yuan trading in a bid to stabilise prices.\par \par \uc2 "There is a degree of calm returning to the market," said Mitul Kotecha, head of Asia-Pacific \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Singapore. "The market certainly perceives that the Chinese authorities don't want the CNY to weaken too dramatically."\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was up 0.6 percent, after U.S. shares rebounded and two out of three main indexes end in positive territory.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei stock index shrugged off early losses and downbeat capital expenditure figures to end up 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Japan's core machinery orders fell a greater-than-expected 7.9 percent in June, down for the first time in four months and adding to fears that data on Monday will show the economy shrank in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 The risk-averse mood after China's moves this week had heightened the appeal of safe-haven government debt, which then pushed down \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields and pressured the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 But benchmark 10-year note prices fell from three-month highs after a lacklustre auction, with the 10-year yield at 2.162 percent in Asian trading, compared to its U.S. close of 2.130 percent.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also suffered as investors pared back bets that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's long-awaited interest rate hike would come as early as its Sept. 16-17 meeting.\par \par \uc2 Short-term U.S. interest rates markets indicated investors were pricing in no more than a 40 percent chance that the Fed will raise rates next month.\par \par \uc2 "Pressure to unwind dollar long positions is mounting and focus is on how long this trend will continue. 'China risk' has dampened hopes for a September rate hike and upcoming data could further chip away at expectations for a Fed tightening," said Junichi Ishikawa, market analyst at IG Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was about 0.2 percent higher at 124.46. The euro edged down about 0.1 percent to $1.1143 after scaling a one-month peak of $1.1215 on Wednesday, helped by the unwinding of euro-funded carry trades in the yuan and other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In commodities trading, spot gold was down about 0.3 percent at $1,120.80 an ounce after logging its fifth straight session of gains.\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange fell about 0.3 percent to $5,177 a tonne, moving back toward the previous session's six-year low of $5,062 a tonne.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures extended overnight gains made on lower U.S. crude stockpiles, but remained not far from six-year lows plumbed this week on fears that China's weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hit imports. U.S. crude was up about 0.4 percent at $43.45 a barrel, while Brent added about 0.5 percent to $49.89. (Additional reporting by Shinichi Saoshiro in Tokyo and Masayuki Kitano in Singapore; Editing by Richard Borsuk & Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-13T07:31:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150813eb8d000ph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
