\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Edge Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 1,197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par               Latest   Change  %Change \par DJIA         17402.51   -0.33 -0.00% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2         5044.39   +7.60 +0.15% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2        2086.05   +1.98 +0.10% \par FTSE 100      6571.19  -93.35 -1.40% \par Nikkei Stock 20478.16  +85.39 +0.42% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2     24076.83 +160.81 +0.67% \par Kospi         1976.08   +0.61 +0.03% \par SGX Nifty     8375.00   -2.00 -0.02% \par (Aug) contract \par  \par           Latest    %Change \par USD/JPY  124.33-34  +0.10% \par Range    124.41-124.07 \par EUR/USD  1.1152-55  -0.05% \par Range    1.1190-1.1151 \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBOT\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wheat Sep $4.922 per bushel (Settlement Price) \par                    Last   Change \par Comex Gold        $1,123.60  +$15.90 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,101.10-$1,125.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $43.40   +$0.32 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks staged an afternoon rally to recover from a second day of losses following China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, though global indexes were broadly lower and investors remained unsettled over the implications of the sudden shift in the second-largest world economy.\par \par \uc2 While growth concerns in China have been present for months, they were brought to the forefront Tuesday after China devalued its tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , causing its biggest one-day loss in two decades.\par \par \uc2 Stock declined sharply in Europe, where economic growth is nascent and more levered to Chinese demand.\par \par \uc2 A slowdown in the second-largest economy dims the outlook for multinational companies that sell goods to China and weighs on their stock prices.\par \par \uc2 "The question that looms large is how much is the Chinese economy slowing," said \uc2 Art Hogan\uc2 , chief market strategist at \uc2 Wunderlich Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China's devaluation might also complicate the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision about when to start raising interest rates for the first time in nearly a decade. That uncertainty pushed the U.S. dollar lower versus the euro and the yen.\par \par \uc2 Investors fled stocks that benefit from higher interest rates, such as financials, and sought out bond-like stocks including utilities.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, \uc2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\uc2 . reported a smaller-than-expected revenue increase in its latest quarter. The Chinese e-commerce giant also said it has authorized a $4 billion stock-buyback program. Shares declined 6.1%.\par \par \uc2 Macy's Inc\uc2 . cut its sales guidance for the year after reporting steeper-than-expected drops in sales and profit in its second quarter. Shares fell 4.6%.\par \par \uc2 Aramark Holdings Corp\uc2 . said profit slid in its latest quarter, driven by a sales decline in its North American food and support-services segment. The company, which generates a significant chunk of overall revenue overseas, said the stronger dollar hit its earnings. Shares slipped 2.1% . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian markets staged a relief rally early Thursday, three days after China's surprise devaluation of the yuan, although investors remain unsettled over the implications of the sudden shift in the world's second largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P/ASX 200 was up 0.3%, the Nikkei Stock Average gained 0.1% and South Korea's Kospi was up 0.2%, all recovering from a two-day decline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks also recovered overnight. Many investors say the global economy is on track to continue expanding and in a low interest rate environment, that leaves returns on stocks still relatively appealing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation has broadly been interpreted as a signal of Beijing's growing worry about slow growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The malaise has spread to stocks with Hong Kong getting especially hurt yesterday. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index fell 2.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Financial markets in South Korea, Malaysia and commodity-producing emerging markets will probably continue to suffer the biggest losses if the renminbi depreciates further against the dollar," analysts at Capital Economists wrote in a research note. "But we doubt that the [\f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] is gearing up for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war, suggesting that emerging market markets will eventually rebound."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  kept rates on hold at 1.5% today, opting against any further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devalued the yuan this week. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY briefly dropped to an intraday low of 6.3850, falling under Wednesday's close to imply a stronger yuan, as markets digested the implications of Thursday's benchmark yuan fixing rate - which was weaker against the dollar for a third day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The USD/JPY remains steady now at 124.39 (from 124.21 late Wednesday in New York) after trimming earlier gains to fall to as low as 124.07 following the yuan's weaker fix again.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across Asia, from the South Korean won to the Indonesian rupiah and the Malaysian ringgit have been hit since Tuesday, after many were already at multiyear lows due to pressures from lower commodity prices and expectations for higher U.S. interest rates. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices are trading near a three-week high in Asian trade. Spot gold is trading at $1,125.60/oz, slightly higher than the opening price of $1,125.40/oz.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper prices rose Wednesday as investors locked in profits on bets against the metal a day after China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation sparked sharp declines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper for September delivery, the most actively traded contract, was recently up 0.8% at $2.3500 a pound in the Comex division of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures are trading mixed in Asia as a weaker dollar and lower weekly U.S. crude oil inventories offset weak market sentiment following China's devaluation of the yuan. Nymex oil futures are down by 3 cents at $43.27/bbl. Brent crude oil is up 13 cents at $49.79/bbl.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strong signs for demand helped oil bounce and sent gasoline prices soaring Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for September delivery gained 22 cents, or 0.5%, to settle at $43.30 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent crude, the global benchmark, gained 48 cents, or 1%, to $49.66 a barrel on ICE Futures Europe. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Says It Is Equipped to Keep Yuan Stable \par China's Yuan Opens Weaker \par Bank of Korea Holds Rate Steady \par Japan June Core Machinery Orders Fall 7.9% on Month \par Billionaire James Packer Steps Down as Crown Resorts Chairman \par Trading Investigation Eyes Ex-Chairman of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dean Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Golfer Phil Mickelson \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  Speaking to Potential Buyers for Fuel-Distribution Business Stake \par Lenovo to Cut Jobs; Profit Falls 50.9% for Quarter \par New Zealand House Prices Still Pushing Higher, Says Reinz \par Warehouse Explosions Kill at Least 17 in Tianjin, China \par Greece Faces Two-Year Recession Amid Bailout Cuts \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 News Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  Posts Loss on Education Write-Down \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Falls After Selling Hong Kong Mobile Business \par EPA Contractor Involved in Colorado Spill Identified as Environmental Restoration \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Brings \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Apparel\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Rating Further Into Junk Territory \par U.S. Appeals Ruling in \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bailout Dispute \par Mattel Senior Executive Tim Kilpin Leaving in November -- Memo \par Mexico's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Cuts 2015 Growth Forecast \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 12, 2015 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150813eb8d000bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
