\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles as markets await China yuan fixing for clues on depreciation plans\b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 02:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar broadly weaker against euro, yen\par \par \uc2 * China's yuan fixing at around 0115 GMT remains in focus\par \par \uc2 * Rebound in commodity prices lift aussie, kiwi from 6-yr lows\par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled early on Thursday after retreating broadly overnight as the yuan's depreciation slowed, with the market awaiting the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily fixing for clues on how far Beijing will let it slide.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hovered at 124.215 yen after dropping 0.7 percent overnight, when it had come tumbling down from a two-month high of 125.28.\par \par \uc2 The greenback reversed amid speculation that the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to slow the yuan's fall by selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan fell to a four-year low of 6.451 on Wednesday before pulling sharply back to 6.387.\par \par \uc2 It has lost about 3 percent since China's surprise devaluation on Tuesday, and sources involved in the policy-making process told Reuters that powerful voices inside the government were pushing for it to go still lower.\par \par \uc2 Their comments, which offered a rare insight into the argument going on behind the scenes in Beijing, suggest there is political pressure for an overall devaluation of almost 10 percent, though economists polled by Reuters see a more modest decline\par \par \uc2 The euro was steady at $1.1156, not far from a one-month high of $1.1215 scaled overnight on growing doubts that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will hike interest rates in September after China's devaluation further clouded the outlook for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Pressure to unwind dollar long positions is mounting and focus is on how long this trend will continue. 'China risk' has dampened hopes for a September rate hike and upcoming data could further chip away at expectations for a Fed tightening," said Junichi Ishikawa, market analyst at IG Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 While trading was quiet early on Thursday, investors braced for the Chinese yuan's midpoint fixing around 0115 GMT.\par \par \uc2 China's surprise move on Tuesday has been of general benefit of the dollar but sent emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Australian and New Zealand dollars into reverse.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to cause ripples the following day by again setting the yuan's midpoint significantly lower.\par \par \uc2 The focus fell on how much lower the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  would set the yuan midpoint on Wednesday, with the dollar expected to gain a breather should the authorities allow the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken significantly.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was little changed at $0.7372 after bounce sharply from a six-year trough of $0.7217 struck overnight thanks to a bounce in prices of commodities mauled earlier in the week by China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar was also mostly steady at $0.6615 after pulling away from six-year low of $0.6468 plumbed on Wednesday. (Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-13T01:47:50.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150813eb8d000pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
