\par\par \b \uc2 Markets Test China's Vow To Ease Grip\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei in Beijing and Anjani Trivedi in Hong Kong   \par \uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 02:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 After \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  engineered what looked like a win-win -- appearing to cede more control of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to markets while also helping domestic exporters -- now comes the hard part: managing expectations so the yuan doesn't enter a free fall.\par \par \uc2 Only a day after it let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drop sharply, \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up, people familiar with the matter said, underscoring Beijing's tricky task. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Wednesday's final moments of trading came after the yuan weakened nearly 2% -- the daily limit -- to its lowest level against the dollar in four years at 6.45.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  -- dubbed yang ma, or big mama, within China -- truly allows the market's whims to guide the yuan, it risks a fall that would prompt Chinese to send a gusher of money abroad and makes it tougher for companies to pay off dollar-denominated debt. More yuan weakness also is likely to intensify tensions with the U.S., which still believes the yuan is kept artificially low to favor China's exporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commotion comes just after turmoil in China's stock markets, which has made Beijing acutely aware that experimenting with market forces exacerbates the risks both to its credibility and its control of the economy. China's leaders have repeatedly signaled they do want to allow freer capital markets, but are torn between a fear of unleashing these forces and a desire to manage the economy according to their own goals.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the challenge for the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is in a way a classic one for central banks around the world: to let market forces influence its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not so much that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate movements hurt the country's underlying economy, trade relations and political priorities.\par \par \uc2 What adds risk in China is that the government is trying to shift away from a state-controlled economy just as the damaging effects of past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are gathering force.\par \par \uc2 Allowing the market to partially dictate the yuan's value is largely uncharted territory for \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which until now has exercised a tight grip over the yuan and has seen little need to try to define its messages to the market.\par \par \uc2 That is already changing. Early Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to reassure investors, depicting greater volatility in the yuan's trading as a "normal phenomenon" and pledging to keep the exchange rate "basically stable."\par \par \uc2 The surprise yuan devaluation rippled across the world, driving down global stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities prices as investors bet that a deepening slowdown in China's economy could force Beijing to further reduce the yuan's value to beef up growth.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  late Tuesday welcomed the devaluation but signaled Beijing has more to do in letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float freely.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just a day later raised questions about its commitment to a greater role for the market.\par \par \uc2 Since late last year, Chinese leaders had talked up the nation's stock market as a way to help cash-strapped companies raise funds. To that end, the government made it easier for foreign and domestic investors to access and trade Chinese stocks. But soon after equities market started to sell off in mid-June, the government unleashed a flurry of drastic measures to prevent further declines. Many of those steps, such as banning state companies from selling shares and halting new listings, were seen as deeply anti-market.\par \par \uc2 "With all the efforts to make the economy more market-oriented, the government is introducing more risks into its financial system," said Zhu Chaoping, an economist at \uc2 UOB Kay Hian Holdings Ltd\uc2 ., a Singapore-based investment bank. "But whenever it starts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, it puts its credibility on the line."\par \par \uc2 Still, officials at the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  don't see any inconsistency between the latest effort to make the yuan's exchange rate more market-driven and the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, according to people close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, despite the bank's attempt to reassure the market, traders rushed to sell the yuan and businesses flocked to convert their yuan holdings into dollars. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  instructed state-owned Chinese banks to sell dollars on its behalf in the last 15 minutes of Wednesday's trading, according to people close to the state banks.\par \par \uc2 The result: The yuan strengthened about 1% against the dollar in the last few minutes of trading, bringing it to 6.3870 yuan against the dollar. The yuan is down 2.8% since Monday's closing.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 13-08-15 0059GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150813eb8d0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
