\par\par \b \uc2 European Midday Briefing: Stocks Tumble Again as China Fears Mount\b0\par\par\uc2 1,401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 13:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par European Markets: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns about China's economy rippled through global markets for a second straight day on Wednesday, sending stocks sharply lower and putting pressure on some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The pan-European Stoxx Europe 600 index was down 2.3% in early trade, with Germany's exporter-heavy DAX index falling 2.4%.\par \par \uc2 Metals prices and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank and shares in companies that rely on sales to China dropped. Haven \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds climbed.\par \par \uc2 The turmoil followed a second consecutive day when the yuan moved sharply lower, following Beijing's devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a slowdown in the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are taking fright at the recent move in the [yuan]. They are afraid that it signals concern about the Chinese economy," said Mark Evans, a fund manager at THS Partners, which oversees $4.6 billion in assets.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, shares in luxury goods firms \uc2 Burberry Group PLC\uc2  and \uc2 LVMH Mo\u235\'00\'EBt Hennessy Louis Vuitton\uc2  were down 3.4% and 4.0% respectively. French car maker \uc2 PSA Peugeot-Citro\u235\'00\'EBn\uc2  SA fell 4.4% and \uc2 Daimler AG\uc2  was down 3.8%. Mining firm \uc2 Glencore PLC\uc2  was down 4.8%.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan could hurt the competitiveness of firms outside China by making their goods and services relatively more expensive. Companies that sell goods in China could find revenues generated in yuan are worth less in their home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil was flat at $49.17 a barrel. Gold was up 0.9% at $1,118.00 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Copper, aluminum and nickel all hit more than six-year lows, while zinc hit a near four-year low. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par American Markets Outlook: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks were poised for another session with steep losses, with futures sliding in early trade after China allowed its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall sharply for a second straight day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures for the Dow Jones Industrial Average slumped 164 points, or 0.9%, to 17,191, while those for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2  index shaved off 20.95 points, or 1%, to 2,059. Futures for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  100 index erased 46.75 points, or 1%, to 4,465.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday's decision was a one-off move, but on Wednesday the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  allowed the yuan to fall even further, essentially devaluing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a second consecutive day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker yuan is good for Chinese exporters, but bad for international companies exporting to the world's second largest economy, such as car makers, luxury-goods firms and technology companies. For example, Apple shares were down 5.2% on Tuesday, as the yuan devaluation makes the iPhone maker's gadgets more expensive for Chinese buyers. In premarket trade on Wednesday, Apple fell 1.6%, while chip maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Micron Technology\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares shaved off 1.5% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Qualcomm\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 1%. Analysts speculate that the yuan moves could force the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to delay its first rate hike.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Job openings for June are due at 1400 GMT, followed by the Federal budget for July at 1800 GMT. New York Federal Reserve President Dudley will give a speech at 1230 GMT. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday to prop up the yuan in the last minutes of trading, according to people familiar with the matter, in an apparent attempt to prevent an excessive fall in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the authorities seek to give the market more say in setting the exchange rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan had dropped nearly 2% to its lowest level against the dollar during mainland trading, with one dollar buying about 6.45 yuan, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  followed through on its pledge to let market forces play a bigger role in determining the yuan's value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To that end, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set Wednesday's reference rate for the yuan based on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's closing level in the previous trading session. In the past, it had often ignored the daily market moves, at times setting the level--also known as the midpoint, or fixing--so that the yuan was stronger against the dollar even on days after the market indicated it should have been weaker.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the move led to more selling of the yuan, and a statement by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the day trying to reassuring investors that there was "no economic basis" for continued yuan depreciation largely failed to stabilize the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  then instructed state-owned Chinese banks to sell dollars on its behalf in the last 15 minutes of Wednesday's trading, according to the people. The result: The yuan jumped about 1% in value against the dollar in the final moments of trading, bringing it to a level where one dollar would buy 6.3870 yuan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is now down 2.8% since Monday's closing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose 0.9% against the U.S. dollar to $1.1141. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par =========================== \par Top Stories: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eurozone Industrial Production Falls\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial production across the eurozone declined more than expected in June, as output fell in the region's three largest economies, another sign that lackluster investment continues to restrain economic activity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. Labor Market Shows Signs of Leveling Off\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Britain's unemployment rate edged up in June and wage growth stayed flat, signs that the jobs market may be beginning to level off after months of strong employment gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil Demand Growing at Fastest Pace in Five Years, Says IEA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for oil is increasing at its fastest pace in five years, boosted by an oil-price drop below $50 a barrel, a top energy watchdog said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ============================ \par Still To Come \par Wednesday, August 12, 2015 \par GMT                        Exp   Prev \par 1230 US      \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank \par              of New York\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \par              \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Dudley\f28 \uc2 \f2  speech \par 1400 US  Jun Job Openings & Labor \par              Turnover Survey \par 1430 US      EIA Weekly Petroleum \par              Status Report \par              Crude Oil        455.28M \par              Net Chg           -4.41M \par              Gasoline         216.73M \par              Net Chg           +0.81M \par              Distillate       144.81M \par              Net Chg           +0.71M \par              Refinery Usage     96.1% \par              Total Prod \par              Supplied          20.22M \par              Net Chg           -0.03M \par 1600 US      World Agricultural \par              Supply, Demand Estimates \par 1800 US  Jul Monthly \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par              Statement of Receipts & \par              Outlays of the U.S. \par              Govt \par 2301 UK  Jul RICS Residential Market \par              Survey \par              House Prices          40 \par 2350 JPN Jun Core Machinery \par              Orders, M/M%       +0.6% \par              Y/Y%              +19.3% \par  \par  ============================ \par Global News: \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China Intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to Support Tumbling Yuan\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the yuan in the last minutes of trading, according to people familiar with the matter, in an apparent attempt to prevent an excessive fall in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  Welcomes China's Yuan Move\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  called China's move to allow a greater role for market forces in the yuan "a welcome step," but signaled Beijing has much further to go in letting its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  float freely. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Other News: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pearson Sells 50% Stake in Publisher of the Economist\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pearson has agreed to sell its 50% stake in the publisher of the Economist magazine to the unit's existing shareholders for about $730 million in cash, the company's latest move to focus on education.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 E.ON\f28 \uc2 \f2  Hurt by Weaker Power Prices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 E.ON SE\f28 \uc2 \f2  has turned a rise in first-half net profit despite weak power and oil prices, as well as the devaluation of the Russian ruble, though its underlying performance betrayed still tough trading conditions for German utilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Airbus\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Safran Clinch Ariane 6 Deal\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Space Agency\f28 \uc2 \f2  kicked off development of Europe's newest space rocket with the award of a $2.67 billion contract to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Airbus Group SE\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Safran SA\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India Seeks Almost $100 Million in Damages From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian government seeks nearly $100 million in damages from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\f28 \uc2 \f2 's local arm, after a food scare involving the company's popular instant noodles forced a nationwide product recall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glaxo Closes Plant After Finding Legionnaires' Bacteria\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Drugmaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 GlaxoSmithKline\f28 \uc2 \f2  shut down a plant in North Carolina after discovering the bacteria that causes Legionnaires' disease in a cooling tower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Talks to Settle 'Dark Pool' Allegations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 , two of the biggest operators of "dark pools," have entered settlement negotiations with the New York attorney general and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Securities and Exchange Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  over allegations of wrongdoing in the private trading venues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to paul.larkins@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 12, 2015 07:01 ET (11:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150812eb8c0017v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
