\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Stage Late Rally\b0\par\par\uc2 2,509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 23:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS AT A GLANCE9\par \par \uc2 (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      LAST     CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                 17402.5  -0.33    0.00% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2                5044.39  7.6      0.15% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2               2086.05  1.98     0.10% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    20392.8  -327.98  -1.58% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2             23916    -582.19  -2.38% \par Shanghai Composite   3886.32  -41.59   -1.06% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  BSE Sensex       27512.3  -353.83  -1.27% \par Australia: S&P/ASX   5382.1   -91.1    -1.66% \par UK: FTSE 100         6571.19  -93.35   -1.40% \par  \par  \par                     PRICE CHG  YIELD% \par U.S. 2 Year         0/32       0.669 \par U.S. 5 Year         0/32       1.523 \par U.S. 10 Year         -2/32     2.15 \par Australia 10 Year   30/32      2.681 \par China 10 Year       0/32       3.58 \par India 10 Year        2/32      7.965 \par Japan 10 Year        11/32     0.363 \par German 10 Year       8/32      0.604 \par  \par  \par                           LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)     0.7381     0.0002 \par Yen (USD/JPY)             124.26     0.06 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)   1173.79    0.21 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)    6.3861     0 \par Euro (EUR/USD)            1.1162     0.0003 \par WSJ Dollar Index          88.32      -0.01 \par  \par  \par               LAST    CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     43.38   0.3     0.70% \par Brent Crude   50.23   0.52    1.05% \par Gold          1123.4  15.7    1.42% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNAPSHOT:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks staged an afternoon rally to recover from a second day of losses following China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation, as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  prices pared earlier gains. The dollar tumbled against the euro and the yen as investors reconsidered expectations for a rate increase in September after China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plunged for a second day. Gold prices rose in safe-haven buying. Oil and gasoline prices soared on signs of higher demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPENING CALL:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing signaled with its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation that the domestic economic slowdown it has failed to reverse is no longer a problem confined within China's borders. It is now the world's problem, too. Chinese officials have cut interest rates four times in the past 12 months, increased the amount of money banks can lend out and pumped funds into the stock market--measures meant to boost domestic demand in the world's second-largest economy. By devaluing the yuan, Chinese authorities are turning to a controversial growth-boosting tactic whose effects by their nature reverberate far and wide.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A cheaper yuan could help boost Chinese exports. It also might complicate the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision about when to start raising U.S. interest rates. It will pressure China's direct trade rivals, such as South Korea and Japan, to follow suit and let their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fall. It raises risks of market volatility in other emerging market economies. More broadly it sends a signal to investors that policy makers in China and elsewhere are straining for tools to address the problem of slow growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EQUITIES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks staged an afternoon rally to recover from a second day of losses following China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation, though global indexes were broadly lower and investors remained unsettled over the implications of the sudden shift in the second-largest world economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While growth concerns in China have been present for months, they were brought to the forefront Tuesday after China devalued its tightly controlled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , causing its biggest one-day loss in two decades.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stock declined sharply in Europe, where economic growth is nascent and more levered to Chinese demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A slowdown in the second-largest economy dims the outlook for multinational companies that sell goods to China and weighs on their stock prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The question that looms large is how much is the Chinese economy slowing," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Art Hogan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , chief market strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wunderlich Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's devaluation might also complicate the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision about when to start raising interest rates for the first time in nearly a decade. That uncertainty pushed the U.S. dollar lower versus the euro and the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors fled stocks that benefit from higher interest rates, such as financials, and sought out bond-like stocks including utilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . reported a smaller-than-expected revenue increase in its latest quarter. The Chinese e-commerce giant also said it has authorized a $4 billion stock-buyback program. Shares declined 6.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Macy's Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . cut its sales guidance for the year after reporting steeper-than-expected drops in sales and profit in its second quarter. Shares fell 4.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aramark Holdings Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said profit slid in its latest quarter, driven by a sales decline in its North American food and support-services segment. The company, which generates a significant chunk of overall revenue overseas, said the stronger dollar hit its earnings. Shares slipped 2.1%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's devaluation of the yuan hit markets across Asia for the second straight day, sending emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fresh lows and stocks in Hong Kong down more than 2%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar tumbled against the euro and the yen as the picture for U.S. interest rates muddied for more investors after China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plunged for a second day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are starting to push back their expectations for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to raise borrowing costs in September as the sinking yuan has increased concerns about Chinese growth and global inflation expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar continued to see volatile trading as investors grappled with the fallout of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 's move on Tuesday to devaluate the yuan by 1.9%. One day after strengthening against most developed-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar weakened as a larger number of investors pulled back from bets that the dollar would rise on the back of a stronger U.S. economy and the prospects of an interest-rate increase in 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, U.S. inflation expectations, a key metric for the Fed, continue to decline under the prospects of a stronger dollar, said Shahab Jalinoos, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the rate-hike-in-2015 story is challenged, the dollar will struggle, " Mr. Jalinoos said. "The market is having to reconsider that scenario, which will be a problem for the dollar over the near-term."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors had piled into the dollar over the latter half of 2014 and earlier this year in anticipation of the Fed raising interest rates around mid-2015, a belief that was shaken in March as U.S. data weakened. Higher U.S. borrowing costs would make the greenback more attractive to yield-hungry investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growing concerns over China's economy continued to grip financial markets, bolstering demand for ultrasafe \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt and sending the yield on the benchmark 10-year note to the lowest level in more than three months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield, a bedrock for global finance, touched 2.047% during the session, the lowest intraday level since the end of April. Yields fall as prices rise. The yield pared the decline in afternoon trade as U.S. stocks rebounded from earlier losses and a $24 billion sale of 10-year notes drew tepid demand, a sign of some buying fatigue in the bond market on lower yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. long-term bond yields have tumbled this quarter after a rise between April and June. Concerns about China's economy and lower commodities prices have boosted investors' appetites for haven debt, highlighting the attractiveness of the $12.7 trillion market, the world's biggest bond market by size, amid sluggish global growth, subdued inflation and historically low yields in high-income countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lower \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields are confounding many investors and analysts who had expected the 10-year yield to rise toward 3%. The prospect of higher interest rates from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , they have argued, would shrink the value of outstanding bonds whose prices had climbed in recent years thanks in part to the Fed's ultraloose monetary stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond prices have been strengthening after China's surprising move on Tuesday to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which followed data showing plunging exports. That generated the biggest one-day loss in the yuan in two decades.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt overall have handed investors a total return--including price gains and interest payments--of 1.25% this quarter through Tuesday, after a 1.58% loss between April and June, according to data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Year to date, the market has posted a return of 1.28% through Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMODITIES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strong signs for demand helped oil bounce and sent gasoline prices soaring.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for crude is increasing at its fastest pace in five years, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Energy Agency\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in its closely watched market report. The report helped calm fears of sluggish demand that had pushed U.S. oil to its lowest settlement in six years on Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gasoline stockpiles in the U.S. fell at a much faster rate than expected last week, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Energy Information Administration\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  is also taking down its largest unit at the Whiting, Ind., refinery for about a month, tightening gasoline markets in the Midwest, analysts and a broker say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gasoline stockpiles fell by 1.3 million barrels in the week ended Aug. 7, EIA said in its weekly report on oil stockpiles. Analysts had expected gasoline supplies to fall by 800,000, and the larger-than-expected draw was the only bullish data in the highly watched gauge of supply and demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It showed oil stockpiles shrank by 1.7 million barrels, 19% shy of analysts' expectations for the week ended Aug. 7. Distillates, which include diesel, added 3 million barrels, three times as much as expected. Diesel futures settled up 2.4 cents, or 1.5%, to $1.5869 a gallon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Aug 2015 17:35 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Stage Late -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EIA data also showed imports up 5.2% for the week at 7.6 million barrels a day, despite U.S. production on pace for its highest tally in more than 30 years. That shows a price-cutting war for customers is still alive and well as exporters such as Saudi Arabia are managing to sell crude into the U.S. despite its own high production and a 60% fall in prices since last year, said Kyle Cooper, managing director of research at IAF Advisors, a Houston consulting firm.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Crude supplies are very, very high," he added, noting that refineries will soon begin maintenance and drain even less from stockpiles. "So those levels are bound to start increasing in the coming weeks."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the short-term setback, the global oversupply of crude may be significantly smaller than some worst-case scenarios suggest, analysts said after reviewing the IEA report. That helped reassure the market after China's plummeting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had spread fear that global demand won't catch up to rampant supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices rose to their highest level in about three weeks, as investors fled to the safe-haven metal after China's devaluation roiled markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S HEADLINES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Huge Blast Rocks Chinese Port City\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A huge explosion rocked the northern Chinese port of Tianjin, killing at least seven people and injuring at least 50, while sending flames and a plume of smoke high into the sky.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets Test \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Vow to Ease Grip\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the yuan a day after it had let it decline sharply, raising questions about its commitment to an exchange rate driven more by supply and demand and less by government direction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cisco\f28 \uc2 \f2  Revenue and Profit Grow\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cisco Systems\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' recent growth pace eased a bit in the latest quarter, but the network-equipment giant and its new chief executive said the company's recent rebound remains on track.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reality Hits Alibaba's Results\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Months after its blockbuster U.S. IPO, Alibaba reported its slowest quarterly revenue growth in more than three years, while its transactions with Chinese consumers also disappointed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  Posts Loss on Education Writedown\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  posted a loss of $379 million in the latest quarter as it wrote down the value of its Amplify digital education unit and started a strategic review of the division.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kraft Heinz to Slash 2,500 Jobs\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kraft Heinz said it will cut 2,500 jobs, including 700 at its Illinois headquarters, as it begins the process of integrating after its merger.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Macy's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Cuts Guidance as Sales, Profit Slide\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Macy's\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut its sales guidance for the year after reporting steeper-than-expected slides in sales and profit in its second quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pure Storage\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Valued at About $3 Billion, Files for IPO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pure Storage\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a data-storage startup valued privately at roughly $3 billion, filed to go public in what may become a crush of IPOs by business-technology firms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dudley: China Moves Have 'Huge Implications,' But Fed Appears on Track\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York President \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Dudley\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have "huge implications for the world economy" but the U.S. appears on track to raise short-term interest rates in the near future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. to Appeal Ruling in \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bailout Dispute\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday filed to appeal a judge's ruling that it violated the law when it took a controlling stake in \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2  in 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alibaba, Tencent Plan to Invest in Internet TV Startup\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As China Ties Fester, Senator Calls for U.S. to Block Bid for American Chip Maker\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  Speaking to Potential Buyers for Fuel-Distribution Business Stake\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece Wins Delay in Privatization Fund\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nomad Set to Buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 Findus Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 's European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Sources Say\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S CALENDAR:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (All times GMT, followed by country and event)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2230 NZ Jul BNZ - BusinessNZ Performance of Manufacturing Index (PMI)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2245 NZ Jul Food Price Index 2350 JPN Jun Orders Received for Machinery\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0100 SKA Aug Bank of Korea Monetary Policy Committee meeting and decision\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0100 AUS Aug Consumer Inflationary Expectations Survey\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0401 MAL Q2 GDP\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0401 MAL Q2 Balance of Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0600 GER Jul CPI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0645 FRA Jul CPI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0800 PHI Philippine Monetary Policy meeting & decision\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0830 H