\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET SNAPSHOT: \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  Stages Biggest Comeback In About 4 Years As Stocks Erase China Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 1,355 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 23:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Wallace Witkowski and Mark DeCambre, MarketWatch\par \par \uc2 Apple swings from 3% loss to 1.5% gain on day\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks erased steep losses Wednesday to close mostly higher, after the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  briefly descended into negative territory for the year and a second day of weakness for the Chinese yuan continued to send shock waves through global markets.\par \par \uc2 Traders unloaded telecommunication and financial stocks as worries about China--the world's second-largest economy--continued after the country \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to manage the devaluation of the yuan ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-intervenes-to-support-tumbling-yuan-2015-08-12-648584"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/\b  \highlight22china\b0 \highlight22 -\b  \highlight22intervenes\b0 \highlight22 -to-support-tumbling-yuan-2015-08-12-648584}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  set in motion what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  described as a pledge to let market forces dictate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's actions have had spillover effects on global markets, raising concerns about the economic health of the country, which is one of the largest importers of goods and buyers of services.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average was virtually flat, closing down 0.33 points at 17,402.51, after having been off by as many as 277 points. Technically, the blue-chip index logged its ninth decline of the past 10 trading days. The last time the index suffered a loss in nine of 10 trading sessions was from May 7 to May 18, 2012, according to \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Session moves of 200 points or more on the Dow have been running well above average this year, accounting for nearly one-fifth of trading days so far. At 27 days with point swings of 200 points or more, 2015 so far has been the most volatile year for the Dow since 2011, which saw 37 trading days where the index closed up or down more than 200 points, according to \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  finished up 1.98 points, or 0.1%, at 2,086.05, after being off by as many as 32 points and venturing into negative territory for the year. The swing higher for the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2 , on a percentage basis, was the biggest positive reversal for the index since Oct. 4., 2011, according to \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite Index closed up 7.60 points, or 0.2%, at 5,044.39, after being down as many as 91 points. The tech-heavy \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  spent much of the day struggling to clear the psychologically significant level of 5,000 reached back in March.\par \par \uc2 In the absence of any big change to China's stance, investors were likely looking at the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2 's near-term support level of 2,050 to stage a turnaround, said Mark Luschini, chief investment strategist at \uc2 Janney Montgomery Scott\uc2 , in an interview. The \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  hit an intraday low of 2,052.09 before bouncing back.\par \par \uc2 "There was pretty violent selling on news that was no longer new, so maybe [China's move] was not as nefarious as it was initially thought," Luschini said.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over the extent of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions remains a big concern for investors in an already struggling U.S. stock market.\par \par \uc2 "The full implications will take some time to play out, ultimately, because we don't know the extent to which China might let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate," said \uc2 Dan Greenhaus\uc2 , chief strategist at BTIG, in emailed comments. "More generally, it's clearly a weak period for U.S. equities and China's news is just piling on," he said.\par \par \uc2 The main stock benchmarks suffered from sharp selling pressure on Tuesday ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/us-stock-futures-skid-after-china-shakes-forex-market-2015-08-11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/us-stock-futures-skid-after-china-shakes-\b  \highlight22forex\b0 \highlight22 -market-2015-08-11}}}\uc2 ), with the Dow tumbling more than 200 points, the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  index dropping 1%, and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Composite Index falling 1.3%. The losses came after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -devaluation maneuvers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said Tuesday's decision was a one-off move, but on Wednesday the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  allowed the yuan to fall further ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-devalues-yuan-for-second-straight-day-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-devalues-yuan-for-second-straight-day-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ), essentially devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second consecutive day.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan is good for Chinese exporters but bad for international companies that compete with Chinese companies or export goods to the world's No. 2 economy, including car makers, luxury-goods firms and technology companies.\par \par \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . shares (AAPL) swung to a 1.5% gain, reversing a more than 3% decline on the day. Apple posted a 5.2% decline Tuesday on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. The yuan devaluation makes the iPhone maker's gadgets more expensive for Chinese buyers.\par \par \uc2 See:The 20 China-exposed stocks to avoid ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/these-are-the-20-china-exposed-stocks-to-avoid-2015-08-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/these-are-the-20-china-exposed-stocks-to-avoid-2015-08-10}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Shares of other technology companies like chip maker \uc2 Micron Technology Inc\uc2 . (MU) and \uc2 Qualcomm Inc\uc2 . (QCOM)closed with slight gains, after paring earlier losses.\par \par \uc2 Luxury-handbag makers including \uc2 Salvatore Ferragamo SpA\uc2  , which fell on Tuesday in the wake of the China news fell for a second day ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/luxury-handbags-and-shoe-makers-shares-slide-for-a-second-day-on-china-worry-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/luxury-handbags-and-shoe-makers-shares-slide-for-a-second-day-on-china-worry-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Analysts speculate that the yuan moves could force the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to delay its first rate hike ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/return-of-currency-war-and-a-drag-on-fed-rate-hike-analysts-on-the-surprise-yuan-devaluation-2015-08-11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/return-of-\b  \highlight22currency\b0 \highlight22 -war-and-a-drag-on-fed-rate-hike-analysts-on-the-surprise-yuan-devaluation-2015-08-11}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Data:\par \par \uc2 Job openings, the number of available jobs in June ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/job-openings-fall-to-525-million-as-quits-rate-stays-at-19-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/job-openings-fall-to-525-million-as-quits-rate-stays-at-19-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ), fell even as companies hired more. There were 5.25 million job openings, down from 5.36 million in May.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Treasury Department said the U.S. was running a $149 billion budget deficit ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/us-runs-149-billion-budget-deficit-in-july-2015-08-12-1491012"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/us-runs-149-billion-budget-deficit-in-july-2015-08-12-1491012}}}\uc2 ) in July.\par \par \uc2 Earnings: Shares of \uc2 Macy's Inc\uc2 .(M) fell 5% after the company cut its outlook.\par \par \uc2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\uc2 . shares (BABA) slumped 5% after the Chinese e-commerce giant reported weaker-than-expected revenue ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/alibaba-shares-tumble-as-revenue-falls-short-of-estimates-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/alibaba-shares-tumble-as-revenue-falls-short-of-estimates-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ) for its fiscal first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Movers and shakers: Shares of \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 's \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway Inc\uc2 . (BRKA) (BRKA) slipped after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Services placed the company's ratings on review ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/sp-to-review-berkshire-hathaways-rating-on-precision-deal-2015-08-11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/sp-to-review-berkshire-hathaways-rating-on-precision-deal-2015-08-11}}}\uc2 ) following news the conglomerate will buy \uc2 Precision Castparts Corp\uc2  ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/berkshire-hathaway-to-buy-precision-castparts-for-32-billion-2015-08-10).(PCP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/berkshire-hathaway-to-buy-precision-castparts-for-32-billion-2015-08-10).(PCP}}}\uc2 ) for about $32 billion. \uc2 S&P\uc2 's move reflects, in part, uncertainty over how Berkshire will finance the deal.\par \par \uc2 Other markets: Crude oil erased earlier losses ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/storyno-meta-for-guid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/storyno-meta-for-guid}}}\uc2 ) and settled higher after the \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2  said demand for oil is increasing at its fastest pace in five years ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/oil-demand-growing-at-fastest-pace-in-5-years-iea-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/oil-demand-growing-at-fastest-pace-in-5-years-iea-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ), boosted by an oil-price drop below $50 a barrel. Gold prices moved higher as the metal drew haven bids ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/gold-prices-head-higher-as-chinas-moves-spark-haven-appeal-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/gold-prices-head-higher-as-chinas-moves-spark-haven-appeal-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The ICE dollar index was down 1% as investors digested the continued slide in the Chinese yuan.\par \par \uc2 European markets were also sharply lower on the yuan news ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-concerns-slam-european-stocks-lower-for-second-day-2015-08-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-concerns-slam-european-stocks-lower-for-second-day-2015-08-12}}}\uc2 ), with the Stoxx Europe 600 index off nearly 3%.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets closed lower almost across the board ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/storyno-meta-for-guid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/storyno-meta-for-guid}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 --Sara Sjolin in London contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 12, 2015 17:16 ET (21:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150812eb8c003nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
