\par\par \b \uc2 Persian Gulf Stock Markets Join Global Rout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nikhil Lohade   \par \uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 18:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 DUBAI--Stock markets in the energy-exporting countries of the Persian Gulf closed lower Wednesday, joining a global markets rout on concerns about China, after the world's second-largest economy sharply devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and depressed oil prices.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fell again on Wednesday despite \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up in late trading. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday pledged to allow the market a greater role in determining the yuan's value, but the timing of the move has raised concerns about the slowdown of growth in China.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan will make dollar-denominated oil more expensive for Chinese buyers, potentially weighing on demand from the world's second-biggest petroleum consumer. Besides, a strengthening dollar may curb the global appetite for crude amid a supply glut and drag oil prices lower.\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision shows that the country's economic slowdown is probably worse than expected, which could impact global growth in the medium term," said Sebastien Henin, the head of asset management at Abu Dhabi-based the National Investor.\par \par \uc2 "For the Gulf region, the main fear is a fall in the demand for oil that would put more pressure on already depressed energy prices," he added.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabian stocks closed 0.6% lower at 8708.31 Wednesday, Dubai's market fell 1.2% to 4022.12, Abu Dhabi's main index shed 1% to 4765.76 and Doha was down 0.1% at 11,772.66.\par \par \uc2 Investors are already worried about the impact of weaker oil prices on the petrodollar-dependent regional economies. A potential slowdown in demand from China, one of the biggest customers for the Gulf's energy exports, will only exacerbate those concerns.\par \par \uc2 But there are also some positive aspects, analysts say. The Gulf countries import a substantial amount of goods from China, and the recent devaluation might have a positive impact on inflation, a recent concern following the sharp reduction of subsidies in several countries in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 "Much will depend on the pace of CNY weakening going forward. After the initial volatility, the CNY's devaluation will likely be gradual," said Monica Malik, the chief economist at \uc2 Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank\uc2 . "Nevertheless, it still does support the outlook for the strong USD. This will keep imported inflation low for the GCC countries."\par \par \uc2 Besides, "a weaker yuan might reduce demand for oil, but this will surely be far outweighed by the sizeable fall in oil prices over the past year," said \uc2 William Jackson\uc2 , senior emerging markets economist at Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 Write to Nikhil Lohade at Nikhil.Lohade@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 12 Aug 2015 12:54 ET  Persian Gulf Stock Markets Join Global Rout--Update\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Nikhil Lohade \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DUBAI--Stock markets in the energy-exporting countries of the Persian Gulf closed lower Wednesday, joining a global markets rout on concerns about China, after the world's second-largest economy sharply devalued its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and depressed oil prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan fell again on Wednesday despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop it up in late trading. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday pledged to allow the market a greater role in determining the yuan's value, but the timing of the move has raised concerns about the slowdown of growth in China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker yuan will make dollar-denominated oil more expensive for Chinese buyers, potentially weighing on demand from the world's second-biggest petroleum consumer. Besides, a strengthening dollar may curb the global appetite for crude amid a supply glut and drag oil prices lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decision shows that the country's economic slowdown is probably worse than expected, which could impact global growth in the medium term," said Sebastien Henin, the head of asset management at Abu Dhabi-based the National Investor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the Gulf region, the main fear is a fall in the demand for oil that would put more pressure on already depressed energy prices," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saudi Arabian stocks closed 0.6% lower at 8708.31 Wednesday, Dubai's market fell 1.2% to 4022.12, Abu Dhabi's main index shed 1% to 4765.76 and Doha was down 0.1% at 11,772.66.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are already worried about the impact of weaker oil prices on the petrodollar-dependent regional economies. A potential slowdown in demand from China, one of the biggest customers for the Gulf's energy exports, will only exacerbate those concerns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But there are also some positive aspects, analysts say. The Gulf countries--most of which peg their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the U.S. dollar--import a substantial amount of goods from China, and the recent devaluation might have a positive impact on inflation, a recent concern following the sharp reduction of subsidies in several countries in the Middle East.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Much will depend on the pace of CNY weakening going forward. After the initial volatility, the CNY's devaluation will likely be gradual," said Monica Malik, the chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Nevertheless, it still does support the outlook for the strong USD. This will keep imported inflation low for the GCC countries."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Besides, "a weaker yuan might reduce demand for oil, but this will surely be far outweighed by the sizeable fall in oil prices over the past year," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Jackson\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior emerging markets economist at Capital Economics.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Nikhil Lohade at Nikhil.Lohade@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 12, 2015 12:54 ET (16:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150812eb8c002im\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
