\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Support Tumbling Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei   \par \uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 12:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--\b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to prop up the yuan in the last minutes of trading, according to people familiar with the matter, in an apparent attempt to prevent an excessive fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the authorities seek to give the market more say in setting the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The yuan had dropped nearly 2% to its lowest level against the dollar during mainland trading, with one dollar buying about 6.45 yuan, as the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  followed through on its pledge to let market forces play a bigger role in determining the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 To that end, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set Wednesday's reference rate for the yuan based on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's closing level in the previous trading session. In the past, it had often ignored the daily market moves, at times setting the level--also known as the midpoint, or fixing--so that the yuan was stronger against the dollar even on days after the market indicated it should have been weaker.\par \par \uc2 But the move led to more selling of the yuan, and a statement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day trying to reassuring investors that there was "no economic basis" for continued yuan depreciation largely failed to stabilize the market.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  then instructed state-owned Chinese banks to sell dollars on its behalf in the last 15 minutes of Wednesday's trading, according to the people. The result: The yuan jumped about 1% in value against the dollar in the final moments of trading, bringing it to a level where one dollar would buy 6.3870 yuan.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now down 2.8% since Monday's closing.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 12, 2015 06:17 ET (10:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150812eb8c0018v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
