\par\par \b \uc2 Macy's\uc2  Misses, Cuts Amid Lessened Markdowns -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 14:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:07 EDT - \uc2 Macy's\uc2  (M) continues to disappoint Wall Street with its 4th miss of consensus EPS views the past 9 quarters--and 3 of the past 5--after beating estimates for 6 years straight previously. However, this one is easily the biggest while F2Q same-store sales fell 2.1% excluding licensed areas. As the retailer cuts guidance for the year--including now seeing flat SSS after previously anticipating 2% growth--the company also announces a China e-commerce JV with Fung Retailing. Whether that merely proves to be a distraction to management while US \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to soften remains to be seen. But CEO \uc2 Terry Lundgren\uc2  in part blames 2Q's struggles on the port slowdown and resultant delays in markdown activity. M falls 3% premarket to $63.50 after already falling 8.3% from last month's record high. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 8:06 EDT - Soda maker \uc2 Dr Pepper Snapple\uc2  (DPS) is paying $20M for an 11.7% stake in BodyArmor, the sports drink startup headed by Vitaminwater co-founder Mike Repole and backed by investors including \uc2 NBA\uc2  star Kobe Bryant. The deal helps DPS take aim at a category dominated by \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2 's (PEP) Gatorade and \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 's (KO) Powerade. BodyArmor, already distributed by DPS, only had about $30M in retail sales last year but is growing quickly. Other investors include co-founder \uc2 Lance Collins\uc2 , who developed the Fuze and NOS brands before KO acquired them, and \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback Andrew Luck and MLB outfielder Mike Trout. (mike.esterl@wsj.com; @mikeesterl)\par \par \uc2 7:54 EDT - \uc2 Alibaba Group\uc2  (BABA) is learning to squeeze more out of mobile. In its Chinese retail marketplaces, its mobile monetization rate reached 2.16% in the quarter ended in June, up from 1.49% a year ago and up from 1.73% in the quarter ended in March. E-commerce companies have a harder time getting revenue from mobile because of issues such as less screen space on which to sell ads than on desktop computers. (carlos.tejada@wsj.com; @CRTejada)\par \par \uc2 7:40 EDT - CF is really revamping itself. Days after reaching a stock deal for fertilizer assets of European peer OCI (OCI.AE), the company has ink an agreement with big American farmer cooperative \uc2 CHS\uc2 . It will invest $2.8B for a minority stake in a CF nitrogen unit and be able to buy UAN and urea at market prices. "This transaction matches us with a reliable partner that will take ratable delivery of product across the year, supported by an attractive valuation," says CF CEO Tony Will. His counterpart at \uc2 CHS\uc2 , \uc2 Carl Casale\uc2 , adds the deal--the entity's largest-ever investment--"positions \uc2 CHS\uc2  and our owners for long-term dependable fertilizer supply, supply-chain efficiency and economic value." CF, inactive premarket, slid 6.5% yesterday after spiking last month on word talks with OCI were occurring. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:35 EDT - On a revenue basis, \uc2 Alibaba Group\uc2  (BABA) is now mostly mobile. The e-commerce giant said mobile revenue from its China retail marketplaces exceeded desktop revenue for the first time. Mobile accounted for 51% in the June-ended quarter, it said. Alibaba said its mobile monetization rate -- or what it makes on mobile transactions -- totaled 2.16%, up from 1.49% a year ago. Still, its overall monetization rate fell to 2.33% from 2.52% in part because it makes more on a percentage basis from nonmobile sources. BABA falls 5.9% to $72.81 premarket.(carlos.tejada@wsj.com; @CRTejada)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EDT - With the Dow industrials closing just 1.4% above the year's lowest finish, a new standard could well be set today if the projected early selling in US stocks can maintain itself all day. With technicals already breaking down, including the index's first "Death Cross" in 4 years, really the last main support before a potential noted decline (being 5% from May's high doesn't cut it) is 17000-17100 for the DJIA. That area proved to be resistance throughout 3Q14 and support during early winter's pullbacks. Can it be again now? \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  futures are down 18 as oil rebounds 1% and gold rises nearly that much. Sovereign debt continues to gain, with the 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield down to 2.11%, and the dollar is lower; the euro is at $1.1150. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:24 EDT - \uc2 SunGard\uc2  pursuing both the IPO and buyout tracks shows that strategy was a winner as the financial-software firm has indeed agreed to be acquired by FIS in a deal valued at some $9.2B. Roughly half that is current \uc2 SunGard\uc2  long-term debt which still remains following its acquisition nearly a decade ago by a cadre of private-equity firms for some $11B. But those investors--which include Bain, \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  (BX) and \uc2 KKR\uc2 --had to hold on to \uc2 SunGard\uc2  longer than the normal investment amid the financial crisis and years of uneven results. Its focus tightened amid the sale several years ago in shedding some assets, including its higher-education business. FIS is inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:15 EDT - Bigger-than-expected growth again at Wayfair (W) as the online homegoods seller continues to move closer to profitability. Its 2Q revenue jumped 66% from a year earlier, and 81% on a direct basis (which largely means through the websites of W's 5 brands). And repeat customers continue to make up a bigger chunk of business at 57% of orders last quarter versus 52% a year ago. In all, the numbers should soothe those who've already bid up W shares 92% this year, including 4.5% this week. They're inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:09 EDT - Alibaba (BABA) drops in initial premarket trading despite a 1c EPS beat as revenue growth was less than Wall Street anticipated. Gross-merchandise-volume increases continued to slow, coming in at 33% on-year in F1Q versus 40% in 4Q. But mobile continues to be a bigger piece of the pie, making up 55% of last quarter's transaction volume, compared with 4Q's 51%. Monthly active mobile users increased 18M during the quarter to 307M, less than the 4Q's 24M-person increase. BABA, which was fell upwards of 8% in the moments after the release, is currently down 4.6% premarket at $73.80, which would be fresh post-IPO low. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 6:55 EDT - Itau says despite Brazil's economic woes, the country's paper-and-pulp seem to be in good shape for the next few years as the industry is likely to take advantage of the expected continued depreciation of the real versus the dollar. That should further increase the competitiveness of local product. Much of Brazil's pulp is exported and most paper is kept domestically. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 6:39 EDT - Below-consensus F4Q results from Applied Industrial (AIT) and a muted new-year view as CEO \uc2 Neil Schrimsher\uc2  the parts distributor has record revenue and EPS for the past year, "we are not satisfied" as results "reflect the continued impact of reduced demand in certain industrial end markets" and current economic conditions are seen persisting through New Year's. With shares already at 2 1/2-year lows in recent days, it would seem there's no immediate impetus for a sustained stock rebound. Down 16% in 2015, AIT is inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 6:14 EDT - \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is quickly learning the impacts of listening to the market when setting policy. Many times, when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  embarks on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --or in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case, make it a little less valuable--more than a few investors will sell initially to see just how much stomach policy makers have to sustain their desire on what the asset in question should be valued at. The PBoC, of course, yesterday moved the midpoint of its daily allowable trading range in line with where the yuan had been trading after the bank's range had largely been above market value of late. When given the chance yesterday to sell further, traders did. That continued today, prompting reported PBoC efforts to prop up the yuan. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 12, 2015 08:07 ET (12:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150812eb8c001ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
