\par\par \b \uc2 China Learns What Happens When You Listen to the Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Kingsbury   \par \uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 13:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is quickly learning the impacts of listening to the market when setting policy, and the potential unintended consequences therein.\par \par \uc2 Many times, when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  embarks on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --or in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case, make it a little less valuable--more than a few investors will sell initially to see just how much stomach policy makers have to sustain their wish on what the asset in question should be valued. The PBoC, of course, on Tuesday moved the midpoint of its daily allowable trading range in line with where the yuan had been trading after the bank's range had largely been above market value of late.\par \par \uc2 When given the chance yesterday to sell further, traders did. That continued today, prompting PBoC efforts to prop up the yuan at the end of today's session in China, The Wall Street Journal is reporting.\par \par \uc2 It will be quite interesting to see where exactly the PBoC puts the trading-range midpoint for Thursday. Will it try to stem the selling by keeping it near where today's session ended, around 6.3870 yuan per dollar? Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did that, it would mean a third-straight sizable cut to the reference rate after Tuesday's 1.9% drop and the 1.6% decline done for today's session.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps it might even put the reference rate near today's level in order to try and show it's seen enough of this week's selling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ahead of today's close shows policy makers are already feeling queasy--a sign they also illustrated last month in trying to stem the sharp pullback in Chinese stocks.\par \par \uc2 It's all a sign that while some in China's government want to let investors decide asset values, fully let go of old habits when the selling picks up is tough. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 12, 2015 07:17 ET (11:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150812eb8c001dl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
