\par\par \b \uc2 China  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Seen Shielding Yuan From Further Selloff -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 06:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent the yuan from suffering a sharper selloff, a day after it gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  what it called a "one-off" devaluation via the reference exchange rate. The dollar-yuan exchange rate is now trading at 6.4260, down from an intraday high of 6.4301 earlier. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair closed at 6.3231 yesterday. "I see the national team defending the yuan against the dollar around the 6.4300 level," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading of a domestic bank, referring to agent banks acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Without such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan would have headed even lower for sure," he said, but added that given China's huge foreign reserves, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has enough ammunition to maintain the yuan's stability in the long run. (hong.shen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: CNG\par \par \uc2 (Delayed by 1 hour)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 12-08-15 0414GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150812eb8c0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
