\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Sliding yuan lays bare uselessness of  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  markets\b0\par\par\uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2015\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Edward Hadas\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 11 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Why has the yuan been devalued by 2 percent against the dollar? It could be market forces. Or a subtle bid for inclusion in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's reserve basket. Or a timid sign of desperation during the early phases of a financial disaster. Or none of these. The fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders can only speculate lays bare the inherent uselessness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Those who seek to make money on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are used to making mistakes. While they can sometimes anticipate daily or weekly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, longer-term trends are beyond them. The way \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values swing around is a rational financial response to the numerous expectations placed on exchange rates in the current global system.\par \par \uc2 On one hand, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a tool. Politicians and central bankers sometimes fix or nudge them to counter economic weakness or to demonstrate economic strength. China's 2 percent devaluation is a subtle example. Turkmenistan and Kazakhstan have given examples of the less subtle kind. Economists treat \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as something that helps to smooth out irregularities. They expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to allocate capital efficiently across borders and to reduce trade imbalances. Investors, meanwhile, see a potential profit opportunity. They want \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to offer a fair mix of risk and return.\par \par \uc2 The list is too long and inconsistent to be realistic, even without Chinese-style official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade surplus, the yuan should rise. To push up GDP, it should fall. To help exporters plan long-term investments, it should be stable. And to respond to current economic trends, it should be volatile. The results of the m\u233\'00\'E9lange of motivations include investment-harming uncertainty, financial excess and fairly frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crises.\par \par \uc2 Political supervision is the best way to tame wild exchange rates. No wonder that national capital controls, which limit the flow of funds across national borders, are coming back into intellectual fashion. But cooperation is more effective than unilateral action. The Bretton Woods agreement, in which governments agreed to minimise the swings between their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , provided the world's biggest economies with two decades of relative stability after World War Two. Perhaps it is time for a new version.\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par               \par CONTEXT NEWS\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the reference rate for the yuan almost 2 percent weaker against the U.S. dollar on Aug. 11. The new rate of 6.2298 compares with the previous fixing of 6.1162 against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  described the adjustment in the partially controlled Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a "one-time correction".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  statement \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/1MhdY92"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/1MhdY92}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - Reuters: China devalues yuan after poor economic data\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RELATED COLUMNS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reasons to stay strong\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Big jolt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brownian motion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by John Foley and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sarah Bailey\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (On \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/edwardhadas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/edwardhadas}}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-14T16:03:21.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150811eb8b00htr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
