\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar's Reserve Status Appears Safe -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2015\par 20:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:47 EDT - While China devalues its yuan, in part at least to advance its efforts to be considered a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , US Secretary of State John Kerry is saying the dollar could lose its role in global reserves if the US rejects the Iran nuclear deal. He warns there may be calls for another reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the US is viewed as "misbehaving" and "not living by agreements that it negotiates itself." Such remarks are a departure from \uc2 US government\uc2  officials' typical reticence toward commenting on the dollar. But investors will need a lot more than that to start worrying about the greenback's reserve status. The dollar dominates in how countries conduct trade, and analysts say there are no viable alternatives today threatening to dethrone the dollar. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:40 EDT - The latest edition of \uc2 UAW\uc2  Local 862's internal On Line magazine is available on the organization's website, and the cover is provocative. Local 862 represents \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  truck workers in Kentucky. On the cover: a snake with its mouth open poised to attack. In the background is Ford's "Glass House" headquarters building with the iconic blue oval logo. The cover declares "ready to strike...if provoked. The issue comes out as \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials and executives at Detroit auto makers hash out a new four year agreement. In the magazine, labor officials said a strike is not in the plans, but warned members to prepare by being financially prudent ahead of time. The contract ends Sept. 14. In a note to members, Local 862 President Todd Dunn encouraged members to participate in a strike authorization vote. "It is this vote that will send a clear message that we are fully engaged in our 2015 Negotiations," he said. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:35 EDT - The value of US poultry and eggs shipped abroad in 1H fell 14% to $2.41B, says industry group USA Poultry & Egg Export Council, citing federal data. It calls the drop a "graphic example of the economic effect" from the spring's avian-flu outbreak across the Midwest, which triggered bans on US products which resulted in a nearly $390M loss in exports by the group's count. Some countries have begun easing restrictions recently as the most-recent detection of the virus was more than 7 weeks ago. (kelsey.gee@wsj.com; @kelseykgee)\par \par \uc2 14:10 EDT - Big food companies contending with a surge of public concern in recent years about genetically modified organisms in recent years may be garnering a new ally. \uc2 BB&T\uc2  notes a spate of media articles in recent months linking GMOs with alleviating global malnutrition and diminishing pesticide usage. The investment bank says conventional food companies should harness those kinds of arguments to "ease pressure to replace GMOs with more-expensive alternatives and potentially create some public goodwill." (ilan.brat@wsj.com; @ilanbrat)\par \par \uc2 14:08 EDT - News that ad-blocking software could cost the digital-advertising industry $22B this year is big considering the total value of digital advertising is about $50B. But growth figures are even-more telling: In the US, the number of users spiked 48% the last year while ad blocking in the UK surged 82%, according to a report by \uc2 Adobe\uc2  and startup PageFair. Despite consumers' zeal for ad blockers, the struggle between paid- and free-content models is far from resolved. In surveys, users overwhelmingly say they are unwilling to pay for ad-free content--even if they dislike ads. Ad blockers give consumers a quick fix, but they're likely to be an interim solution: The digital-ad industry is moving fast to figure out ways to get around this technology. (elizabeth.dwoskin@wsj.com; lizzadwoskin)\par \par \uc2 13:51 EDT - \uc2 Rackspace\uc2  (RAX) hits a 15-month low following its 2Q report as some on Wall Street wonder where the cloud-computing firm is headed. Pacific Crest says the company's "turnaround is taking longer than expected" while \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  questions a new strategy of taking on debt amid RAX's "rapid asset turnover and short contract mix as well as quickly changing environment." Borrowing will go toward stock buybacks. But some investment banks say the 2Q results and guidance, which included an expected lowering of 2015 views, were better than feared. While slashing its price target to $42 from $60, \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  remains bullish, saying RAX ultimately expressed "significant optimism around the company's longer-term competitive positioning." RAX slides 12% to $27.91, putting the year's slump at 40%.(patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 13:38 EDT - In the near-term, the move by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to devalue the yuan stands to benefit haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as uncertainty grows over China's economy, demand for commodities and global inflationary pressures, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Daragh Maher. In this environment, expect the yen and the dollar to strengthen, as investors turn to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that traditionally hold their value in turbulent times. "The markets will be cautious as they watch how this situation evolves in the coming days," Maher says. "You've got summer markets and thinner liquidity, and now another layer of uncertainty." WSJ Dollar Index, which compares greenback against a basket of 16 commonly traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up 0.5% to 89.03. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:37 EDT - \uc2 BE Aerospace\uc2  (BEAV) \uc2 Amin Khoury\uc2  joins the chorus expressing surprise at the price offered by \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  (BRKB) for \uc2 Precision Castparts\uc2  (PCP). Khoury, who is also chairman of rival aerospace parts specialist KLX, says at an investor conference that his "guess" is PCP's outlook isn't as strong as many analysts and outsiders expected. PCP recently down 8 cents at $230.84, still below the $235 offer price. (doug.cameron@wsj.com; @dougcameron)\par \par \uc2 13:34 EDT - If nothing else, Isle of Capri (ISLE) closing its Natchez, Miss., casino and selling nongaming assets there for $11.5M removes some distraction from the 14-casino regional operator. The facility was the smallest revenue generator for the company last FY at just 1.9% and the only one with an operating loss besides a newly opened facility in Pennsylvania. ISLE bought Natchez in 2000 as part of its acquisition of Lady Luck Gaming. ISLE has ticked up from session lows on the news and is now off 2.5% on the day at $18.52; shares have more than doubled this year. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EDT - The risk-off tone in global markets boosts demand for the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2 's $24B sale of 3-year notes, the first leg of this week's new offerings. The new notes sold at a 1.013% yield, in line with dealers' expectations. The indirect bidding, a gauge of foreign demand, jumps to 52.8%, a multi-year high, from 47.7% a month ago. Direct bids pull back to 8.2%, the lowest since March. Three-year notes are among the most vulnerable if the Fed raises rates, so the strong demand comes as China's yuan devaluation overnight casts doubt over potential Fed tightening next month. Ten-year yield near session lows at 2.119%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:07 EDT - While many in the markets believe the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have acted to ward off the effects of an expected Fed rate increase on the yuan, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  group. The yuan's trading band tethers it to a fixing value within 2% in either direction against the dollar, but the dollar's relative strength has tended to push the yuan higher against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And when the Fed raises rates, the dollar will likely strengthen even more, potentially pulling the yuan up with it. "In that environment, it certainly makes sense for China's policy makers to buy some flexibility ahead of Fed lift-off, in particular since the fix had become very 'peg-like' in its stability in recent months," Goldman says. "In short, this is about Fed lift-off most obviously and further dollar strength." (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:06 EDT - The yuan's devaluation will hurt American steelmakers, says the industry's chief Washington lobbyist. "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has consistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to control the value of the yuan versus the US dollar to make their exports more competitive and impose new barriers to imports," contends \uc2 Tom Gibson\uc2 , head of the \uc2 American Iron and Steel Institute\uc2 . He calls for the US to impose more import tariffs: "Our government must address the massive damage that China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is causing to our nation's manufacturing sector, especially the steel industry." (john.miller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2015 14:47 ET (18:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150811eb8b002tg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
