\par\par \b \uc2 China Acts to Devalue the Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2015\par 04:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued its tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , causing its biggest one-day loss in two decades, as the world's second-largest economy continues to sputter.\par \par \uc2 Chinese authorities said the change would help drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward more market-driven movements. The move also signaled the government's growing worry about slow growth. A shift toward a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help flagging exports at a time when many other efforts to boost the economy haven't proven very effective.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan has been on an upward track for a decade, during which the country's economy grew to be the second largest in the world and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained importance globally. The devaluation Tuesday was the most significant downward adjustment to the yuan since 1994, when as part of a break from Communist state planning, Beijing let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall by one-third.\par \par \uc2 China sets a midpoint for the value of the yuan against the U.S. dollar. In daily trading, the yuan is allowed to move 2% above or below that midpoint, which is called the daily fixing. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sometimes ignores the daily moves, at times setting the fixing so that the yuan is stronger against the dollar a day after the market has indicated it should be weaker.\par \par \uc2 With Tuesday's move, the fixing will now be based on how the yuan closes in the previous trading session. As a result, the yuan's fixing was weakened by 1.9% Tuesday from the previous day, leaving it at 6.2298 to the U.S. dollar, compared with 6.1162 on Monday. The yuan dropped as much as 1.99% from its previous close to 6.3360 against the dollar in Shanghai and fell as much as 2.3% in Hong Kong in early trading.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement posted on its website that the yuan's midpoint has diverged quite a bit from the market rate for a relatively long time and that it was time to make the midpoint more market-based.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has astutely combined a move to weaken the yuan with a shift to a more market-determined exchange rate," said Eswar Prasad, a \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  professor and former China head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China's leadership has been urging the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to declare the yuan an official reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on par with the dollar, euro, the Japanese yen and the British pound\u8212\'20\'14a move that could raise China's influence on the world stage just as Beijing increasingly challenges Washington in global affairs.\par \par \uc2 To that end, the leadership has been resisting an outright devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the country's plunging exports, for fears that a move like that could jeopardize the yuan's chance of joining the elite group of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rather, it chose to do so by giving what the longtime critics of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy have long been clamoring for: Let the market play a bigger role in deciding the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 The engineered fall in the yuan is likely to cause political ripples around the world. In particular it may reignite criticism of China's tight control over the yuan's exchange rate within the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  and some American businesses, which have long said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already too weak and set at a rate that allowed Chinese exporters to sell their goods artificially cheap on world markets.\par \par \uc2 As recently as April, other central bankers were speaking confidently that China wouldn't devalue. This puts pressure on them to follow suit. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move also could pose a challenge for the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 . \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is preparing to raise U.S. interest rates later this year. One source of concern for the Fed this year has been a strong U.S. dollar, which is squeezing exports and helping to hold U.S. inflation below the Fed's 2% target. China's move puts more upward pressure on the dollar, which could be exacerbated further when the Fed actually raises rates.\par \par \uc2 In a semiannual report about world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates published in April, the Treasury Department credited China for allowing the yuan to rise in recent years but also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained "significantly undervalued" and "that fundamental factors for [yuan] appreciation remain intact, highlighting the need for further strengthening over the medium-term."\par \par \uc2 But in May, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said China's yuan was no longer undervalued for the first time in more than a decade, a milestone in the country's efforts to open its economy.\par \par \uc2 Despite China's depiction of its move as an effort to let the market determine the yuan's value, the devaluation is likely to rankle policymakers in Washington given what they saw as commitments from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After high-level talks with Chinese officials in June, U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "only when it's necessitated by disorderly market conditions and will consider additional measures to transition to a market-oriented exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 Still, U.S. officials, who had objected to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's latest assessment of the yuan, considered that a narrow victory given China's considerable latitude in determining what constituted "disorderly conditions."\par \par \uc2 "The real test is going to be, when there's upward pressure on the [yuan], will \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" Mr. Lew said at the time. "And that is still an open issue."\par \par \uc2 The news comes weeks before China's President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is due to visit Washington for a summit with U.S. President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who has resisted calls to describe Beijing as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator during his time in office but is now likely to face new pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  question.\par \par \uc2 China's decision to allow the yuan to depreciate is likely to create an outcry in Congress as the presidential-election season heats up, said \uc2 Conference Board\uc2  economist \uc2 Andrew Polk\uc2 . While China has taken the step for domestic reasons, that is unlikely to stop a divided Congress from reacting, he said. "It's going to be a headache. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to have a field day with this," he added, referring to the real-estate developer and Republican presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 The devaluation on Tuesday comes just over 10 years after China began a process that sharply lifted the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and which together with other efforts to restructure its exchange rate helped make the yuan one of the most actively used \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia.\par \par \uc2 On July 21, 2005, China surprised global financial markets by saying it would remove a hard peg that for over a decade had valued the U.S. dollar at 8.28 yuan. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2 's initial 2.1% move in 2005 pushed the dollar down to 8.11 yuan, and the yuan kept rising in subsequent years. By yesterday's market close in Shanghai trading, the U.S. dollar bought 6.2097 yuan, meaning the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had risen about 33% against the dollar during the decade.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's move could give a shot in the arm to China's weakened exports sector, which remains an important part of the economy despite Beijing's effort to seek growth beyond its traditional sources. On Saturday, Chinese officials said July exports fell a surprising 8.3% from a year earlier. Exports for the first seven months of the year were down 0.8% in dollar terms compared with a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The strong exchange rate against the euro has been a drag on China's exports. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has kept the yuan steady against the dollar but doesn't as regularly influence its exchange rate against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing the yuan to follow the dollar's rise against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the euro. The latest data showed exports to the \uc2 European Union\uc2  fell 12% in July from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 China's economic growth in the second quarter came in at 7% year over year, the slowest pace in six years, due to a weak property market and soft domestic demand in addition to exports. China's cabinet, the State Council, said last month it would offer tax breaks and other incentives to help the trade sector. Other measures Beijing has taken include four rate interest-rate cuts since November to provide funds for domestic companies.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action raises questions about whether China will be able to pull off its shift to an economy oriented toward domestic demand and away from export-led growth.\par \par \uc2 "The real proof in whether this change is about reform or growth will come when authorities resist the urge to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  down the road when another policy goal that could be achieved by a significant revaluation or devaluation comes knocking," said Scott Kennedy, an analyst at the \uc2 Center for Strategic & International Studies\uc2 , a Washington think tank.\par \par \uc2 "China wasn't able to resist that urge on the stock market, so the government doesn't get the benefit of the doubt on this quite yet," Mr. Kennedy said, a reference to China's recent moves to prevent further declines in its equities markets.\par \par \uc2 China shares wavered between positive and negative territory after the announcement, a day after Shanghai posted its largest daily percentage gain in a month. The Shanghai Composite Index was last flat at 3929.26 while the smaller Shenzhen market was up 0.7% at 2290.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 10, 2015 22:25 ET (02:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150811eb8b0005e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
