\par\par \b \uc2 RPT GLOBAL MARKETS-Stock markets see grim Chinese data and decide it's good news\b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2015\par 14:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Global designation to headline; no change in text.)\par \par \uc2 * Some stock markets prosper, commodities take a hit\par \par \uc2 * Europe off to subdued start, mining stocks fall\par \par \uc2 * Some optimism over Greece\par \par \uc2 * Malaysian ringgit plumbs 17-year lows\par \par \uc2 By Lionel Laurent\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 10 (Reuters) - Slowing demand in China led some stock markets to rise on Monday, on hopes of more policy stimulus, but commodity prices fell.\par \par \uc2 China's stock markets ignored caution in Asian equities, with major indices up between 2 and 4 percent. European trading got off to a subdued start as mining and energy stocks kept UK shares performing worse than the euro zone's.\par \par \uc2 Chinese producer prices in July hit their lowest point since late 2009 and exports tumbled 8.3 percent in the same month. That stoked expectations of more action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after months of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities to tame \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unruly stock market.\par \par \uc2 "Expectations of further easing are building and announcements of liberalisation have boosted the equity market," said Kit Juckes, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 The outlook in China contrasted with solid U.S. data on Friday, which kept on track expectations that interest rates would rise as early as September.\par \par \uc2 The dollar stayed near a four-month high against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. Ten-year German and \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields were one basis point higher. U.S. equity futures were 0.4 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 "The dislocation from a strong dollar, rising long rates and falling oil prices is still being factored into share prices in our view," said Sean Darby, a strategist at Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI All-Country World index was flat. Emerging-market equities were down 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone equities rose after a survey showed investor and analyst sentiment weakened only slightly in August, suggesting a relatively robust economic recovery. Major financial shares got a lift from broker upgrades.\par \par \uc2 There was also some optimism over Greece, where an official said the government hoped to wrap up on Tuesday talks on a new bailout. The benchmark Athens stock-market index rose 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Commodity prices took a hit: London copper traded at six-year lows and crude oil futures touched multi-month lows before recovering. London-listed mining shares like \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  and \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  fell around 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit dropped to lows last seen during the Asian financial crisis 17 years ago, after a fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves raised doubts over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to withstand pressure from political uncertainty and slower growth.\par \par \uc2 Turkish stocks fell and the lira slipped after an attack on the U.S. consulate building in Istanbul and a car bombing at a police station that injured 10 people, weeks after Turkey launched what it described as a "synchronised war on terror". (Editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-10T13:45:46.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150810eb8a00g2g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
