\par\par \b \uc2 China's Yuan Slides on \uc2 IMF\uc2  Reluctance to Confer SDR Status -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 234 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2015\par 05:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/CNY spiked briefly to 6.2122 from its Tuesday close of 6.2096, implying a 0.04% depreciation of the yuan versus the U.S. dollar, after the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  issued a report saying that the yuan is not yet ready for inclusion into the special drawing rights basket. Inclusion in the SDR would confer upon the yuan the status of a global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yuan fell in a kneejerk reaction to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  report, but then recovered on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of wrangling the yuan into a tiny range over the past three weeks. While the yuan's move was miniscule, it is the biggest move the market has seen since early July. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  said the yuan is still not "freely usable" and that access to onshore markets and a market-determined interest rate--two key factors that support SDR-inclusion--are still absent. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  said its decision to alter the SDR composition could be delayed into 2016.\par \par \uc2  USD/CNY is now 6.2097 versus its Tuesday close of 6.2096. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor JSM\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 04, 2015 22:59 ET (02:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 05-08-15 0341GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020150805eb850000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
