\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Down 0.8%; US Jobs Data Wait Induces Selling -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2015\par 03:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is down 0.8% at 20415.70 as futures selling leads the cash market lower; the dollar is also down (USD/JPY now 123.94). All eyes are now turning to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  jobs data due this Friday, say traders. "This is the critical round of employment figures for the Fed to decide on a September rate rise, so the stakes are high," says Tatsunori Kawai, investment strategist at \uc2 kabu.com Securities\uc2 . "Weak figures will likely cause the hike to be put off, while strong numbers will have to be really convincing to sway the market." He notes that with most Japanese manufacturers taking next week off for the annual 'Obon' summer holiday, stock market participation levels are likely to remain thin, even as corporate earnings results continue to come in. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) is up 7.1% at Y4,261 after booking a strong 1Q. \uc2 Toyota Tsusho\uc2  (8015.TO) is down 7.7% after booking weaker on-year results. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Dairy giant Fonterra (FCG.NZ) is likely to lower its forecast payout for its farmer shareholders to the vicinity of NZ$4.25 per kilo of milk solids for the 2015-16 season, says ASB rural economist Nathan Penny. Fonterra is currently forecasting NZ$5.25. By the end of the season, however, Penny expects Fonterra's forecast to be NZ$4.50 per kilo of milk solids. He says he expects July's dairy price falls to be temporary and Fonterra's recent move to switch production away from whole milk powder into higher-priced products plus a lower production outlook to together put upward pressure on overall prices in time. He expects a mixed result at the GlobalDairyTrade auction overnight Tuesday, with downside risks. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @FarroHoward) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0119 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities are set to be buffeted by data releases as China's official manufacturing PMI for July falls to 50.0 versus 50.2 in June, disappointing analysts: "We continue to expect supportive policy measures to be rolled out until there are consistent signs of a sustained growth recovery and policy makers getting comfortable with achieving the growth target," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Property data also shows stabilization during July, climbing for a third consecutive month: "The housing market appears to have escaped contagion from the stock market selloff that started in mid-June and turned into a panic in early July," says ING, citing data compiled by the China Index Academy. Private sector PMI reports from Japan, China and Korea are due after Hong Kong opens. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index closed down 2% last week at 24,636.28, while index futures are up 0.1%. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0114 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Indonesia rupiah could hit a new low versus the U.S. dollar today as risk appetite is fragile before manufacturing data due later. The increasingly bullish chart bias for USD/IDR is another reason to keep a negative outlook on the rupiah versus the dollar. Friday's USD/IDR spike to a 17-year high of 13,525 steepened the path of the Bollinger uptrend channel, implying that the pace of rupiah-weakening may accelerate in the near-term. The ongoing rally for the U.S. dollar shows little sign of abating after bearish China manufacturing PMI data released Saturday cast a shadow on risk appetite for emerging markets. China's manufacturing index shrank to 50.0 from the June reading of 50.2 and Indonesia's July manufacturing PMI is due later today at 0300 GMT. USD/IDR is now 13,510 from its Friday close of 13,525. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0113 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The yuan remains stuck within a narrow range centered around 6.2095 and appears unlikely to fluctuate unless the authorities relax their grip on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But in the unregulated offshore market, there are signs that traders are turning bearish on the yuan. The widening of the benchmark price spread between the 1-month and the 1-year USD/CNY nondeliverable forward contract suggests that speculators are expecting the dollar to strengthen ahead. Meanwhile, stocks on the Shanghai composite index still look bearish, as the index is still inside its daily Bollinger downtrend channel that caps at 3,829.65. The negative chart signal hints at an interim bottom at 3,302.80. Investors may be losing confidence in stocks as the government continues to prop up the equity market through direct and indirect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Analysts think another round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy easing--which has a more lasting impact than short-term \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures--may be needed to reinvigorate the stock market. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0105 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/MYR is climbing, implying a weaker Malaysia ringgit versus the U.S. dollar, after spending most of last week firmly capped by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The pair could hit a new 17-year peak if it surpasses Friday's high of 3.8260. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has in recent weeks been suspected of selling U.S. dollars on the spot market - and thereby depleting their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves - to cushion against ringgit weakness. But the authorities may be relaxing their stance as regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have also slid. Southeast Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have fallen to multi-year lows versus the U.S. dollar on expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve is preparing to raise interest rates this year. But the ringgit has suffered the worst, depreciating as much as 8.7% due to domestic political instability causing foreign investor anxiety. USD/MYR is now 3.8250 from its Friday close of 3.8160. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0052 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A downside bias to the USD/JPY could emerge if the pair breaks out of its current tight range between 123.80 (upper end of Ichimoku cloud) and 124.57 (a Fibonacci retracement line), says IG Securities market analyst Junichi Ishikawa in a morning note. While the RSI momentum indicator is still above 50, "we don't get the feeling the dollar will accelerate higher," he says. Even if the pair breaks through the upper end of the range, it's hard to assume the pair will then jump straight above the 125 mark, as the upper half of the 124 range is widely considered a politically sensitive zone in Japan, he says. Instead, investors should be prepared for a possible downside bias on stock market instability that may send the pair to 122.50 (line on Ichimoku chart) or even 122.17 (at lower end of Ichimoku cloud). (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0044 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/SGD is rising again after a pullback Friday sparked by the euro's bounce. The Singapore dollar is now weaker at 1.3734 from its last close of 1.3716 to the U.S. dollar, after China's July manufacturing PMI data released Saturday disappointed. China's manufacturing index slipped to 50.0 from 50.2 the previous month, and was below the street's expectation for a print of 50.1. Risk appetite in Asia is thus diminished, which could keep the safe haven U.S. dollar supported. If USD/SGD ends the day above 1.3716 the Bollinger uptrend channel will remain in effect. This bullish chart formation may motivate buying-on-dips for an immediate target of 1.3800 - which if breached could trigger a spike higher due to stoploss orders being activated to cover short-USD positions. USD/SGD is now 1.3734 from its Friday close of 1.3716. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0042 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW opened lower Monday at 1,165.0 from its Friday close of 1,170.0 due to a euro bounce Friday, but the pair is still biased higher on the daily chart, implying that the South Korea won may weaken again versus the greenback. If USD/KRW ends Monday above 1,166 the Bollinger uptrend channel will remain in effect, keeping bullish pressure on the U.S. dollar. The weaker-than-expected China manufacturing PMI released Saturday may bode poorly for South Korea's own PMI due later at 0140 GMT. If the data accentuates that the MERS-hit economy is still weak, the won may weaken on expectations of further monetary policy easing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  last cut interest rates in June to a record low of 1.5%; the BOK meets on Aug. 13 for its next monetary policy decision. USD/KRW is now 1,167.4 from its Friday close of 1,170.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0038 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/TWD is biased higher as long as it stays within the daily Bollinger uptrend channel that currently supports at 31.530. The target for the USD/TWD is the 32.000 round-figure trading barrier which if breached could cause a short-covering spike. The bullish-USD sentiment can be clearly seen in the offshore market where the price gap between the benchmark 1-month USD/TWD non-deliverable contract and the spot contract has expanded to 0.050 from around par on Friday, implying speculative demand for the greenback is increasing. The safe haven U.S. dollar is likely to stay up due to the weaker-than-forecast China official manufacturing PMI data for July released Saturday- at 50.0 versus market expectations for 50.1 and lower than June's 50.2 - which is weighing on the Australia dollar and adding to a tone of mild risk aversion in Asia. USD/TWD ended Friday at 31.682. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0028 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesian 2014/2015 wheat imports and consumption are set to decrease to 7.6 million metric tons and 7.3 million metric tons respectively, according to the USDA Foreign Agricultural Services Indonesia Grain and Feed Update. It notes the decline is due to Indonesia's economic slowdown and depressed consumer purchasing power. It adds despite the end of Indonesia's wheat flour import quota, the industry reports that the depreciating rupiah has resulted in higher freight rates, discouraging wheat flour imports from Sri Lanka, India or Turkey. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Aug 2015 21:25 ET \par Nikkei Down 0.8%; US Jobs Data Wait Induces -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0011 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY (now at 124.07) needs a clearer signal to push through the so-called "Kuroda ceiling" around 124.60, a senior Japanese bank dealer says, pointing to U.S. labor data out later this week as a possible cue. The dealer's comments follow mixed signals for the pair Friday as sluggish U.S. wage data disappointed investors, while the pair got support from comments by \f28 \uc2 \f2 St. Louis Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \uc2 \f2 James Bullard\f28 \uc2 \f2  that the Fed is "in good shape" to raise rates in September. "We are hoping for another cue" to enable the dollar to break through the Kuroda ceiling, the dealer said, referring to resistance around the mark the USD/JPY was positioned at when BOJ Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda made controversial JPY remarks that pushed down the pair. The USD/JPY touched 124.37 on Friday but again failed to cross the Kuroda ceiling and then nosedived to 123.52 after the release of the U.S. employment-cost index. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 02, 2015 21:25 ET (01:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150803eb830006o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
