\par\par \b \uc2 MW The bull in China's shop has no more room to run\b0\par\par\uc2 By Satyajit Das   \par \uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2015\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Investors in Chinese stocks were mopping up after that market's biggest one-day percentage slide since 2007. Ironically, the same forces that unleashed Chinese investors' animal spirits have brought the bull to its knees.\par \par \uc2 Mounting concern that Chinese authorities could scale back efforts to support stock prices sunk the country's main Shanghai Composite Index ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/what-china-watchers-are-saying-about-shanghais-ugly-selloff-2015-07-27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/what-china-watchers-are-saying-about-shanghais-ugly-selloff-2015-07-27}}}\uc2 ). and the smaller Shenzen Composite and Shenzhen ChiNext indices.\par \par \uc2 But that same government policy to boost stock prices succeeded beyond expectations and fueled a speculative boom. Daily trading turnover quadrupled. At one stage, mroe than 500,000 new trading accounts were being opened weekly. China's stock market capitalization soared, overtaking Japan to become the second-largest stock market in the world.\par \par \uc2 Now China's bull is spent. No one factor is to blame for the sharp correction ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-has-only-itself-to-blame-for-its-stock-market-collapse-2015-07-24"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-has-only-itself-to-blame-for-its-stock-market-collapse-2015-07-24}}}\uc2 ). The market simply ran out of momentum and investors lost confidence. The market had become increasingly driven by debt-fueled liquidity and manic speculation.\par \par \uc2 Valuations had become stretched, with the rise in share prices at odds with slowing economic growth and deterioration in corporate earnings. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/how-to-own-chinese-stocks-without-losing-much-sleep-2015-07-10)New"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/how-to-own-chinese-stocks-without-losing-much-sleep-2015-07-10)New}}}\uc2  initial public offerings were oversubscribed, in some case by 240 times the shares on offer. Purchasing new listings increasingly required investors to sell existing investments, creating selling pressure.\par \par \uc2 The effect of falling prices was amplified by the leverage, in the form of margin loans. At its peak, margin loans reached around $350 billion, around 12%-14% of the size of the stock market. In comparison, the level of margin loans in the U.S. is around 5%-6% and 1% in Japan. Falling prices triggered margin calls. There was forced liquidation of positions as investors needed to raise cash or could not meet demands for additional collateral.\par \par \uc2 As the market fell with increasing rapidity and price changes became disorderly, Chinese authorities responded with a mixture of communist propaganda and borrowed capitalist tricks.\par \par \uc2 Chinese media blamed short-sellers and market manipulators. Patriotic calls sought to discourage investors betting on price falls. Chinese police instigated ritual investigations into short-selling to scare even legitimate sellers out of positions.\par \par \uc2 Following the emergency plunge-protection guidelines patented by the U.S. authorities, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped money into the financial system. Interest rates were cut. The reserve ratio and loan to deposit limits were altered to allow banks to increase lending. Margin finance rules were relaxed allowing anything from real estate to antiques to be used as collateral for loans.\par \par \uc2 Given the limited effect of these measures, the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  more directly in the market. The government-controlled \uc2 Securities Association of China\uc2  arranged for the 21 big brokerage firms to establish a fund worth around $20 billion, to buy shares in large companies.\par \par \uc2 In addition, China's securities regulator barred major shareholders (with stakes exceeding 5%), corporate executives, and directors from selling their shares for six months. State-owned enterprises and investment vehicles were instructed not to sell shares. There were suggestions that some SOEs would buy back their own shares to support prices. New listings were deferred. With currently planned share offerings topping $600 billion, the authorities sought to limit the claims on available investor funds.\par \par \uc2 The government encouraged companies to apply for trading halts. This resulted in suspension of trading in around 1,400 companies listed on Chinese exchanges, representing over $$2.5 trillion worth of shares or 40% of the stock market's capitalization.\par \par \uc2 Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets further has constraints. Expansion of credit runs the risk of adding to inflationary pressures and further complicates the task of dealing with a large pre-existing credit bubble. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might push up the value of the yuan, ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-and-the-dollar-may-be-about-to-collide-2015-07-27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/china-and-the-dollar-may-be-about-to-collide-2015-07-27}}}\uc2 ) making China's embattled exporters even less competitive.\par \par \uc2 Still, given the centralized political and economic command and control in China, it is unwise to assume that the authorities cannot prop up share markets. Large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves ($4 trillion) and the ability to use state-controlled banks to expand their balance sheets provides the government with significant resources to purchase shares.\par \par \uc2 In this uncertain time, Chinese authorities are discovering an old capitalist truth -- bubbles are hard to see, and even harder to catch.\par \par \uc2 Satyajit Das is a former banker and the author of the forthcoming "A Banquet of Consequences'"(in Australia and Europe); "Age of Stagnation" (North America and Asia). This article is part two of a three-part series.\par \par \uc2 -Satyajit Das; 415-439-6400; AskNewswires@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 27, 2015 16:10 ET (20:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150727eb7r002x1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
