\par\par \b \uc2 To Predict Earnings Surprises, Look at Company's Margins: \uc2 Barclays\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2015\par 06:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts looked at companies that had disappointing earnings and one theme emerged: they had declines in their end-product pricing. "In those sectors where there hasn't been pricing discipline, where the end-product pricing has come down, margin erosion has had a much more significant impact compared to stock market movements," said Paul Louis, Asia ex-Japan real estate research analyst at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . Sectors such as telecoms services are performing better than, for example, capital goods equipment companies, because they haven't dropped their prices as much, he says. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @anjiezheng) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Marine Shipper \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) is higher after a weekend Nikkei business daily report saying that the firm's 1Q recurring profit will come in around Y20 billion--well above the Y10.7 billion street consensus. Probable drivers include strong showings in the tanker and car carrier businesses, yen depreciation, and a decline in fuel costs--which should also be a positive readthrough for the other two marine transport companies--\uc2 Mitsui OSK Lines\uc2  (9104.TO) and \uc2 Kawasaki Kisen\uc2  (9107.TO)--says JPMorgan Securities analyst Dan Lu. \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  reports for 1Q this Friday; there is a strong possibility of profits topping the market's low expectations, he adds. \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  is now up 1.5% at Y344. \uc2 Mitsui OSK\uc2  and \uc2 Kawasaki Kisen\uc2  are both down. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0433 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Fears that China's stock market slump could derail Beijing's plans to cure the ills of its debt-encumbered corporate sector are unwarranted, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In fact, it was never a viable strategy to begin with. The equity market rally "never really was able to help this deleveraging process," says Jian Chang, China economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The only really meaningful way is the government has to tolerate an even lower pace of growth," she says. Back to the drawing board, then. The Shanghai Composite falls 2.4% on Monday, putting its gain at 22.8% year-to-date, even after having lost 23% from highs reached in June. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0413 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities sink in the morning session, with a gauge of industrial goods stocks losing the most among industry groups after Chinese industry profits declined 0.3% during June, reversing earlier signs of stabilization. Though economic data released last week pointed to a softening Chinese economy, "the bigger problem may be a lackluster earnings season," says BlackRock. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index falls 2.8% to 24,422.19, while H-shares tumble faster than a corresponding 2.4% drop in the Shanghai Composite; the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index slumps 3.6% to 11,257.15. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Resources Land\f28 \uc2 \f2  (1109.HK) falls most, down 6% at HK$21.75, while China Overseas Land & Investment (0688.HK) is off 5.9% at HK$24.90. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0401 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hiroshi Nakaso\f28 \uc2 \f2  was greeted with the usual skepticism as soon as he laid out an upbeat price projection. His latest speech in southern Japan boils down to this: inflation will reach 2% roughly by September 2016 on mild recovery in global oil prices, growing wages and likely widening in Japan's positive output gap. But first of all, oil prices have resumed falls and their outlook is murky. Secondly, the pace of wage growth has disappointed many BOJ officials, though they haven't said so publicly, people close to the BOJ say. Further, "the BOJ is basically the only one saying that Japan's negative output gap has closed," with the government and many economists suspecting that slack remains in the economy, says Norinchukin Research Institute chief economist Takeshi Minami.(takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0353 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese shares weakened, as new inflows into the market slows, say analysts. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 2% at 3990.33, with over half of the firms in the red. The Shenzhen Composite Index shed 0.7% at 2305.31 and the ChiNext board lost 1.3% at 2860.57. As of July 27, of the 1,812 firms which have disclosed half-year results, 62% forecasted gains in the second half, giving the market a boost of confidence in the short-term, said Qian Qimin, analyst at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Government-backed funds remain a pillar supporting recovery; a total of 45 firms have received funds from China Securities Finance Corp as of July 24, according to Wind Information. The daily turnover on the A-share market hovered around 1.2 trillion yuan in recent trading days, compared with over 2 trillion yuan before the market slump, according to Qian from Shenyin Wanguo Securities. "It means that investors are still uncertain about the impact of any potential policy changes," added Qian. (Yifan.xie@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0313 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei is down 0.7% at 20394.41 midday extending its early losses, now on pace for its 3rd loss in the last 4 sessions. All other major Asian bourses are also lower. "There is still plenty of regional sensitivity to negative equity sentiment--especially regarding Chinese equities and their volatility," says an equity trading director at a European brokerage. "Worries that corporate earnings may disappoint just adds to the case for taking money off the table for a lot of investors." Recently bid up drug makers are leading the market lower, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shionogi & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (4507.TO) down 2.3% at Y5,040 and Eisai (4523.TO) down 3.5% at Y8,138. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0310 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A federal judge last week dismissed a lawsuit against \f28 \uc2 \f2 AXA\f28 \uc2 \f2 's U.S. unit involving controversial "captive" insurance. Captives are affiliated entities that hold some claims and aren't subject to the same strict funding requirements that insurers are when holding obligations directly. The suit was brought by policy holders who alleged \f28 \uc2 \f2 AXA\f28 \uc2 \f2  made "materially misleading representations as to its financial condition" by failing to disclose transaction details. The judge said "the pervasiveness of shadow insurance in New York... may well pose a threat to the stability and reliability of the state's insurance system" but plaintiffs didn't establish injury. \f28 \uc2 \f2 AXA\f28 \uc2 \f2  said these are "perfectly legal transactions that [New York Department of Financial Services] vetted and approved." (leslie.scism@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0256 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY weakens, briefly touching 123.49, its lowest since July 15, as stock market weakness prompts investors' flight into the perceived safety of the JPY, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Kenji Yoshii. "The yen is being bought as stocks remain weak," he says. The Nikkei is down 0.7% midday. Investors are already taking a wait-and-see stance ahead of key events such as the FOMC and the release of U.S. GDP data later this week, he says. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The bullish USD/INR technical chart suggests that more U.S. dollar strength - thereby rupee weakness - is likely in the near term. The pair is above the Ichimoku Cloud consolidation zone - which has now turned into a support level at 63.87 - and is also inside the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel that points to an interim ceiling of 64.56. But rupee weakness may be mitigated by the continued decline of crude oil prices. U.S. oil production remains near multi-decade highs, even as drillers fret over the impending influx of Iranian oil into the global supply chain. Cheaper imported fuel costs will keep India's current account deficit more manageable, which would thus improve the economic fundamentals that might encourage more foreign investors to place their bets on India. USD/INR is expected to open at 64.07 from its Friday close of 64.10. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0247 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair stays above its key support at 0.7040 and remains on the upside. The intraday relative strength index lacks downward momentum. Thus, even though a continuation of the consolidation cannot be ruled out, its extent should be limited. A first upside target is set at July 23 top at 0.7100. A break above this resistance would open the way to further bounce towards 0.7130 and 0.7150 in extension. Only a break below the key support at 0.7040 would call for further consolidation towards 0.7010 at first and towards July 23 bottom at 0.6985 in extension. This content was produced exclusively for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  by Trading Central (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ), a leading investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) () \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China is very likely to further widen the yuan's trading band from currently 2% to 3% in two months' time, say CICC analysts. The estimate came after China's State Council last week said it would further expand the trading band in a policy to support the nation's exports and imports sectors. The move to give the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more leeway to trade will help Beijing's efforts to lobby the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  to include the yuan in its basket of global reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a scheduled review late this year, CICC analysts say. After the yuan's trading band widening, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may weaken against U.S. dollar in a short run, the analysts predict. But the move could help \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gradually exit from market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , they add. (grace.zhu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 27, 2015 00:43 ET (04:43 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150727eb7r0008p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
