\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2  Seeks More Overhauls as China Pursues Reserve Status for Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley   \par \uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2015\par 01:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON--The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said it wants more financial-system overhauls from China as Beijing pushes for the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to label the yuan a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The comments Thursday appear to mirror the U.S. position on China's bid to have the yuan included in the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Washington has hedged its support, trying to leverage Beijing's endeavor to encourage a stronger revamp of the financial sector. Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  has said the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is likely to award the yuan reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status at some point, but left the timing open.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff have been gathering data, studying China's markets and talking with investors and government officials as the fund prepares an official recommendation for the executive board to consider.\par \par \uc2 "An important part of those discussions are financial market reforms in China," IMF spokesman \uc2 Gerry Rice\uc2  said in a regular press briefing. "We would be looking to progress being made in that area as we head down this path towards the end of the year."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  and other senior administration officials have said over the last several months that Beijing's reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort is fortuitous timing. The U.S. views the bid as incentive for China's government to move forward with promised policies that will help liberalize its financial markets.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , has outlined Beijing's plans to move forward with financial-market liberalization. But recent turmoil in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's markets, questions about Beijing's market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a deceleration in economic growth are fueling doubts about the government's commitment to deliver on policy overhauls.\par \par \uc2 Although the technical study prepared by \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff will certainly matter--such as whether there is sufficient unhindered trade in the yuan globally, for example--many economists say the decision will ultimately be a political one made by the fund's largest shareholders. Several U.S. allies have already voiced support for yuan reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status.\par \par \uc2 But while the U.S. could be overruled if a majority of the shareholders back Beijing's bid, the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's latest comments may indicate the fund sees some wisdom in Washington's strategy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2 's board will hold an informal discussion on the matter at the end of the month, but an official decision isn't expected until at least until October or November. Fund officials have signaled, however, that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  could postpone its judgment until the new year, giving the world more time to assess China commitment to market overhauls.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ian Talley at ian.talley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 23-07-15 2341GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150723eb7n000kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
