\par\par \b \uc2 Behind China's Dwindling  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserves, A Plan to Boost the Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2015\par 02:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 If changes in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are any indication, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last quarter. The goal: to bolster the yuan's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  on Tuesday reported $3.69 trillion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings as of the end of the second quarter, down $40 billion from the previous three months. The drop, which followed a record $113 billion plunge in the reserves in the first quarter, is the least in a year, indicating that the amount of capital fleeing China's shores has eased.\par \par \uc2 But that didn't come without cost. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sold large amounts of its dollar holdings to prevent the yuan from sliding further against the U.S. dollar, analysts say. A relatively stable yuan -- also known as renminbi, or People's \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- in turn has reduced the desire among businesses and investors to take money out of the country.\par \par \uc2 Julian Evans-Pritchard, chief economist at Capital Economics, estimates that \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a combined $13.7 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings in April and May to prevent the yuan from falling against the greenback. The latest data suggest the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  "increased the pace of sale in June, most likely in order to provide some support to the renminbi," Mr. Evans-Prichard wrote in a report issued Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 PBOC\uc2  officials declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Evans-Prichard noted, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has "diminished dramatically" since mid-2014, which he said supports the view that the yuan is now trading close to its "true market value."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  traditionally has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by purchasing the dollar from its exporters and simultaneously selling its yuan holdings -- a move many Western officials have said has helped keep the yuan's exchange rate artificially low, thereby benefiting Chinese exporters in foreign markets. (A cheaper yuan makes Chinese goods more competitive overseas.)\par \par \uc2 But in recent months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has mostly involved the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchasing the yuan while selling its dollar holdings, as the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  intends to prevent too much capital from flowing out of the country because of a falling yuan.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, by keeping the yuan largely stable, China's leadership also has its eyes abroad: it is trying to get the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to declare the yuan a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later this year, like the dollar, the euro and the yen. To further that goal, China announced late Tuesday that foreign central banks and sovereign-wealth funds will have open access to the country's $6.1 trillion bond market.\par \par \uc2 Too big of a depreciation in the yuan's value could hurt Beijing's chances of winning the status as that could incur more wrath from Washington and elsewhere and lead foreign institutions and investors to balk at holding a declining Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the same time, however, some economists within China have been calling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to devalue the yuan to help bolster the country's flagging growth. In an analysis released in May, Zhang Ming, a senior economist at the government think tank \uc2 Chinese Academy of Social Sciences\uc2 , said the yuan's strength has "seriously deviated" from China's economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "A devaluation by a proper amount," Mr. Zhang argued, would help revive China's exports and growth. China on Wednesday reported that its GDP rose 7% in the second quarter, unchanged from the first quarter and beating economists' estimates of 6.8%. The better-than-expected growth data came even though exports, investment and manufacturing activities remained anemic.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 PBOC\uc2  officials have said it's not necessary to resuscitate growth by devaluing the yuan. They point to China's trade surplus, which has narrowed in recent years but remains relatively big, indicating Chinese exports are still strong.\par \par \uc2 According to data from the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 , the yuan has gained more than 2% against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the end of last year. It's almost flat against the dollar so far this year, while most of its Asian peers lost have lost ground to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Tuesday following its latest quarterly monetary-policy meeting, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeated its pledge to keep the yuan's exchange rate "relatively stable" in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 --Lingling Wei. Follow her on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @Lingling_Wei.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-07-15 0033GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150716eb7g0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
