\par\par \b \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  Wages Internal Debate on China Crash Impact -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2015\par 06:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Whether the recent crash in Chinese stocks could ripple through the real economy is a contentious issue on which even analysts within the same bank always agree. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's chief regional economist, Dong Tao, warns about impact on consumption from the sharp market plunge as stocks now make up 30% of liquid assets of Chinese urban households. "The wealth effect is clear," he says. "The longer the stock stays low, the more likely consumer confidence will be affected." But the bank's head of China research, Vincent Chan, argues otherwise, saying that consumption will not be affected by the crash, apart from hurting sentiment. It says consumption hasn't really surged during the market rally and stocks still account for only a small portion of net household wealth. "[The] labor market is still healthy with strong wage growth, impact on consumption should not last for too long," it says. (alice.kantor@wsj.com; Twitter: @alicekantor) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Besides reaffirming the wild-west nature of investing in China, the recent market collapse is likely to deal its banks and brokers a big hit, and have an extended effect on the credit distribution in the country, says \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 . Brokers and banks, already the largest providers of margin financing, have been tasked with shoring up the market. This means they will bear the brunt of selling pressure if there is another big panic, says BAML's equity strategist David Cui. Some banks already have a raft of nonperforming loans on their books. "If banks have another big source of losses, they will be more cautious in extending loans and granting mortgages in the future," he adds. The economy is already highly leveraged. So a shock like the market rout of the past few weeks has a high possibility of destabilizing the entire system, says Cui. "The chance of a financial crisis has risen quite a deal, and the timeline has been brought forward." (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0400 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China bonds and the yuan, in both onshore and offshore markets, have gathered strength alongside the sharp rebound in stocks. In the morning session Friday, the offshore yuan strengthens against the dollar. It currently trades at 6.2143 per dollar, down from nearly 6.22 late yesterday. Onshore yuan is almost muted at 6.2 given a flat fixing. That reflects overseas investor sentiment has improved somewhat, though offshore yuan is still weaker than onshore. In the domestic market, bond yields grind lower. The benchmark 10-year government bond yield falls 0.06 percentage points to 3.4242. One-year government bond is even stronger, with yield down 0.18 percentage points to 2.3247%. Bond prices move inversely to yields. In the offshore market, bonds of both high-quality issuers and risky high-yield borrowers have sharply recovered from the heavy selloff earlier this week. "It was purely panic selloff two days ago," says a fixed income fund manager in Hong Kong. (Fiona.Law@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0339 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Vietnam has approved Thailand's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (SCB.TH) takeover of struggling Vinasiam Bank, the government said. State-run Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development has a 34% stake in Vinasiam Bank, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charoen Pokphand Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  each owns 33%. The government said the sale of Vinasiam Bank is in line with its efforts to strengthen the local banking system. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has taken over three banks so far this year. (trong-khanh.vu@wsj.com; Twitter: @TrongKhanhVu) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0337 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's industrial production index likely rose 3.0% in May, according to the median forecast of 15 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal, as sluggish global demand and weak commodities prices weighed on the country's manufacturing and mining sectors. This compares with April's 4.0% year-over-year gain. The official data are due 0400 GMT. "Manufacturing activities appears to be soft across the region as overall external demand remains weak," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Malaysia's exports contracted for the second consecutive month in May, down 6.7% year-on-year. Shipments of electronic products, which make up about a third of total exports, slipped 0.6% in May while exports of liquefied natural gas fell 48%. "Low global energy prices continue to drag on mining production, while the introduction of a goods and services tax last month is dampening manufacturing," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (jason.ng@wsj.com; Twitter: @ByJasonNg) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0322 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's equity-market collapse won't hurt consumption much, Capital Economics said. "Even at the peak of the bubble, only 50 million investors, roughly one in 30 Chinese people, owned A-shares," analyst Julian Evans-Pritchard said in a note, comparing it to the one-in-seven rate in the U.S. "Given that the stock market didn't provide any noticeable boost to spending on the way up, there is no reason to expect it to be a drag on the way down." Chinese stock investors also tend to have more disposable income, as over 80% have account balances exceeding 10,000 yuan ($1,610), which is more than half of China's median annual salary. "Low-income households, which would be most likely to cut back on consumption following a fall in wealth, should have been largely unaffected by the equity slump," Capital Economics said. (chuin-wei.yap@wsj.com; Twitter @YapCW) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's June exports likely rose 0.5% from a year earlier, which is still weak but shows signs of improvement thanks to possible recovering demand from advanced economies, according to a median forecast of 14 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. Imports in June likely fell 16.0% from a year earlier, compared with a 17.6% year-over-year drop in May, according to the polled economists. "We expect a gradual recovery in external demand and stabilization of key commodity prices to help improve China's trade growth in the second half of the year," say \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists in a research note. China's trade surplus likely stood at $55.3 billion in June, narrowing from $59.49 billion in May, the polled economists say. (grace.zhu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ELM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0307 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei is +0.4% at 19937 midday, recovering from a modestly negative start. "Thanks to a rebound in China shares--where the composite Shanghai SSE index is up 5.7%--Japan stocks are seeing a decent amount of buying support, but the selloff in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9983.TO) is a big drag due to its huge weight in the Nikkei," says an equity trading director at a foreign brokerage. "In terms of short-term volatility, China continues to exert strong influence, but Sunday will tell more about the mid-term picture, especially if Greece ends up leaving the eurozone. Meanwhile, U.S. earnings reports will influence Japan individual stock and sector movements." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2  remains down 6.1% at 53,930 yen. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0306 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The price gap between different quality iron ore narrowed as the closely followed 62% price plummeted to decade lows, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  doesn't expect that to last for too long. In recent weeks, the spread between the 62% and 58% ores has narrowed to 13%, which reflects the average in 2012\u8209\'20\'112013 when prices were considerably higher and compares to a record 33% last July, says analyst Vivek Dhar. "Despite the recent convergence of the two benchmarks, we expect the spread to remain wide as low iron-ore prices encourage steel mills in China towards higher-quality iron ore," Dhar says. Ore with 62% iron content rose 9.5% Thursday to US$48.30/ton, according to The Steel Index. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0305 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Lieutenant General Hernando Iriberri was appointed Philippine military chief on Friday, becoming the sixth commander to serve during the five-year-old presidency of Benigno Aquino. The Philippine military is implementing a long-overdue modernization program as it grapples with territorial disputes with China, but a rule forcing soldiers to retire at 56 is disrupting those efforts, Philippine defense analysts have long complained, as it necessitates constant reshuffles of the top brass. The outgoing commander, General Gregorio Pio Catapang, held the top post for just under a year. Iriberri himself hits retirement age in May. Aquino had pledged to introduce fixed three-year terms of armed forces chiefs, irrespective of age, but his failure to deliver on that means the Philippine military must continue to live without leadership continuity as it attempts to transform itself into a viable outfit. (trefor.moss@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0304 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Australia dollar has risen 0.7% versus the U.S. dollar as stock markets in China rebound a second day, sparking demand for the risk-sensitive commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The daily AUD/USD chart still shows a bearish bias but this could be nullified if the pair closes Friday above 0.7520 and thus out of the Bollinger downtrend channel. Confirmation that the downtrend channel has been negated could motivate short-covering that might propel AUD/USD toward the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance line at 0.7641 in the days ahead. The Shanghai composite stock index appears to have finally gained some traction, rising 6.1% Friday, after a slew of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures announced by the authorities. The latest move by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government was to ban selling by major shareholders, and to investigate "malicious" short-selling. AUD/USD is now 0.7488 from its Thursday close of 0.7442. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Jul 2015 00:17 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wages Internal Debate on China -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0301 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] One might argue that the sharp drop in Australian home-loan approvals in May removes a major hurdle to a further interest rate cut in Australia. The 6.1% seasonally-adjusted fall from April is eye-popping, so too the 3.2% drop in investor lending over the same period. Combined, they suggest that recently applied clamps on bank lending to property investors are working and heat is coming out of the housing market. But the question of whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is feeling the need to cut rates again is also worth asking after news Thursday of lower-than-expected unemployment in June. It's likely the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  is noting a solid improvement in the job market. Certainly the RBA's forecast of a cyclical peak in unemployment of 6.5% looks set to be missed by a big margin. NAB economist Tapas Strickland said the RBA looks to be "comfortably on hold" until the end of the year. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter: @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 10, 2015 00:17 ET (04:17 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150710eb7a000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
