\par\par \b \uc2 *U.S. Treasury Bonds Rise On Fed Minutes, China Stock Rout\b0\par\par\uc2 1,826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2015\par 21:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8 Jul 2015 15:31 ET *U.S. Treasury Bonds Strengthen For 4th Consecutive Day\par \par \uc2 8 Jul 2015 15:31 ET *U.S. 10-Year Yield At 2.205%, Lowest Closing Since June 1\par \par \uc2 8 Jul 2015 15:32 ET *U.S. 2-Year Yield At 0.545%, Lowest Closing Since May 15\par \par \uc2 8 Jul 2015 15:48 ET  \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Bonds Strengthen for Fourth Consecutive Day\par \par \uc2 By Min Zeng\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds strengthened Wednesday for a fourth consecutive session as the minutes from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's June policy meeting signaled continued caution in shifting into higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 A deepening rout in China's stock markets heightened anxiety over the potential knock-on effect on the global growth outlook, adding to demand for ultrasafe \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 The Fed\uc2  appears to be extremely cautious in their liftoff of rates," and with the risk from China rising, this is a "favorable environment" for bonds, said Gary Pollack, who helps oversee $12 billion as head of fixed-income trading in New York at \uc2 Deutsche Bank AG\uc2 's private wealth management unit.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pollack said he has reduced holdings of corporate bonds this week and moved cash into longer-dated Treasury debt and municipal bonds.\par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trading, the yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 2.205%, compared with 2.231% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 It marks the yield's lowest closing level since June 1. Bond yields fall as prices rise.\par \par \uc2 U.S. bond yields have dropped in July after the biggest rise in two years during the second quarter. Greece's debt crisis and a sharp pullback in China's stock markets after a recent strong bull run have driven investors to cut risks and preserve capital in haven bond markets. The twin factors are complicating the Fed's plan to raise short-term rates for the first time since 2006.\par \par \uc2 In a sign that investors dialed back risk, U.S. stocks sold off Wednesday and U.S. crude-oil prices settled at the lowest level in near three months. A $21 billion sale of 10-year Treasury notes drew solid demand from both domestic and foreign investors. Buyers accepted a yield of 2.225%, lower than what dealers had expected.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  minutes showed Wednesday that most \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials wanted more information before raising rates, with several mentioning uncertainties about Greece and China.\par \par \uc2 Investors say the global uncertainties mean the Fed will continue to monitor data. Should external factors derail the U.S. economic outlook, the Fed could delay the timing of its tightening campaign.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on Tuesday reiterated its call for the Fed to delay raising interest rates until 2016. Higher interest rates by the Fed would shrink the value of outstanding bonds.\par \par \uc2 Fed-funds futures, used by investors and traders to place bets on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, showed that investors and traders see a 7% likelihood of a rate increase at the September 2015 meeting following the Fed minutes, according to data from the \uc2 CME Group\uc2 . The odds were 9% right before the release.\par \par \uc2 The odds for a rate increase at the December 2015 meeting were 37% compared with 39% before the minutes' release.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the two-year Treasury note, among the most sensitive to changes in the Fed's rate policy outlook, was 0.545% Wednesday, compared with 0.573% Tuesday. It marks the yield's lowest closing level since May 15.\par \par \uc2 Mike Buchanan, deputy chief investment officer at \uc2 Western Asset Management Co\uc2 ., which has $454.8 billion in assets under management, said he doesn't think the Fed "will respond to China's stock market volatility."\par \par \uc2 The key point, Mr. Buchanan says, is whether the turmoil in China would spill over into China's economy and undercut the U.S. growth outlook. Given the uncertainties, he expects the 10-year Treasury yield to trade in a broad range between 2% and 2.75% on the balance of this year.\par \par \uc2 A weekslong selloff in China's stock markets escalated even as policy makers in the world's second-largest economy have taken measures aiming to boost confidence. Shares listed on the country's main Shanghai market dropped 5.9% Wednesday, deepening a slump that has seen it fall by nearly a third since mid-June.\par \par \uc2 The gloom in China's stock markets has started to spread to other markets. Many commodities -- crude oil, copper and iron ore -- have sold off in July, reflecting concerns over soft demand driven by a slowing Chinese economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in commodities-exporting countries have lost ground against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The bond market's price strength was contained Wednesday because of signs of progress in Greece's debt talks, though many investors remain cautious and await a clear outcome.\par \par \uc2 The cash-strapped country formally requested a three-year bailout from the eurozone's rescue fund Wednesday and pledged to start implementing some of the reforms demanded by its creditors. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    COUPON  ISSUE   PRICE      CHANGE   YIELD     CHANGE \par  \par    5/8%    2-year  100 5/32   up 2/32  0.545%    -3.2BP \par    1%      3-year  99 28/32   up 3/32  0.912%    -3.7BP \par    1 5/8%  5-year  100 20/32  up 4/32  1.493%    -2.9BP \par    2 1/8%  7-year  101 11/32  up 7/32  1.916%    -3.2BP \par    2 1/8%  10-year 99 9/32    up 7/32  2.205%    -2.6BP \par    2 1/2%  30-year 100 10/32  up 19/32 2.984%    -3.0BP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2-10-Yr Yield Spread: +166.0BPS +165.8BPS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Tradeweb/WSJ Market Data Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Min Zeng at min.zeng@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Jul 2015 15:40 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bonds Sell Off as Fears Dial Back\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Min Zeng\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors flocked out of ultrasafe \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds on Thursday as worries over China and Greece dialed back, pushing bond prices lower for the first time in five days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A $13 billion sale of 30-year Treasury bonds drew tepid demand, adding to the selloff.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "An air of calm has come to the fore," said Ian Lyngen, a government-bond strategist at CRT Capital Group LLC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late-afternoon trading, the yield on the benchmark 10-year note was 2.301%, compared with Wednesday's one-month low of 2.205%. Yields rise as prices fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese stocks posted the biggest one-day rally in six years Thursday and stalled the recent rout. The rebound offered some hope that the market could find its footing after fresh actions by Chinese policy makers to instill confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, investors welcomed some signs of progress in Greece's debt talks with international creditors. Greece has formally requested a three-year bailout and leaders of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  will gather this weekend to decide whether the terms are sufficient for fresh funding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts and money managers caution any setback in China and Greece, the twin focus for global investors this week, could jolt investors' sentiment again and boost demand for \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volatility in financial markets has been rising amid concerns over stock and bond valuations as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  is prepared to raise benchmark interest rates for the first time since 2006.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kathryn Koch, portfolio manager at Goldman Sachs Asset Management, which has approximately $1.024 trillion in assets under supervision, said she expects markets to remain "volatile" in the short term. She has pared an overweight position on European stocks given risks on Greece, and holds an "underweight" position on Chinese stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is "incredibly difficult" to draw a conclusion on the outlook for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's equity markets" given the level of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," she said, adding that the recent bull run in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stocks was disconnected with a slowing economy in China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's market turmoil and Greece's debt crisis are muddling the global economic outlook. Worries that the stock market rout could undermine an already slowing China's economy had rattled commodities including copper, iron ore and crude oil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Softer commodities have lowered investors' expectations for inflation in the U.S., which is complicating the Fed's plan in raising interest rates. Many inflation indicators in the U.S. have been staying below 2%, a level Fed officials consider as desirable for price stability. Tame inflation has been one of the main factors making the Fed cautious in shifting into tighter monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The minutes of the Fed's June policy meeting released Wednesday showed most Fed officials wanted more information before raising rates, with several mentioning uncertainties about Greece and China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Money managers say it takes time to draw a conclusion on potential fallout from China and Greece, and that the developments so far haven't derailed the Fed's plan toward higher interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors will likely continue agonizing over China's rate of economic growth, [but] it's not likely to have a long-term impact on global growth unless we see a material decline in their growth rate," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 James Sarni\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior managing partner at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Payden & Rygel\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Los Angeles, which manages $95 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Sarni said he expects Treasury bond yields to rise in coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns about the Fed had driven investors to shed bondholdings in recent months as they are concerned a shift in policy could shrink the value of outstanding bonds. The 10-year Treasury note's yield climbed to 2.5% at one point in June, the highest level since September, from below 2% in April.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  has signaled that the rate-tightening campaign will be gradual and slow, bolstering expectations that it would keep a lid on the rise in bond yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new 30-year Treasury bonds were sold at a yield of 3.084%, higher than 3.07% expected by dealers and signaling weaker-than-expected demand. The bright side came from 51.1% indirect bidding, a gauge of foreign demand, which was higher compared with the average of 49.7% for the previous eight auctions. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COUPON  ISSUE   PRICE      CHANGE   YIELD     CHANGE \par  \par 5/8%    2-year 100 2/32    dn 2/32  0.585%     +4.0BP \par  \par 1%      3-year 99 24/32    dn 5/32  0.962%     +5.3BP \par  \par 1 5/8%  5-year 100 8/32    dn 12/32 1.573%     +8.2BP \par  \par 2 1/8%  7-year 100 24/32   dn 19/32 2.009%     +9.3BP \par  \par 2 1/8%  10-year 98 15/32   dn 27/32 2.301%     +9.6BP \par  \par 2 1/2%  30-year 98 2/32    dn 2 7/32 3.098%    +11.5BP \par  \par 2-10-Yr Yield Spread: +171.6BPS +166.0BPS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Tradeweb/WSJ Market Data Group\f28 \par \par