\par\par \b \uc2 Beijing's Effort to Halt Market Decline Yields Mixed Results\b0\par\par\uc2 1,299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2015\par 03:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks in Hong Kong tumbled, while China's stabilized, as Beijing takes unprecedented steps to stem a three-week selloff and Greece voted to reject its bailout terms.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index shed 3.2% Monday, its worst one-day performance since 2012. The index is now down 11.3% since its April high, entering correction territory, defined as a drop of more than 10%. A gauge of Hong Kong-listed Chinese companies, known as H-shares, is down 3%. To date, Hong Kong had largely avoided the roller-coaster trading that wiped out about $2.4 trillion in value from Chinese shares during a three-week decline.\par \par \uc2 "The sentiment is really bad amid concerns over not only the problem in Greece but also the problems in the mainland China stock market," said Dickie Wong, executive director of research at Hong Kong-based brokerage Kingston Securities.\par \par \uc2 Potential for volatility sparked by Greece's vote and recent slides in mainland-listed shares put Hong Kong's securities regulator on standby earlier Monday. "Given the 'No' vote in the Greek referendum and the latest developments in the mainland A-shares market, there may be increased volatility in the Hong Kong markets," said \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a statement. "The banking system in Hong Kong is highly liquid and is well equipped to handle any such volatility. The HKMA stands ready to provide liquidity support to the banking system should it become necessary to do so."\par \par \uc2 China's indexes also cooled after initial spikes earlier Monday. The Shanghai Composite ended up 2.4% compared with earlier gains of as much as 7.8%. The smaller Shenzhen market is down 2.7% and the ChiNext board, composed of small-cap stocks, is down 4.3%. All indexes still are off more than a quarter from highs reached in June.\par \par \uc2 Despite Monday's recovery, nervous investors still whisked the most capital out of China via the Stock Connect than on any day since the trading link between Hong Kong and Shanghai launched in November. A total of 13.4 billion yuan ($2.16 billion) was withdrawn over the course of the trading day. Chinese investors also withdrew a net 1.45 billion yuan from Hong Kong-listed stocks.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have turned to an array of tools to prop up the market in recent days: from encouraging stock buying with borrowed money to rallying state-affiliated firms to invest. But some say the measures won't have much staying power.\par \par \uc2 "These policies are aimed at stabilizing market confidence for the short-term, but still fall short of expectation to push the market to a higher level," said Jacky Zhang, an analyst at BOC International. "Investors may remain cautious about long-term investment."\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, China's big state-controlled securities firms, mutual funds and a unit of China's giant sovereign-wealth fund committed to buying shares. The Securities Association said that 21 brokerages pledged to try to increase investments in the stock market through a 120 billion yuan market-stabilization fund as long as the Shanghai Composite Index stays below 4500.\par \par \uc2 That measure could have prodded Chinese brokerages on Monday to reallocate liquidity toward the fund, selling off Hong Kong-listed shares and buying up mainland ones instead, said Kay Van-Petersen, Asia macro strategist at Saxo Capital Markets. "It's the most liquid names that are getting hammered." One of China's largest brokerages, \uc2 Haitong Securities Co\uc2 ., traded 6.7% higher in Shanghai but tumbled 15.2% in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Investors appear to be taking stock of Beijing's attempts\u8212\'20\'14some previously untested\u8212\'20\'14over the past several days to restore investor confidence. After the weekend, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indirectly will help investors borrow to buy shares and regulators agreed to halt all new initial public offerings.\par \par \uc2 Late Sunday, the top securities regulator said the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  would "provide liquidity assistance" to China Securities Finance Corp., a company owned by the stock regulator. The company will use the money to lend to brokerages, which could then make loans to investors to buy stocks. It marks the first time \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds will be directed to institutions other than banks.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of [the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] is unprecedented," said Li Bin, a Shanghai-based analyst at \uc2 Capital Securities\uc2 , and shows "the government is highly concerned about potential market stampede caused by margin calls." While margin trading can amplify returns on the upswing, losses can pile up quickly when investors are forced to sell holdings to pay back brokerages from whom they have borrowed.\par \par \uc2 Other brokerages are more hopeful. Regulators have plenty of options at hand to stabilize the Chinese market, and the unwinding of margin positions could encourage more risk taking in the future, said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Regulators are "committed" to preventing further falls in mainland A-shares, and "more favorable policies are expected to be rolled out to stabilize the market if volatility remains high," analysts from the bank wrote in a research report. "We estimate that the worst of deleveraging and forced selling in the A-share market could be behind us."\par \par \uc2 Moreover, a big source of liquidity hasn't yet been tapped for stock investments, said analysts at Bernstein Research. Liquidity from wealth-management products and money-market funds, rather than bank deposits, drove much of the earlier rally. "This is good news for the broader market, as the equity market rally has yet to tap into the largest liquidity pool in the system, i.e. bank deposits, so future liquidity supply is not yet a constraint," they say.\par \par \uc2 Investors elsewhere in Asia also weathered bumpy trading. In Malaysia, political pressures have helped push the ringgit to its weakest level against the U.S. dollar since September 1998. The attorney general said an official investigation into a troubled state investment fund has uncovered documents related to allegations that money was transferred into the personal bank accounts of Prime Minister \uc2 Najib Razak\uc2 . The Wall Street Journal reported on Friday that Malaysian government investigators looking into the activities of \uc2 1Malaysia Development Bhd\uc2 ., or 1MDB, had traced almost $700 million in deposits into what they believe are Mr. Najib's personal accounts. Mr. Najib has denied wrongdoing. The ringgit hit 3.8165 on Monday, down more than 8% against the dollar, making it Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asian shares also are lower after results of Greece's referendum Sunday show a victory for the "no" campaign, which rejected austerity policies set out by the eurozone and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . Creditors have said the outcome imperils future compromise and puts Greece closer to leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 Stock Average shed 2.1% while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 1.1%. South Korea's Kospi was down 2.4%.\par \par \uc2 The euro sank 0.6% to $1.1053 against the U.S. dollar and fell 0.7% against the Japanese yen as investors sought safer assets. The yen also rose 0.2% against the dollar. Gold prices were flat at $1,163.20 per troy ounce, while Brent crude futures dropped 2.3% to $58.93.\par \par \uc2 Dominique Fong, Yifan Xie and Grace Zhu contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 HSBC Holdings Plc\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=GB0005405286"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=GB0005405286}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Rakuten, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=JP3967200001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=JP3967200001}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 HSBC Holdings Plc\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US4042804066"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US4042804066}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 05, 2015 21:15 ET (01:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150706eb7600043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
