\par\par \b \uc2 'Unprecedented' \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Action Underscores Margin-Call Worries -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2015\par 04:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China markets jumped higher Monday after opening, yet the gains are narrowing within the first half hour trading, suggesting that investors are uncertain about the effectiveness of Beijing's market-stabilization measures. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index gains 2.2% to 3766.90 after surging 7.8% at the immediate open, while the Shenzhen Composite Index rose 0.3% to 2103.55. This after China authorities announced many more market support measures over the weekend. Perhaps most significant of which was a statement by \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it will provide liquidity to China Securities Finance Corp, a state-owned firm that lends money to brokerages for margin finance. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is unprecedented...[it] shows that the government is highly concerned about potential market stampede caused by margin calls," said Li Bin, analyst at \uc2 Capital Securities\uc2 . Since hitting the peak at 5166.35 on June 12, the index has fallen by more than a quarter.\par \par \uc2  (Yifan.xie@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0215 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysian crude palm-oil futures are likely to see weakness due to concerns from the broader markets as fears of an eventual Greek exit from the euro-zone mount after most Greeks voted "no" to creditors' demands in Sunday's referendum. A Kuala Lumpur-based broker says the only saving grace is the depreciating ringgit, which is likely to limit losses in palm-oil; he tips support at MYR2,223-MYR2,232/ton as traders await for a deluge of industry data on Thursday. Benchmark September crude palm-oil futures on the Bursa Malaysia Derivatives ended 2 ringgit higher at 2,269 ringgit Friday. (huileng.tan@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @huileng_tan) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0213 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Tocom RSS3 rubber futures are lower after the U.S. dollar dropped against the yen due to fears of an eventual Greek exit from the eurozone mount after most Greeks voted "no" to creditors' demands in Sunday's referendum. Traders say jitters are widespread and broad-based as eurozone is a major rubber buyer, so any contagion effect from Greece's move may rock fundamentals for the rubber markets; sentiment is cautious as rubber prices have been rocked numerous times in the last fears years on eurozone fears and are currently at a depressed level. Benchmark December rubber trades Y3.3 lower at Y215.9/kg. (huileng.tan@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @huileng_tan) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0203 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan shares are down 0.1% at 9345.60, off its earlier low at 9293.31. Local investor confidence cheered as China stocks rise sharply after China's top brokerage firms said Saturday they would together buy around $19 billion worth of equities to stabilize China stock markets, says a local trader. "Taiwan's government-backed funds also likely step in to prevent the Taiex from falling below its 9300 support level amid concerns over Greece," the trader adds. Tech shares are mixed; TSMC (2330.TW) is down 0.7% at NT$141.50, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hon Hai Precision\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2317.TW) sheds 0.2% at NT$97.10 but HTC (2498.TW) gains 2.4% at NT$75.60. Financials are weak, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cathay Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2882.TW) off 0.8% at NT$52.00 and Fubon Financial (2881.TW) down 0.2% at NT$62.10. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0202 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it expects "increased volatility" in the Hong Kong markets, given the "No" vote in the Greek referendum over the weekend and the latest developments in China's A-share market. "The banking system in Hong Kong is highly liquid and is well equipped to handle any such volatility," it said in an emailed statement. The HKMA stands ready to provide liquidity support to the banking system should it become necessary to do so." The city's de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said investors are advised to remain calm and to manage their risks prudently. The benchmark \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index is down 0.34%. (fiona.law@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0200 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A fresh bout of risk aversion - triggered by Greece's vote against a eurozone bailout - has rallied the safe haven U.S. dollar broadly and imparted a firm tone to USD/TWD. The pricing of the benchmark 1-month nondeliverable forward contract in the offshore market indicates USD-bullishness in the speculative community. The broad rise of the greenback could lift USD/TWD clear of the daily Ichimoku Cloud consolidation zone and into the Bollinger uptrend channel that begins at 31.240. If the pair closes Monday above this mark, more U.S. dollar strength is likely in the near term. Risk appetite deteriorated after Greeks voted against further EU-imposed austerity measures, bringing closer the prospect of a Greek exit from the eurozone. U.S. stock futures slumped 1.5% in reaction and the euro fell, propelling the U.S. dollar index higher. USD/TWD is now 30.940 from its Friday close of 31.120. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0200 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index is 0.8% lower at 3316.66 shortly after Monday's open, as local companies fall victim to market fears that Greece will soon exit the eurozone. This after Greek citizens voted not to accept the country's bailout terms in a referendum over the weekend. In Singapore most large-caps are lower, led by Noble Group's (N21.SG) 2.7% fall after the company said it would buy back 2020 bonds early. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Genting Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  (G13.SG) is 1.7% lower and ComfortDelGro (C52.SG) loses 1.6%. Jardine Strategic (J37.SG) and Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG) are among the few gainers, up 0.8% and 0.4%, respectively. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Singapore Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S68.SG), which reported strong June market activity, falls 0.5% despite the good news. (jake.watts@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0155 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Recent volatility in Europe's peripheral bond market shows that developments in Greece can still have major knock-on effects beyond its borders, says Alliance Bernstein in a market note. "If Greece actually leaves the euro area, these knock on effects will intensify. That's because the departure of any country from the euro area would shatter the myth that membership is irrevocable," it says. This would lead to a higher risk premium in other countries, that could rise, "and act as a dangerous accelerant, at times of economic or financial market stress." (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0154 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/USD reaction to the Greek referendum results looks more moderate than the slide seen a week ago and it wouldn't be a surprise to see the pair showing some resilience later today during Europe trading, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a morning note. The EUR/USD touched a low of 1.0952, down 1.4% from late Friday in New York, compared with a nearly 2% fall to 1.0955 a week ago when Greece roiled markets with the decision to call the referendum. The pair is now clawing back lost ground at 1.1044. "We expect the bond yield spread between Germany and neighboring nations to widen" following the referendum results, \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Because of the current correlation between the yield spread and the EUR--with a wider spread leading to a stronger EUR--it wouldn't be a surprise if the EUR doesn't suffer further sharp falls during the day, the note adds. A EUR/USD value consistent with the U.S.-German yields gap is around 1.0972, \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  says, adding that over the mid-, long-term, it is keeping intact its EUR/USD outlook of 1.05 at year-end. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0151 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Greece failing to make debt payment to \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due July 20 increases the probability of the country exiting the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  zone, says Yuichi Kodama, chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Meiji Yasuda Life Insurance\f28 \uc2 \f2 . That failure would likely lead to the suspension of \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  emergency lending program that had helped Greeks banks operate, he says. He says the impact from Greece exit on the Japanese real economy wouldn't be big, but there may be an impact felt through financial markets such as lower Japan stocks and a higher yen, he says. European authorities are unlikely to compromise easily to Greece because doing so would prompt other debt-laden economies to demand easier terms for their own debt. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0148 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korean Air Lines\f28 \uc2 \f2  (003490.SE) plane skidded off a runway while landing at an airport in Guam on Sunday, but no injuries were reported. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korean Air\f28 \uc2 \f2  Flight KE2115 carrying 75 passengers tried to land at the airport early Sunday morning when it veered off the runway, says a spokesman for the carrier. "It was raining when the plane made its landing. We're looking into it to determine the exact cause of the incident," he says. The incident comes amid persistent aviation safety concerns after an \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asiana Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2  (020560.SE) plane in April overran a runway while landing in Hiroshima Airport in Japan, leaving about 20 passengers injured. Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korean Air\f28 \uc2 \f2  rise 2.2% at 42,400 won as fears of the outbreak of Middle East Respiratory Syndrome in the country subside. The broader market falls 0.7%. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Consolidation is likely for USD/CNY as the pair continues to tread water within a range of 6.2042-6.2092 demarcated by the respective entrances of the Bollinger downtrend and uptrend channels. The yuan has been relatively stable even as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unleashes yet more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures to stem the rout of its stock markets. Hopes for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 's inclusion of the yuan in its basket of reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later this year may be helping to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  attractive to investors. The Shanghai composite stock exchange has opened 7.8% higher Monday after China announced that its top stock brokerages would together buy around $19 billion worth of equities to prop up the market. Additionally, new IPO launches have been suspended; the recent slew of fresh listings were partly blamed for triggering an outflow from stocks as investors liquidated positions to subscribe for the IPOs. USD/CNY is now 6.2057 from its Friday close of 6.2049. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 05, 2015 22:21 ET (02:21 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150706eb7600027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
