\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks fall, bonds up as Greece veers toward default\b0\par\par\uc2 1,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2015\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to U.S. market open, adds comment, changes dateline from previous LONDON)\par \par \uc2 * Euro pares losses, Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt franc rise\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  easing fails to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  share rout\par \par \uc2 By Rodrigo Campos\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, June 29 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks added to a global selloff on Monday as markets digested news of capital controls in Greece and the country veered toward a default on its debt, while the euro recouped some of its earlier losses against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Talks between Athens and its creditors broke down over the weekend after Prime Minister Alexis Tsipras called a surprise referendum on the austerity cuts in the aid package proposed by Greece's creditors. Tsipras late Sunday announced moves to prevent a collapse of the banking system.\par \par \uc2 After an initial wave of selling, European stocks and the euro recovered some ground as investors judged that the Greek crisis still had some way to run. On Wall Street, benchmark \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  futures fell as much as 2 percent late on Sunday, but the index fell less than 1 percent in early Monday trading.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBOE\uc2  Volatility index, a measure of the premium traders are willing to pay for protection against a drop in the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2 , jumped as much as 19.1 percent to 16.7 points, the highest in three months. It was last up 13.7 percent at 15.94, not far from its historic average.\par \par \uc2 The session's market moves in Europe were considerable, but paled in comparison to the market impact of the financial crisis of 2008 or the last major round of Greek-spurred turmoil in 2011-12.\par \par \uc2 "This weekend's developments increase the likelihood of a (Greek) default, although there is still a significant chance that the pressure applied by the Europeans will lead to a surprise positive resolution to the drama," said Brian Reynolds, chief market strategist at New Albion Partners in New York.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  industrial average fell 109.23 points, or 0.61 percent, to 17,837.45, the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  lost 12.17 points, or 0.58 percent, to 2,089.32 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Composite dropped 35.47 points, or 0.7 percent, to 5,045.04.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 .FTEU3 index shed 1.7 percent, the most in a month. It earlier fell as much as 3.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 'WORRISOME' CHINA DROP\par \par \uc2 Greek banks and the stock market were closed on Monday and were expected to remain closed until after the July 5 referendum.\par \par \uc2 The Global X FTSE Greece exchange-traded fund (ETF) , which tracks the Athens stock market, was down 15.9 percent from its Friday closing price. Euro zone banks fell 4.3 percent in value.\par \par \uc2 Greece's bailout program expires on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the gloomy backdrop, China shares dived another 3.3 percent, bringing the losses in the past two weeks to more than 20 percent, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts in interest and reserve rates on Saturday failing to calm jittery investors.\par \par \uc2 "The way the creditors are dealing with Greece made sense; it sends a message to radical parties in the periphery that reneging on promises brings pain," said Brian Jacobsen, chief portfolio strategist at \uc2 Wells Fargo Funds Management\uc2  in Menomonee Falls, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 "I think the China decline is more worrisome," he said. "I'm not that worried as it mainly looks like froth coming out of the A shares market (but) if it spills into Hong Kong, that could be a tipping point."\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite fell 3.3 percent and Hong Kong's \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  fell 2.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 BUY THE DIP?\par \par \uc2 Until late last week, investors were hopeful that an 11th hour deal would prevent a Greek default and that the impact on other markets from a possible default would be minimal.\par \par \uc2 "The European financial system now has much less exposure to Greece than in 2011 and 2012," said Stephanie Flanders, Chief Market Strategist for Europe at \uc2 JPMorgan Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro fell overnight to as low as $1.0953, off 2 percent versus the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but recouped much of those losses to trade down just 0.4 percent at $1.1118.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe the market doesn't believe that a Greek exit could create the type of contagion that it would have done a few years ago," said \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Jane Foley.\par \par \uc2 "But if there is a no vote at the weekend, that theory will be tested."\par \par \uc2 The planned vote in Greece would decide whether to accept stronger austerity measures demanded by the creditors.\par \par \uc2 The euro received support as the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  confirmed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter gains for the franc against the bloc's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen strengthened 0.8 percent versus the greenback, giving up some of the gains that were as much as 1.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 TREASURIES, COMMODITIES\par \par \uc2 Benchmark \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields fell to one-week lows on Monday, with some traders flocking to U.S. debt as a risk-off move on worries about the impact of a Greek default on global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year Treasury note rose 26/32 in price to yield 2.3819 percent, after earlier hitting 2.292 percent, the lowest in a week.\par \par \uc2 "The coast is not clear ahead of quarter-end," said George Goncalves, head of U.S. interest rates strategy at \uc2 Nomura Securities International\uc2  in New York. "There is still a belief there's a solution (for Greece) even though I think it's misplaced."\par \par \uc2 Government borrowing costs in Europe's indebted southern countries shot up on worry about a possible contagion from the Greek developments.\par \par \uc2 However borrowing costs in Italy, Spain and Portugal were still less than half the levels seen in late 2011 and early 2012 at the height of the most recent debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold pared a gain of more than 1 percent on the day to trade mostly unchanged near $1,175 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude was down 1.8 percent at $62.15 a barrel and U.S. crude fell 1.7 percent $58.64 a barrel after having hit $58.04, a three-week low.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Richard Leong in New York, John Geddie, Anirban Nag, Jemima Kelly and Sudip Kar-Gupta in London, Nicola Saminather in Singapore and Hideyuki Sano in Tokyo; Editing by Bernadette Baum) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-6-29T15:37:21.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150629eb6t00ilo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
