\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan stays flat on suspected c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2015\par 06:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, June 26 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan was flat on\par Friday as dollar sales by big state-owned banks offset rising\par corporate dollar demand for dividend payments at the end of the\par first half of the year, traders said.\par     Traders suspected the dollar sales were at the behest of \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which sometimes uses state banks to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Senior Chinese officials have pledged to maintain the\par stability of the yuan amid the current economic slowdown.\par     In another sign of its aim to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stable, the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 set its official midpoint rate\par  at 6.1137 per dollar prior to market open, 0.02\par percent firmer than the previous fix at 6.1148.\par     The spot market opened at 6.2093 and touched an\par intraday low at 6.2104 per dollar in early trade, propelled by\par seasonal demand for dollars from Chinese listed companies, that\par typically need to prepare dollars in late June to pay dividends\par to overseas shareholders next month.\par     State-owned banks began selling dollars after the yuan\par weakened beyond 6.21, traders said. The yuan was changing hands\par at 6.2094 at midday, unchanged from the previous close.\par     Traders expected the yuan to continue trading in a narrow\par range in the near term.\par     "The seasonal dollar demand will soon ease, and there is no\par big motive for us to push the yuan's rate lower," said a trader\par at an Asian bank in Shanghai.\par     The yuan will end flat for the week if it holds onto its\par midday level.\par     A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official told a forum in Shanghai on Griday\par that China will gradually open its capital account, and promote\par the launch of an outbound investment pilots for institutional\par investors. The comments had no impact on\par trading, traders said. \par     The offshore yuan rate was roughly matching the onshore spot\par at 6.2095 per dollar.\par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item                        Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint               6.1137   6.1148    0.02%\par  Spot yuan                   6.2094   6.2094    0.00%\par  Divergence from midpoint*   1.57%              \par  Spot change ytd                                -0.09%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation             33.29%\par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument                         Current   Difference\par                                               from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan        *        6.2095    0.00%\par  Offshore non-deliverable forwards  6.2425    -2.06%\par                **                             \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par \par  (Reporting by the Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Simon\par Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par