\par\par \b \uc2 Taiwan Shares Tad Lower; CTBC Soars on Deal With China Citic -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2015\par 03:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan shares are down 0.1% at 9663.94, as investors tread cautiously after declines on Wall Street Tuesday. Tech heavyweight TSMC (2330.TW) is down 0.3% at NT$146.00 while Hon Hai (2317.TW) drops 0.1% to NT$97.30. Lender CTBC (2891.TW) jumps 4.6% to NT$23.95, after unveiling a share-swap deal with \uc2 China Citic Bank\uc2  and its unit. The news also drums up financial stocks: Fubon (2881.TW) rises 1.0% to NT$64.00 while \uc2 Cathay Financial\uc2  (2882.TW) is up 0.2% at NT$55.40. First Capital says foreign fund managers have recently increased their short positions in the Taiex futures, indicating the index could trend lower in the near term, possibly to 9500. (aries.poon@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Technology One\uc2  (TNE.AU) ticks a lot of boxes for Morgans...except one. The computer software company's profits and dividend yield continue to rise, but the stock has become such a darling of investors that Morgans finds its lofty valuation hard to fathom. \uc2 Technology One\uc2  is forecasting 10-15% profit growth in FY15, which Morgans believes would be a good outcome. "But given \uc2 Technology One\uc2  is trading on a PE of 35 times, we think it needs much more growth to justify the current multiple," analyst Nick Harris says. As a result, he retains a hold call after lifting \uc2 Technology One\uc2 's price target by 22% to A$3.82/share. Harris prefers Seek (SEK.AU), \uc2 REA Group\uc2  (REA.AU), and \uc2 Carsales.com\uc2  (CAR.AU). TNE last traded at A$4.00. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Anticipating short-term yen appreciation, it "wouldn't be too bad" for USD/JPY investors to lock in profits around 123-124, says Nomura chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Yunosuke Ikeda in a morning note. Ikeda puts forward three theories for the yen spike. First, there may be jawboning from Finance Minister Taro Aso about an increasingly volatile USD/JPY trade. Second, the pair (now at 123.00) needs fresh news/trading cues to break above the 125-mark. And, finally, there may be a possibility that the upcoming U.S. non-farm payrolls falls short of an increase of 200,000 in May. Taking a longer view, however, Ikeda favors the dollar, on the strength of two factors: higher U.S. yields and yen selling stemming from investment outflows from Japan. He expects the USD/JPY pair to hit 130-135 next year. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0139 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] It's a negative start for Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index on Wednesday. The index is 0.7% lower shortly after the open, last at 3437.39, with most of the large-cap stocks losing ground -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hongkong Land Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (H78.SG) is down 1.7%, and City Developments (C09.SG) is 1.4% lower. A handful of smaller companies announce various developments; \f28 \uc2 \f2 KTL Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  (EB7.SG) falls 1.1% after it says it is in talks to buy part of an equity stake in a South Korean firm. ISOteam (5WF.SG) gains 2.3% after announcing a placement of up to 9 million new ordinary shares. Among the morning's few large-cap gainers is \f28 \uc2 \f2 SembCorp Marine\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S51.SG) which is up 0.3%. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0132 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The possibility of Thai exports growing in 2015 remains slim, according to Bangkok-based SCB Economic Intelligence Center. "While exports value showed some signs of improvement in 2Q15, they may not be sufficient to help push the sector to return to growth this year," says SCB. The house expects to see a 1.3% drop in exports this year, putting Thailand on track for a record third consecutive year of export contraction. Thailand's exports shrank 3.99% during the first four months of 2015, after contracting 0.41% in 2014. "In 2Q15...export value contracted at a slower rate while the baht \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started weakening after \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut [its policy] rate--which should lend support to exports in the near future," adds SCB. Noting that various factors such as an economic slowdown in major trading countries and the end of tax privileges for Thai products to EU, will likely continue to put pressure on Thailand. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0117 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW gaps higher Wednesday in reaction to the sharp USD/JPY rally overnight which saw the Japanese yen fall to an 8-year low. The pair are closely-correlated due to \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 's vigilance in keeping the won relatively weak versus the yen - to maintain exporters' price-competitiveness. If USD/KRW ends the day above the 1,110 top of the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone, more upside for the pair is highly likely. The U.S. dollar index is at its strongest in a month as the euro continues to languish on Greece debt default concerns, while the U.S. Federal Reserve appears more likely to raise interest rates this year. USD/KRW is now 1,107.1 from its Tuesday close of 1,101.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0115 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR may soon notch a new 17-year high as it rises along the daily Bollinger uptrend channel, powered by yet another rise of the U.S. dollar index. The safe haven dollar rallied overnight, most prominently against the beleaguered euro and the Japanese yen. USD/IDR will notch a new 17-year high if it surpasses March's peak of 13,245 but may be capped on an intraday basis by the 13,265 top of the Bollinger uptrend channel. The ongoing Greek debt negotiations will likely continue to keep the euro depressed, thereby pressuring most USD/Asia pairs higher; but if Greece manages to get bailout funds released by the Eurogroup soon, EUR/USD may rebound very sharply. USD/IDR is now 13,235 from its Tuesday close of 13,205. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0112 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei is down 0.2% at 20397.08, off its session lows, as the dollar's abrupt surge after the prior day's market close helps to contain profit-taking, say traders. USD/JPY is near an 8-year high (now 123.00). "I think the only thing that pulled back (from going even higher) was U.S. stocks' fall. I expect Japan shares to go higher after any knee-jerk reaction to the Wall Street selloff," says Chris McGuire, CEO at Phalanx Capital Management, a Chicago-based hedge fund with assets in both Japan and China. "The yen will continue to weaken. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will make sure of it; it will be a slower grind for Japan shares to keep rising, but the direction is clear." Exporters are largely higher, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fuji Heavy Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7270.TO) up 3.1% at Y4,636, while heavyweights such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 KDDI\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9433.TO) and SoftBank (9984.TO) drag the index down; the pair are off 1.2% at Y2,838 and down 0.3% at Y7,382, respectively. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0109 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CNY may rise Wednesday - though technical boundaries nearby may limit upside - as the pair displays a return of correlation with the U.S. dollar index this week. The latter spiked overnight as the euro sank and the yen fell to an 8-year low. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  may hence set the daily USD/CNY benchmark rate higher accordingly, from Tuesday's 6.1172. The yuan has generally been very stable in a consolidation range between the lower and upper Bollinger bands (standard deviation 1), but if USD/CNY manages to break above 6.2122 and thus enters the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone, it might consolidate within a higher range for the near term. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has likely been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading in a stable range, as it seeks approval to make the yuan a reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; an unstable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would likely reduce the yuan's credibility as a store of value. USD/CNY closed Tuesday at 6.2040. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0103 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A third consecutive rise of the benchmark U.S. dollar index could negate the bearish path of USD/TWD. If the pair ends the day above 30.630 the Bollinger downtrend channel will be nullified, and short-term speculators could be spurred to trim short-USD positions. Speculative sentiment for USD/TWD - gauged by the offshore market - is turning bullish as the price of the 1-month nondeliverable forward contract creeps up toward par with that of the spot contract; it was priced at a discount earlier this week. External factors such as the ongoing Greece negotiations with Eurogroup ministers for the release of financial bailout funds continue to influence USD/Asia in general, though to a slightly less extent for USD/TWD. Recent hints from the U.S. Federal Reserve chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  that an interest rate hike is coming this year may fuel USD/TWD higher as well. USD/TWD closed Tuesday at 30.708. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0040 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude is likely to consolidate with risks skewed to the downside near-term after settling down $1.69 at $58.03/bbl Tuesday as long as it stays below hourly chart resistance at $59.50, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is tilting negative as the MACD indicator is bearish, the slow stochastic measure is turning bearish, while the rate-of-change momentum indicator is declining inside negative territory. Support is at $57.71 (Tuesday's low, near the lower 20-day two-standard deviation Bollinger Band); a breach would expose downside to $56.54 (April 29 low), then to $56.07 (April 28 low), $55.76 (April 23 low), $54.85 (April 20 low) and $53.39 (April 15 low). But a rise above $59.50 (near the middle 20-day Bollinger Band) would temper the negative near-term view, targeting $60.25 (Tuesday's high), then $60.80 (Friday's high), $60.94 (Thursday's reaction high), the upper 20-day two-standard deviation Bollinger Band (now at $61.30) and $61.85 (May 13 reaction high). July crude is up 32 cents at $58.35/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 26 May 2015 21:51 ET \par Taiwan Shares Tad Lower; CTBC Soars on Deal With -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0022 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP likely to consolidate with bearish bias Wednesday after hitting two-and-a-half month low 0.7057 on Tuesday. Cross undermined by weak euro sentiment as fears mount over that Greece may default on its debts. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold levels; five-day moving average below 15-day moving average and declining. Drop below 0.7057 would target 0.7031 (March 12 low), then 0.7010-0.7000 band (March 11 seven-year low-psychological line) and 0.6891 (Oct. 9, 2007 low). Resistance at 0.7103 (Tuesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.7117 (Monday's high), then 0.7163-0.7166 (Friday's high-Thursday's high). Spot EUR/GBP is at 0.7071. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 26, 2015 21:51 ET (01:51 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150527eb5r0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
