\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: Asia Manufacturing Rut Deepens; Europe Stabilizes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2015\par 13:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey and Alen Mattich\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: We've had more signs that Asia and Europe are going separate ways. Manufacturing data from the former were especially disappointing, whereas the eurozone's purchasing managers' end-April indexes were mostly better than their mid-April preliminary readings and generally confirmed a picture of expansion in the region. In Asia, the culprit is China, which is unambiguously undergoing a slowdown and hurting demand for its neighbors' exports. A key question is what measures Beijing takes to contain it and whether these might include letting the yuan fall -- a step that comes just as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is planning to declare that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fairly valued. One sign that the pressure for competitive devaluations across Asia is growing comes from Thailand, where official measures have driven the baht to its lowest level in five years. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: Final April \uc2 HSBC\uc2  manufacturing purchasing managers' index 48.9, down from 49.2 in mid-April "flash" reading and from 49.6 at end-March.\par \par \uc2 It didn't take long for the relief provided by the official \uc2 China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing\uc2  index of manufacturing activity -- which came in slightly stronger-than-expected last week at 50.1 -- to give way to dismay over the warning signals emitted by \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's measure. \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's index covers small and medium-sized, mostly private-owned companies whereas as the CFLP report focuses on larger companies and is skewed by a heavy inclusion of state-owned enterprises. The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  number is perhaps a better indication of how nonsubsidized firms are faring. It's clear that China is in a significant slowdown, one that's steeper than people had expected. The calls for more stimulus action from Beijing are growing. One question of concern to the rest of the world is what the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  will do about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, which until now it has refused to let fall. (MC)\par \par \uc2 ASIA: Other PMI readings also showed weakness in the region's manufacturing sector.\par \par \uc2 --S OUTH KOREA: PMI was 48.8 in April from 49.2 in March.\par \par \uc2 -- INDONESIA: PMI was 46.7 vs. 46.4 in March.\par \par \uc2 -- INDIA: PMI was 51.3 vs. 52.1 in March.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from China's slowdown is rippling out across the region. Even India, which had bucked the trend until now, had a disappointing manufacturing result, although it is at least in expansion mode. Indonesia's improvement is cold comfort -- that result is still deeply in the sub-50 level signifying contraction and only marginally higher than the record low set in March. South Korea needs exports to pick up, it's only hope being that a spring recovery in the U.S. boosts demand for its electronics goods. (MC)\par \par \uc2 EUROPE: Markit final April manufacturing purchasing managers indexes.\par \par \uc2 -- EUROZONE: Flash PMI was 52.0 vs. 51.9 for mid-April flash reading and vs. 52.2 end-March.\par \par \uc2 -- GERMANY: PMI was 52.1 vs. 51.9 for mid-April flash reading and vs. 52.8 end-March.\par \par \uc2 -- FRANCE: PMI 48.0 vs. 48.4 for mid-April flash reading and vs. 48.8 end-March.\par \par \uc2 -- ITALY: PMI 53.8 vs. 53.5 expected and 53.3 in March.\par \par \uc2 -- SPAIN: PMI 54.2 vs. 54.3 in March.\par \par \uc2 The eurozone's PMI readings were softer than last month but in aggregate slightly better than the flash readings. Given that there have been signs of recovery in other recent eurozone data, investors shouldn't be too fazed by these results overall. Notable, however, is the struggle that France is having -- in stark contrast to a continued rebound in Italy, a solid expansion in Spain and steady growth in Germany. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  is close to declaring the yuan to be fairly valued in coming months, The Wall Street Journal reports.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  isn't known for being a leader on such matters. Market activity should make it patently clear that the yuan is actually overvalued. Amid a slowdown in China's economy, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has frequently traded at the bottom end of the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. Meanwhile, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has more or less kept the midpoint of that band steady to the dollar while virtually every other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen against the greenback over the past year. Nonetheless, a belated recognition from the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has political significance as it undermines China's critics on Congress and could give greater weight to Beijing's broader designs to reshape the international economic order. China wants the yuan to be included the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's list of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and included in its Special Drawing Rights basket. An \uc2 IMF\uc2  vote of approval on valuation helps in that cause, though it will need to have the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fully floating and convertible to earn total recognition -- ironically, moves that would probably drive it lower right now. (MC)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND: The Thai baht is responding to some aggressive measures by the \uc2 Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to plunge to its lowest level in five years.\par \par \uc2 A combination of a rate cut on Wednesday last week and a relaxation of capital controls has given investors both the excuse and the ability to cash out of Thailand in recent days. Some are calling it Thailand's contribution to Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war. There's a symbolism to it, too: the devaluation of the Thai baht in July 1997 is what set off the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis of that era. Although we are in different circumstances, where most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are no longer rigidly pegged to the dollar, there's indeed a risk that continued actions like these will balloon into a cycle of all-out competitive devaluation across the region, especially if China were to weaken the yuan. (MC)\par \par \uc2 WORLD: Various markets remain closed for May Day.\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 12:25 p.m. EDT. Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago President \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  speech at the Columbus Economic Development Board Annual Meeting.\par \par \uc2 The unofficial spokesman for the doves on the Federal Open Market Committee can be expected to reiterate his case for waiting beyond the end of this year to raise rates. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 3:10 p.m. EDT. (12:10 p.m., San Francisco) Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President \uc2 John Williams\uc2  speech at the \uc2 Pacific Community Ventures\uc2  annual luncheon.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Williams is more of a centrist than Mr. Evans and of late has suggested a willingness to move toward a rate increase in the months ahead. Has the bout of weak first-quarter economic news softened his position on that? (MC)\par \par \uc2 To sign up for this email, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 HSBC Holdings Plc\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=GB0005405286"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=GB0005405286}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 HSBC Holdings Plc\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US4042804066"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US4042804066}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 04, 2015 07:56 ET (11:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150504eb540014a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
