\par\par \b \uc2 RRR Cut Is China's Fix for Growth Slowdown, Capital Flight -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2015\par 06:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2 's latest aggressive move to cut the reserve requirement ratio--the second time this year--is a response to continuing capital outflows and further deceleration in the country's economic growth, \uc2 Standard Chartered Bank\uc2  says. It says an RRR cut releases longer-term liquidity, lowers banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs (with the benefit likely to be passed on to borrowers), and provides a strong signal that the government intends to use available policy space to achieve its growth targets. The bank expects \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely cut the benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points by end-June to lower its cost of capital. Moreover, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is prepared to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent significant Chinese yuan depreciation, and would cut the RRR further, if necessary, to offset the loss of domestic liquidity due to such an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank adds. It says \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic growth may revive in the second half, supported by policy easing.\par \par \uc2  (chester.yung@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 Editor JSM\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 20, 2015 00:14 ET (04:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-04-15 0445GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020150420eb4k0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
