\par\par \b \uc2 The Yuan Strikes Back -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos   \par \uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 06:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Someday the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might buckle under the weight of a slowing economy. Beijing will make sure this isn't the year for that to happen.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan has strengthened mightily in the past couple of weeks, and is now just about where it started the year versus the dollar. That is despite a darkening growth outlook and expectations for increasingly loose monetary policy, factors that normally should exert downward pressure on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But China is different. While the government has made steps toward letting the market determine the exchange rate, it remains tightly controlled.\par \par \uc2 This year, it might make economic sense to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide to give growth a boost, similar to how a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is benefiting the eurozone and Japan. Some investors are even betting on a major devaluation. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would provide relief to exporters after the yuan has strengthened 15% on real, trade-weighted terms over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 But such a move is unlikely. China prizes stability in most things, and that extends to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , too. A fall of a few percentage points against the dollar, as happened last year, or maybe a smidgen more, seems around what Beijing is willing to tolerate.\par \par \uc2 There is also a wild card this year. The composition of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  known as the special drawing right, or SDR, is up for review starting in May, and China wants in.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  and other officials have been beating the drum this week about the yuan's inclusion in the SDR, which serves as an accounting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and means to move around capital among \uc2 IMF\uc2  members. Getting in would be a milestone in China's effort to position its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an eventual rival to the dollar in international finance.\par \par \uc2 There is nothing to preclude a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that goes down from joining the small group that is included in the SDR, namely the dollar, euro, yen and pound. But China would hardly want this to be the year when the yuan causes financial-market stress. A devaluation would also risk reigniting the politically-charged exchange-rate debate with the U.S., which holds sway in \uc2 IMF\uc2  matters.\par \par \uc2 The terms of inclusion state that an SDR \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be "freely usable." That doesn't mean China has to fully liberalize its capital account, or even stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the level of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mr. Zhou pointed to the Shanghai-Hong Kong Stock Connect program as a sign of progress since 2010, when the \uc2 IMF\uc2  rejected the yuan's inclusion. To convince the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , Mr. Zhou will need to deliver more changes, something he has promised.\par \par \uc2 In the long term, gaining SDR inclusion could strengthen the yuan, as it would become a more viable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for governments and central banks to hold. Of course, opening more to capital flows would also mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could weaken when the economy slows.\par \par \uc2 But for now, with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continuing to set a daily exchange rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep it near that rate, any falls will be guided by Beijing's hand.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alex Frangos at alex.frangos@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 27-03-15 0547GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150327eb3r0005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
