\par\par \b \uc2 Beijing Helps to Drive Yuan Higher-- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shen Hong   \par \uc2 1,054 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 23:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan recorded its best week in over seven years, as Beijing stepped into the markets to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher and outflank investors betting on losses as the economy cools.\par \par \uc2 The yuan gained 0.9% against the U.S. dollar since a week earlier, a sharp move for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that lost 2.5% against the greenback all of last year. It was only the third time in 20 years that the yuan made a weekly move of that magnitude.\par \par \uc2 The sudden gains serve as a reminder that Beijing still keeps a firm grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and won't allow heavy losses or one-way speculation. Capital has also been flowing out of China this year as the yuan has weakened--such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation turns off investors seeking higher-yielding assets. Worries linger that further yuan losses could exacerbate the trend and destabilize the already fragile financial system.\par \par \uc2 "It's pretty clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want the market to build up one-way expectations for the yuan and it wants to generate more two-way fluctuations," said Chaoping Zhu, economist at UOB Kay Hian Holdings in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's recent gains signal China may not be eager to join with central banks around the world that are making moves to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to boost exports by making their goods cheaper to buyers abroad. Severe devaluation would hurt China's goal of rebalancing the economy away from relying on exports and more toward domestic consumption.\par \par \uc2 And a volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swayed by speculators would threaten China's effort to make the yuan a more widely used global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Officials at the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 The yuan touched a three-month high Friday before slipping back to 6.2046 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets a daily fix on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allows it to trade within 2% of that level. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  raised that morning reference rate most days this past week, and traders say Chinese state banks also were buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up its value.\par \par \uc2 The surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a reversal from last year, when \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  engineered an unexpected wave of yuan depreciation, forcing an unwinding of billions of dollars in highly leveraged bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gaining value. Back then, Beijing was concerned that overseas cash was flooding its economy with excess funds and contributing to asset appreciation, such as in property prices.\par \par \uc2 "It's a sort of d\u233\'00\'E9j\u224\'00\'E0 vu. The only difference is that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is buying the yuan this time," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a Chinese bank in SHYShanghai.\par \par \uc2 With the dollar surging in global markets and the U.S. Federal Reserve poised to tighten monetary policy, Beijing's decision to cut interest rates twice since November to help juice growth has made its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even less appealing to investors seeking higher returns. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively tracks the dollar, so it has appreciated strongly against nearly every other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world alongside the greenback.\par \par \uc2 That has made it harder for Beijing to ease its monetary policy to stimulate its economy--a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes China's exports more expensive and foreign goods cheaper.\par \par \uc2 Investors have bet China would allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate to stimulate growth. This year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen 0.9% until this week. Investors have predominantly sold yuan nearly every day this year, consistently pushing it to the weaker end of the trading band.\par \par \uc2 But China is also battling a trend of capital leaving the country; $150 billion exited in the first two months of the year, according to \uc2 Westpac Bank\uc2  strategist Huw Mckay. Businesses in China have been less likely to hold yuan because of their fears of depreciation, and that has been an additional drag on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value. The outflows have made funds scarcer in the Chinese financial system, adding hurdles at a time when economic growth has significantly slowed already.\par \par \uc2 Allowing a substantial depreciation also would make it harder for Chinese companies to pay off the billions of dollars in dollar-denominated debt they have accumulated in recent years.\par \par \uc2 "Simply relying on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation won't revive China's exports either, because the current difficulties result from a confluence of factors, including weak external demand [and] rising labor and raw material costs," said Liu Dongliang, a senior analyst at \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many experts believe China will make further moves to stimulate its economy, including allowing banks to lend more by reducing the amount of reserves they need to hold, or cutting interest rates for a third time.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment toward the yuan has turned increasingly bearish in recent months, fueled in part by worries about China's economic growth having slipped 7.4% last year, its slowest pace in nearly a quarter century.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial institutions sold a net 108.3 billion yuan ($17.5 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in January, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, the second consecutive month of such net sales. There were net purchases of 42.2 billion yuan in February, though economists attributed that rebound to favorable comparisons with an earlier period, when Chinese exports were weak amid the Lunar New Year holiday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the yuan's reference exchange rate higher in three of the five trading days this past week. In addition, it has been using large state banks to act as its agent to sell dollars and buy yuan in the market, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "Without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan wouldn't have become so strong," said the head of trading at a Chinese bank.\par \par \uc2 However, if it is the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's goal to discourage speculators, the current round of engineered yuan strength is unlikely to last long.\par \par \uc2 "I think it will be short-lived. China's weak economic fundamentals make it difficult to sustain a strong yuan," said Mr. Zhu, predicting the yuan will likely end up dropping 3% against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 20, 2015 18:52 ET (22:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150320eb3k00429\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
