\par\par \b \uc2 Fed rate hike view hurts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  sentiment; yuan exception: Reuters poll\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon   \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Bearish bets on most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased in the last two weeks, with short positions in the Singapore dollar at a six-year high, as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  moved closer to raising interest rates, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Short positions on the Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS>, however, were reduced on perceptions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the survey of 12 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts from banks and asset management companies conducted between Tuesday and Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  dropped a reference to being "patient" on rates from its policy statement on Wednesday, opening the door wider for a hike this year. At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  downgraded its economic growth and inflation forecasts, signaling it is in no rush to raise borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 That caused investors to scale back expectations of a rate hike in June. Top Wall Street firms now see a Fed lift off in September.\par \par \uc2 While the Fed is expected to raise rates, central banks in Asia are likely to ease policy to tackle economic sluggishness and deflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 Pessimistic bets on the Singapore dollar <SGD=D3> soared to their largest since January 2009.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar fell to its weakest since July 2010 last week as a slowing economy and inflation added to expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ease monetary policy further in April.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah's <IDR=ID> short positions hit the highest since January 2014 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to its weakest since August 1998.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah came under pressure from capital outflows and dollar demand from local companies. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the worst performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year, but it refrained from reversing the trend.\par \par \uc2 Short positions in the South Korean won <KRW=KFTC> increased to the largest since early December after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly cut its policy interest rate <KROCRT=ECI> to a record low of 1.75 percent last week.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment on the Thailand's baht <THB=TH> became the most pessimistic since mid-December as the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  also slashed its benchmark interest rate <THCBIR=ECI>.\par \par \uc2 Short positions on the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=MY> rose to the highest level since late January as sliding oil prices underscored concerns that lower crude may hurt the country's current account surplus and result in a wider fiscal deficit. Malaysia is a net oil exporter.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under further pressure from growing worries about a cut in sovereign ratings.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment towards the Philippine peso <PHP=PDSP> turned negative for the first time since late January as investors scrambled for dollars to cover short positions.\par \par \uc2 The yuan bucked the regional pessimism, hitting a two-month high.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb speculators betting on its depreciation amid a gloomy outlook for the world's second-largest economy. It also set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's midpoint firmer.\par \par \uc2 The poll is focused on what analysts believe are the current market positions in nine Asian emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : the Chinese yuan, South Korean won, Singapore dollar, Indonesian rupiah, Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP>, Indian rupee <INR=D2>, Philippine peso, Malaysian ringgit and the Thai baht.\par \par \uc2 The yuan firmed sharply for the third straight day on Thursday, breaking through its 200-day moving average and recouping all of its losses for the year as corporate flight out of long-dollar positions intensified. [CNY/]\par \par \uc2 The poll uses estimates of net long or short positions on a scale of minus 3 to plus 3.\par \par \uc2 A score of plus 3 indicates the market is significantly long U.S. dollars. The figures include positions held through non-deliverable forwards (NDFs).\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Shaloo Shrivastava in BENGALURU; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-19T08:23:51.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150319eb3j009yd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
