\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Fed rate hike view hurts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  sentiment; yuan exception\b0\par\par\uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 08:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Singapore dollar short positions at 6-year high\par     * Rupiah bearish bets largest since January 2014\par     * Won short positions largest since early December\par     * Yuan sentiment less pessimistic\par \par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, March 19 (Reuters) - Bearish bets on most\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increased in the last two weeks, with\par short positions in the Singapore dollar at a six-year high, as\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  moved closer to raising interest rates, a\par Reuters poll showed on Thursday.\par     Short positions on the Chinese yuan, however,\par were reduced on perceptions that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would support\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to the survey of 12 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts\par from banks and asset management companies conducted between\par Tuesday and Thursday.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped a reference to being "patient" on rates from\par its policy statement on Wednesday, opening the door wider for a\par hike this year. At the same time, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  downgraded\par its economic growth and inflation forecasts, signalling it is in\par no rush to raise borrowing costs. \par     That caused investors to scale back expectations of a rate\par hike in June. Top Wall Street firms now see a Fed lift off in\par September. \par     While the Fed is expected to raise rates, central banks in\par Asia are likely to ease policy to tackle economic sluggishness\par and deflationary pressures.\par     Pessimistic bets on the Singapore dollar soared to\par their largest since January 2009.\par     The Singapore dollar fell to its weakest since July 2010\par last week as a slowing economy and inflation added to\par expectations that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may ease monetary policy\par further in April.\par     The Indonesian rupiah's short positions hit the\par highest since January 2014 as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell to its weakest\par since August 1998.\par     The rupiah came under pressure from capital outflows and\par dollar demand from local companies. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the worst performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so\par far this year, but it refrained from reversing the trend.\par     Short positions in the South Korean won increased\par to the largest since early December after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par unexpectedly cut its policy interest rate to a\par record low of 1.75 percent last week. \par     Sentiment on the Thailand's baht became the most\par pessimistic since mid-December as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  also\par slashed its benchmark interest rate.\par  \par     Short positions on the Malaysian ringgit rose to\par the highest level since late January as sliding oil prices\par underscored concerns that lower crude may hurt the country's\par current account surplus and result in a wider fiscal deficit.\par Malaysia is a net oil exporter.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under further pressure from growing\par worries about a cut in sovereign ratings.\par     Sentiment towards the Philippine peso turned\par negative for the first time since late January as investors\par scrambled for dollars to cover short positions.\par     The yuan bucked the regional pessimism, hitting a two-month\par high.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par stabilise the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb speculators betting on its\par depreciation amid a gloomy outlook for the world's\par second-largest economy. It also set the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's midpoint\par firmer.\par     The poll is focused on what analysts believe are the current\par market positions in nine Asian emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : the\par Chinese yuan, South Korean won, Singapore dollar, Indonesian\par rupiah, Taiwan dollar, Indian rupee,\par Philippine peso, Malaysian ringgit and the Thai baht.\par     The yuan firmed sharply for the third straight day on\par Thursday, breaking through its 200-day moving average and\par recouping all of its losses for the year as corporate flight out\par of long-dollar positions intensified. \par     The poll uses estimates of net long or short positions on a\par scale of minus 3 to plus 3.\par     A score of plus 3 indicates the market is significantly long\par U.S. dollars. The figures include positions held through\par non-deliverable forwards (NDFs).\par     The survey findings are provided below (positions\par in U.S. dollar versus each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ):\par \par  DATE     CNY   KRW   SGD   IDR   TWD   INR   MYR   PHP   THB\par  19-Mar   0.46  1.10  1.48  1.33  0.53 -0.30  1.63  0.33  0.44\par  5-Mar    0.90  0.36  1.24  1.10  0.46 -0.64  1.34 -0.28  0.05\par  18-Feb   0.35  0.32  0.86  0.58  0.39 -0.15  0.74 -0.17 -0.15\par  5-Feb    0.62  0.04  1.16  0.35  0.36 -0.62  1.42 -0.35  0.10\par  22-Jan  -0.04  0.02  0.72  0.38  0.19 -0.73  1.69 -0.02  0.03\par  8-Jan    0.11  0.49  1.05  0.88  0.80 -0.15  1.98  0.13  0.40\par  18-Dec   0.29  0.35  0.89  0.89  0.94 -0.07  1.65 -0.22  0.62\par  4-Dec   -0.51  1.13  1.25  0.43  0.92 -0.47  1.59  0.13  0.47\par  20-Nov  -0.88  1.37  1.12  0.07  0.88 -0.25  1.16  0.50  0.56\par  6-Nov   -0.87  1.02  0.74 -0.02  0.41 -0.62  1.01  0.28  0.54\par     \par \par  (Additional reporting by Shaloo Shrivastava in BENGALURU;\par Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-19T08:13:26.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150319eb3j008t3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
