\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan little changed, traders say dollar supply matches demand\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 05:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, March 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan was little\par changed against the dollar on Tuesday, with dollar supply and\par demand roughly in balance while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set an official\par midpoint almost flat.\par     The balance helped offset the impact of another rally in the\par U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in global markets, traders said.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 set the midpoint rate\par  at 6.1572 per dollar prior to market open, moderately\par weaker than the previous fix of 6.1563.\par     Spot yuan opened at 6.2640 per dollar and was\par changing hands at 6.2639 at midday, 5 pips weaker than the\par previous close and 1.73 percent away from the midpoint. The spot\par rate is currently allowed to trade with a range 2 percent above\par or below the official fixing on any given day.\par     Traders said dollar demand was strong in the Chinese market,\par partly driven by the greenback's continuous bull run since the\par middle of last year, but the demand had largely been offset by\par heavy inflows from China's repeated record high trade surpluses.\par   \par     Data on Tuesday showed China's annual consumer inflation\par recovered in February, exceeding expectations, but producer\par prices continued to slide, underscoring deepening weakness in\par the economy and deflationary risks. \par     The data had no immediate impact on the market, traders\par said, adding, however, that if China's economy was persistently\par faced downward pressure and the U.S economy continued to\par recover, pushing the Federal Reserves to hike interest rates,\par the yuan would face greater depreciation pressure.\par     The yuan, which hit its trough since October 2012 last week,\par has so far fallen 1 percent this year after a 2.4-percent slump\par in 2014, its first significant annual depreciation since 1994.\par     The depreciation runs contrary to an urge by U.S. critics\par that China should let the yuan appreciate to balance bilateral\par trade between the world's two largest economies.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nathan Sheets\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Undersecretary for\par International Affairs and America's top financial diplomat, on\par Monday called on China to more swiftly adopt policy changes that\par would allow market forces to determine the value of its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     The comments came even after Sheets acknowledged last month\par that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had apparently stopped \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in exchange\par markets to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since July.\par     On Tuesday, the offshore yuan was trading 0.21 percent\par weaker than the onshore spot at 6.277 per dollar. \par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item                       Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint              6.1572   6.1563    -0.01%\par  Spot yuan                  6.2639   6.2644    0.01%\par  Divergence from midpoint*  1.73%              \par  Spot change ytd                               -0.96%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation            32.13%\par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument                         Current   Difference\par                                               from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan        *        6.277     -0.21%\par  Offshore non-deliverable forwards  6.401     -3.81%\par                **                             \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par  (Reporting by the Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-10T05:13:27.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150310eb3a003ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
