\par\par \b \uc2 Up & Down Wall Street: Dwindling Fodder For The Bull -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 1,796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 06:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 5\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM BARRON'S 3/9/15) \par    By Randall W. Forsyth \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Is the "patient" about to pass?\f28 \par \par \uc2 That isn't a person of which we speak, but a key phrase used by the Federal Open Market Committee to connote that monetary policy is to remain on hold for a couple of meetings to come. When the Fed's policy-setting panel gathers on March 17 and 18, there's a good chance the "patient" will be gone. That would leave the path open for the FOMC to lift its target for the federal-funds rate at the June 16-17 confab from the near-zero level that has prevailed since the crisis days of December 2008.\par \par \uc2 Such were the inferences of the global-interest-rate, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and equity markets after Friday's news of a stronger-than-expected employment report. U.S. stocks plunged by more than 1%, while Treasury yields shot to their highs for the year, and the dollar traded at levels not seen in over 11 years -- all on the prospect for a nudge higher in the Fed's key policy rate.\par \par \uc2 But, in that, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be going against the tide of rate cuts and other monetary-easing measures being taken by its counterparts abroad. In particular, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week announced terms of its long-awaited quantitative easing, consisting of purchases of 60 billion euros per month in euro-zone sovereign obligations -- even at the negative interest rates becoming more common. At the exchange rate for the euro by week's end, that would be worth some $65 billion; but that's a moving target, given the euro's steep descent through $1.10 and apparently toward parity with the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the world, where there still are interest rates on the plus side of zero, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  became the latest of 20-odd central banks to cut rates this year. That followed a reduction in the growth target in the world's second-largest economy to "around 7%" announced last week by Beijing; the addition of that unusual modifier left room for a shortfall in that goal, already the lowest in two decades.\par \par \uc2 With an estimated $2 trillion worth of government bonds yielding less than zero, investors piled into corporate securities paying something, anything. Companies obliged by issuing more than $50 billion in new securities last week, including a $21 billion deal from \uc2 Actavis\uc2  (ticker: ACT) to help fund its acquisition of Botox maker \uc2 Allergan\uc2 . So, it seems that at least some of the flow of liquidity from \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions is finding its way into stocks via the corporate credit markets.\par \par \uc2 The prospect that some of that flow could be slowed by a Fed rate hike -- even one as far off as June, which seems an eternity away from the endless winter we're suffering in the East -- sent the Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbling some 278 points, or 1.5%, on Friday. That was without the support of Apple (AAPL), which is due to be added to the blue-chip gauge on March 19, replacing \uc2 AT&T\uc2  (T) -- ironically the sole wireless carrier initially to offer the iPhone, the device mainly responsible for making Apple the world's most valuable company.\par \par \uc2 The February jobs report, showing a 295,000 gain in nonfarm payrolls, about 60,000 more than predicted by economists, plus a dip in the unemployment rate to 5.5% from 5.7% in January, evidently was enough to convince the markets that a June Fed rate hike is now likely. The June fed-funds futures contract was pricing in a 70% probability of a move to 0.25% to 0.5% at Friday's settlement, up from 48% the day before, according to the \uc2 CME\uc2 . By September, the probability is 82% of a hike, with a 22% chance of two quarter-point hikes by then.\par \par \uc2 But while the markets have been focused on the Fed and the ECB, Albert Edwards, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's bearish head of global strategy, writes they've been missing the liquidity tightening "that is strangling the global economy." That is, the contraction of central banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 The accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is equivalent to more-familiar QE, he asserts. Both involve the purchase by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of assets, whether domestic bonds or foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which expands its balance sheet. Central banks abroad, especially in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had engaged in massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising (while the dollar was sinking in previous years) and thus hurting export competitiveness. That also expands domestic liquidity and risks creating credit bubbles.\par \par \uc2 In China, the renminbi's real trade-weighted exchange rate has risen, despite easing slightly against the dollar (the RMB is up against the euro and the yen, which are down substantially from their peaks a few years ago). "In the current deflationary environment, the Chinese authorities simply can no longer tolerate the continued appreciation of their real exchange rate caused by the dollar link," Edwards writes.\par \par \uc2 As Chinese growth slows, policy makers are unlikely to lean too heavily on conventional monetary or fiscal stimulus measures, according to the Global Investment Strategy letter from BCA research. That leaves the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "the instrument of choice for boosting demand," with the RMB likely to decline some 7% to 8% over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 As the world's largest holder of Treasuries, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be expected to slow its accumulation of U.S. securities. Other central banks may become sellers of dollar assets, as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  come under pressure, as the Mexican peso was on Friday in the wake of the rise in U.S. bond yields -- after the jobs report. All of which points to a receding tide of liquidity, the main factor that has lifted the markets' boats.\par \par \uc2 I am a New Yorker by birth. While I grew up near the northern tip of Manhattan, where the local high school can boast the likes of both \uc2 Alan Greenspan\uc2  and Henry Kaufman, as well as Rod Carew and Manny Ramirez, among its alumni, a big chunk of my adult life was spent in Brooklyn. A good part of it was in the nether reaches of the borough, which was connected by the Brighton Line to "The City," as Manhattan was referred to on the signs of my station at Gravesend Neck Road, an aptly named thoroughfare if there ever was one. One of my enduring memories of the old neighborhood was my unsuccessful attempt to purchase a copy of Barron's after I had my first byline. The local newsstand didn't carry the august financial weekly you're reading, but did feature a large display of magazines devoted to the study of the female anatomy.\par \par \uc2 Brooklyn has come a long way since then, with areas that used to be charitably described as gritty becoming chic. One of them is Dumbo, the made-up acronym for Down Under the Manhattan Bridge Overpass, where old factories have given way to ritzy lofts. It's also the location of \uc2 Etsy\uc2 , an online marketplace for various crafts. Folks sell things they've made or fixed up, such as television stands or towels or various cutesy items to peddle on an online marketplace far more attractive than \uc2 Craigslist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dumbo sits across the East River from Wall Street, so it's not surprising that \uc2 Etsy\uc2  would want to sell stock along with handicrafts. Last week, it filed its initial public offering with the stated intention of raising $100 million, which would values the company at about $1.7 billion. Not bad for an outfit that generated about $196 million in revenue last year from merchant fees and commissions on the sale of the cutesy crafts sold on the site.\par \par \uc2 Etsy\uc2  prides itself in eschewing the usual stuff, which extends to its accounting. Based on its measure of adjusted earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization, the company made some $23 million last year. But it lost $15 million under generally accepted accounting rules. Yet again, a hot new company doesn't count stock-based compensation as an expense because that doesn't involve a cash outlay, a dubious proposition.\par \par \uc2 But is \uc2 Etsy\uc2  another sign of the seeming technology bubble? Not necessarily, according to Aswath Damodaran, a finance professor at NYU's Stern School. There should be more truth in labeling, he writes on his blog: "In my book, \uc2 Tesla Motors\uc2  [TSLA] is an automobile company, Uber is a car service (or transportation) company, and \uc2 Lending Club\uc2  [LC] is a financial-services company, and none of them should be categorized as technology companies. The fact that these firms use technology innovatively or to their advantage cannot be used as justification for treating them as technology companies, since technology is now part and parcel of even the most mundane businesses.\par \par \uc2 "Both companies and investors are complicit in this loose labeling, companies because they like the 'technology' label, since it seems to release them from the obligation of explaining how much they need to invest to scale up, and investors because it allows them to pay multiples of revenue or earnings that would be difficult (if not impossible) to justify in the actual businesses that these firms are in."\par \par \uc2 It isn't tech companies per se that are richly priced, he continues, "but baby and young technology companies. It is true that young social-media companies have gone public to rapturous responses over the last few years, but \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  (SHAK), which is definitely not a technology company (unless you can have a virtual burger and an online shake), also saw its stock price double on its offering day."\par \par \uc2 Etsy\uc2  must hope that sort of spell is cast on prospective buyers of its coming stock offering. It may not qualify as a tech company, but by Damodaran's assessment, neither would have Pets.com during the Internet bubble. \u57352\'E0\'08\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 e-mail: randall.forsyth@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 AT&T, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US00206R1023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US00206R1023}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Allergan, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0184901025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0184901025}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Apple, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0378331005"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0378331005}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor K