\par\par \b \uc2 Another Culprit of Low Inflation: Asia's Stronger--Yes, Stronger-- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel Rosenthal   \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 11:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Falling oil prices are exacerbating low inflation across Asia but there's yet another culprit: \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.\par \par \uc2 True, most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia are weaker against a resurgent dollar. Still, many have strengthened against the euro and the yen, which have fallen amid a recent spate of dismal economic data and easing measures.\par \par \uc2 East Asia buys large amounts of goods from Europe and Japan, so this is making imports cheaper and adding to downward pressures on prices. DBS economist David Carbon thinks strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have an even bigger impact on inflation than falling crude oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Asia's imports comprise 20% to 35% of gross domestic product, while imports of crude oil make up 1% to 6%, he says. "When you do the math, strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are just as important a driver of low inflation as oil."\par \par \uc2 Asia is now importing more from Europe and Japan than from the U.S., so local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength against the weakening euro and yen is having an outsize effect on prices. Asia's imports from Europe in the final months of last year were more than 40% higher than those from the U.S. in dollar terms--and from Japan, roughly 10% higher, according to data from HSCB Holdings PLC.\par \par \uc2 Given worries over deflation across the region, central banks should be happy to see \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, which would give a lift to exports. But as always, there are other concerns at work.\par \par \uc2 In China, authorities fear a weak yuan would spur capital outflows as investors lose confidence, particularly as the U.S. prepares to raise interest rates. A signal of this trend: China's banks posted net sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. That's also a reason the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong, even against the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves have fallen by about $150 billion since mid-2014 and the yuan has appreciated more than 10% against a triple-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket of U.S. dollars, euros and yen, according to DBS.\par \par \uc2 A stronger yuan also would give consumers more local spending power. That's a bigger priority for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  than propping up China's shrinking export economy, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Fred Neumann. The share of exports as a portion of GDP has fallen by a third over the past decade to 26%, he says, adding that competitive pricing from a weaker yuan may help exporters' profit margins, but isn't enough to rouse overseas demand.\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, the baht's appreciation, by 10% against the triple-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, spurred local business groups pinched by waning competitiveness to campaign for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Thailand's foreign reserves have fallen by $10 billion over the past six months.\par \par \uc2 An average of 10 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen about 7% against the triple-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket over the past six months, according to data from DBS. Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen over that same period in many of those countries: China, Singapore, Malaysia, Thailand, Taiwan, Korea and the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 Still, others hesitate to assign too much importance to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  factor.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves "are of secondary importance," says ING economist \uc2 Tim Condon\uc2 . Weak demand is sapping saps firms' wiggle room to raise prices. Add to that falling oil--which has slashed prices of other commodities and food--and a pretty clear picture emerges for the cause of Asia's deflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 06-03-15 1032GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150306eb36000as\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
