\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. lawmakers battle over  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  in trade deals\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason Lange and Krista Hughes   \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2015\par 01:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Feb 25 (Reuters) - U.S. lawmakers are drafting battle plans to punish foreign rivals accused of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation but a growing chorus of critics argue the battle is already won and warn the push may derail a massive trade pact.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan bill introduced this month and proposals from other lawmakers seek to stop trading partners such as China and Japan from manipulating exchange rates to make their goods cheaper for overseas buyers.\par \par \uc2 The trigger for the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  offensive is a nearly completed Trans-Pacific Partnership (TPP) trade pact. It encompasses 12 Pacific Rim trading partners including Japan, whose stimulus program has helped lop off a third of the yen's value since late 2011, worrying U.S. automakers like \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And China is seen by many Americans as a great stealer of jobs, following years in which Beijing weakened its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to undercut American manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Insistence on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  chapter in the deal is widely seen as a "poison pill" which could derail the entire pact and reflects many lawmakers' distrust of more open trade.\par \par \uc2 "You always need a bogeyman, someone to blame your troubles on," said Jeffrey Frankel, a Harvard professor who was a top economist in the Clinton White House.\par \par \uc2 A series of respected economists and former top officials have voiced concern that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions will derail the trade pact, although some are also lined up on the other side.\par \par \uc2 "Almost certainly, the effort would be a classic deal breaker," former Treasury official Ted Truman wrote in a blog post last week.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  spoke out against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposals on Tuesday, telling senators such rules could "hobble" monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Evidence these days is scant that either Japan or China, America's top trading partners in Asia, are engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation following decades of U.S. pressure to lay off. Even backers of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules concede Japan is not manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, although they warn it could again in future.\par \par \uc2 Japan's yen is weakening thanks in part to the same policies pursued by the U.S. Federal Reserve and it maintains that it has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2011.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo signed on for a 2013 \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  agreement, pushed by Washington, in which it agreed to use monetary policy only to boost domestic growth.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  has been increasing pressure on South Korea, another potential TPP partner, to let the won appreciate, but does not consider Seoul a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 China, according to the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , has stuck by a deal struck in 2014 that it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yuan has appreciated 57 percent in real trade-weighted terms since 2004, more than almost any other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Beijing could choose to reverse course and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still controls the exchange rate by setting an official trading band, economists say slowing economic growth, interest rate cuts and capital outflows mean market forces, rather than purchases of dollars, are weakening the yuan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  now appears to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up, as evidenced by the slight decline in its near $4 trillion in dollar reserves during the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 "We have to get used to the new reality," said David Dollar, who was the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 's top emissary to China between 2009 and 2013, a time when America was actively pressing Beijing to stop weakening the yuan. (Reporting by Jason Lange and Krista Hughes; Editing by Lisa Shumaker) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-26T01:04:15.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150226eb2q000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
