\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan weakens on deflation fears\b0\par\par\uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2015\par 04:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Feb 11 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened\par marginally on Wednesday amid concerns that deflationary risks\par are growing, increasing the need for more policy easing.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.1315 per dollar prior to the market open, weaker than the\par previous fix of 6.1295. \par     The yuan has been weakening against the dollar since late\par last year, mostly reflecting the dollar's massive rally as the\par U.S. economy has improved, fuelling expectations of a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal\par Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate rise this year. \par     A raft of weak Chinese economic data have also raised\par expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par guide the yuan lower. Consumer inflation was weaker than\par expected in January and factory deflation worsened, while the\par country's trade performance slumped last month.\par     Spot yuan opened at 6.2450 per dollar and was\par changing hands at 6.2418 at midday, 2 pips weaker than the\par previous close and 1.80 percent away from the midpoint. The spot\par rate is currently allowed to trade with a range 2 percent above\par or below the official fixing on any given day.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to loosen policy further\par after cutting bank reserve requirements last week for the first\par time in over two years, seen as a mostly defensive move against\par capital outflows. That followed a surprise cut to benchmark\par interest rates in November, also the first such move in more\par than two years, to lower borrowing costs and support growth.\par     Traders remained sceptical, however, that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par would allow a major depreciation of the yuan, which could\par trigger more outflows.\par     "The weak data yesterday didn't have much impact on\par markets," said a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at the Japanese bank Mizuho in\par Shanghai.\par     "The overall trend for the yuan should remain a gradual\par appreciation."\par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.06 percent weaker than the\par onshore spot at 6.2453 per dollar. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.3665, -3.69 percent away from the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate, and now that the trading band has been widened to 2\par percent in either direction, corporates are much warier of using\par the NDF to hedge given the basis risk inherent in them.\par     As a result the market has lost liquidity in recent years\par and has frequently proven an unreliable measure of market\par sentiment.\par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint      6.1315   6.1295    -0.03%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.2418   6.2416    0.00%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    1.80%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change ytd                       -0.61%\par  Spot change since 2005                32.60%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.2453    -0.06%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.3665    -3.69%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par \par  \par   \par \par \par \par  (Reporting By Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-11T04:50:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150211eb2b00566\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
