\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore's MyRepublic An Emerging Telco, Nomura Says -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2015\par 04:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 0343 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Fledgling Singapore Internet service provider MyRepublic is fast growing and looking to expand in New Zealand, Australia and other regional markets, making it a company to watch as a future wireless provider, Nomura said. "Given the progress and the impact made by MyRepublic in fixed broadband, along with its strong and prolific shareholder backing, one shouldn't dismiss the possibility of it entering the wireless space in Singapore," the research house said. "It may be a 2016 event and beyond even, so we need to keep monitoring this." Nomura noted that MyRepublic, which provides broadband services, now has around 30,000 subscribers in Singapore, or a 3%-4% market share. (jake.watts@wsj.com; _jmwatts) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] India and the US are aiming for trade of about $500 billion by 2020, according to \uc2 US India Business Council\uc2 , a trade group. U.S.-India bilateral trade has approached $100 billion from $19 billion in 2000, and the two-way trade relationship supports nearly 300,000 jobs in the US and over 500,000 jobs in India, according to the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2 , according to the council. Members of USIBC have already identified over $41 billion in investments over the next two to three years, it added. The U.S. is India's second-biggest trading partner after China. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @jaikrishna) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0338 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Oil prices continue to consolidate for over a month now but there's still the possibility that prices either spike upwards or downwards, analyst Daniel Ang at Phillip Futures says. "We continue to believe in the consolidation range of $49.58 and $44.35 for WTI, and $51.37 and $46.4 for Brent," he says. Mr. Ang says in order to take advantage of this consolidation investors are advised to take long positions when prices hit the lower price limit and short positions when prices hit the upper limit of the price range identified. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  WTI crude is up 5 cents at $45.20/bbl, Brent crude is up 13 cents at $48.29/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0338 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's yuan rises slightly as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guides the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher after the euro clawed back some ground overseas. The yuan is at 6.2520 vs 6.2542 at Monday's close. But the yuan is still trading very close to its 2% downside limit of 6.2591, with the central parity at 6.1364 vs 6.1384 Monday. "Demand for the U.S. dollar remains strong, mostly from enterprises' real demand, who are concerned the yuan could fall further," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.3580 from 6.3705 late Monday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0329 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The chips are down in Macau, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Galaxy Entertainment Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0027.HK) is surging after banks' estimates from average daily table revenue show that it was the best performer among casinos during the start of January. The casino's shares rose as much as 5.1%, and currently fetch 4.3% at 42.65 Hong Kong dollars (US$5.50), taking its gains in the past week to 15.1%. Still, analysts remain cautious on Macau. "Our on-the-ground research points to a further deterioration in the VIP and mass segments' dynamics over the past three months," said Daiwa Capital Markets, cutting its 2015 forecasts for gross gambling revenues from a 7% year-over-year decline to 21%. "As we see it, recent developments overturn the 'build it and they will come' mantra for 2015 and we expect delays to new property openings," Daiwa said. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/PHP is likely to remain under pressure despite rebounding to 44.17 from its Monday close of 44.09. The strong chart resistance of the 200-day moving average - currently at 44.28 - may deter traders from betting on a sustained USD/PHP rally in the near term. Instead, short-term speculators may attempt to sell the dollar if it reaches the 200-day moving average line, betting on an impending downside break of the 44.00 round-figure trading barrier - which if breached may trigger stoploss orders to sell USD/PHP. The possibility of financial market distress over Greek bonds and continued oil price weakness has been detrimental to risk-taking in other parts of the world and thus firming up the safe haven U.S. dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0315 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is extending earlier loss to fall to as low as 118.13 (from around 118.50) following Economy Minister Akira Amari's comments that the government and the BOJ haven't set a clear deadline for 2% inflation. The comments suggest "the government side is slightly loosening its interpretation" about the inflation target, thus receding expectations for early monetary easing by the BOJ and inviting the JPY buying, says a senior European bank dealer. The pair is now at 118.22. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0314 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The yuan has been rising against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of other emerging economies despite its weakness against the U.S. dollar, and this will likely put pressure on China's exporters this year, economists at Capital Economics say. The research house cited U.S. data as showing that prices of imports from China have remained stable in dollar terms while prices from other emerging economies have been falling. "In recent weeks, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to have been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the yuan from weakening. [The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] will come under pressure to allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slide if exports start to suffer," its economists say in a note to clients. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0308 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/THB's bearish technical bias remains as long as the 32.60 top of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel is intact by Tuesday's close. Though the U.S. dollar is broadly supported in Asia, USD/THB has been on a controlled descent over the past two weeks, augmented by bearish technical chart signals that suggest a move to 32.34 is possible in the near term. Amid spates of risk aversion due to crude oil prices collapsing, a potentially destabilizing Greek election outcome, and escalation in the Russia-Ukraine conflict, the baht has been rising. Thailand is typically less affected by Western risk aversion compared to its peers, but it may show more reliance on Japan's export performance due to heavy investment in production facilities by Japanese companies. USD/THB is now 32.59 from its Monday close of 32.56. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is slightly weaker at 118.31 (falling as low as 118.24 from around 118.50) in late morning as the USD buying "ran its course," says a Japanese bank dealer. The market mood turned toward risk with Tokyo stocks going up and U.S. yields increasing, inviting USD buying. "But the buying failed to last," the dealer says. Another dealer says the pair seemed show a slight but quick falloff due to Japanese Economy Minister Akira Amari's comments that the government and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  haven't set clear deadline for a 2% inflation target. Mr. Amari also said both an unlimited fall or unlimited gain in the yen would be undesirable. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PAB \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0238 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Japanese government should keep its policy of providing financing for the construction of new coal-fired power plants overseas in order to support Japanese power plants makers, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Makiko Ono. In Asia, Chinese power plant makers are expanding their businesses as the country's own demand for new coal-power plants slow. Sometimes their businesses comes with low-interest rate financing by China state-owned banks, against which Japan should compete, Ono says in a report. Many developed countries have said they wouldn't lend money for construction of new coal-fired power plants because they want to curb global warming. (mari.iwata@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0232 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan government bonds fall, tracking overnight losses in U.S. Treasurys as concerns over the Greece election results eased, says a local trader. Investors are turning cautious after recent sharp gains in local bonds after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced larger-than-expected bond-buying stimulus Thursday. The five-year bond yield has hit record low at 0.994% Monday. "The below-one-percent yield of the five-year bonds was considered really too low...so some investors prefer to take profits now," the trader adds. But the outlook for local bonds remains sound as more market participants expect the U.S. to put off its expected interest rate hike until later this year. The five-year yield rebounds to 1.0055% from 0.9985% late Monday; the 10-year yield rises to 1.4988% from 1.4848%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 26, 2015 22:48 ET (03:48 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150127eb1r000ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
